Thus every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized, and no other value is possible.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nRecall that a line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition\n\nIf we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny.  \nHence any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) satisfying (1)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Choosing the sunny lines\n\nSelect \\(k\\) distinct points on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\); write them as  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(a_i,b_i),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(a_i,b_i\\ge 1\\) and \\(a_i+b_i=n+1\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\) choose a real number \\(m_i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nm_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\n\\]\n\nand define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=m_i x + (b_i-m_i a_i).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEquation (2) guarantees that \\(L_i\\) passes through \\(P_i\\).  \nBecause \\(m_i\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe slopes \\(m_i\\) are chosen pairwise distinct, so the lines \\(L_i\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Points already covered\n\nEach sunny line \\(L_i\\) contains its defining point \\(P_i\\).  \nConsequently the set  \n\n\\[\nB:=\\{b_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates that are already covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Completing the covering with non‑sunny lines\n\nFor every integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) that is **not** in \\(B\\) consider the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_b:\\; y=b .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach such line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has its second coordinate \\(b\\notin B\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(H_b\\).  \nIf \\(b\\in B\\) the point is already on the sunny line that passes through \\((a_i,b_i)\\) with \\(b_i=b\\); indeed the line \\(L_i\\) contains **all** points of the form \\((a_i+t,\\,b_i-m_i t)\\) and, because \\(m_i\\neq 0\\), it meets the vertical strip \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) in at least one lattice point for each admissible \\(a\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered either by a sunny line \\(L_i\\) or by a horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\nThe number of horizontal lines introduced is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{b\\mid 1\\le b\\le n,\\;b\\notin B\\}\\,| = n-k .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Counting the lines\n\nAltogether we have  \n\n\\[\nk\\ \\text{sunny lines } L_1,\\dots ,L_k\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nn-k\\ \\text{horizontal lines } H_b,\n\\]\n\nwhich are all distinct.  The total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Verification of the construction\n\n* **Distinctness.**  \n  The sunny lines have distinct slopes, so they are distinct.  \n  The horizontal lines have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, and none of them coincides with a sunny line because a sunny line is never horizontal.\n\n* **Coverage.**  \n  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \n  If \\(b\\in B\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_i\\) that contains the point \\((a_i,b)\\) (the line \\(L_i\\) passes through every point with second coordinate \\(b\\) because its slope is not \\(0\\)).  \n  If \\(b\\notin B\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  \n  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\nThus the construction satisfies both conditions of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* Necessarily \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
