+1-a_i)\\) because substituting \\(x=a_i\\) gives \\(y=n+1-a_i\\).  \nSince \\(m_i\\neq 0,-1\\) and the line is not vertical, its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) nor \\(\\infty\\); therefore \\(S_i\\) is sunny.*\n\nBecause the abscissas \\(a_i\\) are distinct, the points \\((a_i,n+1-a_i)\\) are distinct, and with distinct slopes \\(m_i\\) the lines \\(S_i\\) are pairwise different.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Points already covered by the sunny lines\n\nFix \\(i\\).  For any integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a_i\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb = m_i\\bigl(a_i-a_i\\bigr)+(n+1-a_i) = n+1-a_i,\n\\]\n\nso the point \\((a_i,b)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\) **iff** \\(b=n+1-a_i\\).  \nHowever, because the slope \\(m_i\\neq 0\\), moving along \\(S_i\\) changes the \\(x\\)-coordinate; consequently the vertical line \\(x=a_i\\) intersects \\(S_i\\) only at the single point \\((a_i,n+1-a_i)\\).  \n\nTo guarantee that *all* points with abscissa \\(a_i\\) are covered we use the following observation: the set of points of \\(T_n\\) having abscissa \\(a_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a_i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a_i\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll these points lie on the **vertical** line \\(x=a_i\\).  Since we already have the sunny line \\(S_i\\) passing through the topmost point \\((a_i,n+1-a_i)\\), we will supplement the family with the vertical line \\(x=a_i\\) **only when** \\(a_i\\) is not among the chosen abscissas for sunny lines.  In our construction the abscissas of sunny lines are exactly the chosen \\(a_i\\); therefore for each such \\(a_i\\) we do **not** add a vertical line, and the single point \\((a_i,n+1-a_i)\\) is already covered by \\(S_i\\).  All other points with the same abscissa will be covered by a vertical line introduced in the next step (see below).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Adding non\u2011sunny vertical lines\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nA:=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{a_1,\\dots ,a_