------------------------\n#### 2.5  Counting the lines and sunny lines\n\nWe have constructed  \n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\);\n* \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(V_a\\) for \\(a\\in A\\).\n\nAll these lines are distinct: the sunny lines have distinct slopes and distinct intercepts, while each vertical line has a different \\(x\\)-coordinate, and no vertical line can coincide with a sunny line because a sunny line is never vertical.  \n\nHence the total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* Necessarily \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
