ch is insufficient. Hence a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line cannot exist.\n\nThese two lemmas together show that any admissible configuration must contain **at least two** non‑sunny lines, i.e. the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThe remaining problem – determining which values \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\) actually occur – is left open.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny lines cover at most one point of \\(S_n\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in S_n\\).  \nSince \\(a_i,b_i\\ge 1\\) and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), the differences satisfy  \n\n\\[\na_2-a_1\\in\\{-n,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad b_2-b_1\\in\\{-n,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf the line through the two points were sunny, its slope would be  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBut the only possible slopes obtained from integer differences with the above bounds are exactly \\(0\\) (when \\(b_2=b_1\\)), \\(\\infty\\) (when \\(a_2=a_1\\)), or \\(-1\\) (when \\(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1)\\)). Hence any line joining two points of \\(S_n\\) must be non‑sunny. Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (A single non‑sunny line covers at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\))\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non‑sunny line is of one of the three forms:\n\n1. **Horizontal:** \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 1\\).  \n   Points of \\(S_n\\) on this line satisfy \\(b=c\\) and \\(a\\le n+1-c\\).  