)) can intersect each such row or column in **at most one point**, because two distinct points with the same \\(b\\) (or the same \\(a\\)) would force the line to be horizontal (or vertical).\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (A single non‑sunny line can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\)).**  \n   A non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   * Horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); hence at most \\(n-1\\) points.  \n   * Vertical line \\(x=c\\) is symmetric.  \n   * Line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); it contains the points \\((a,c-a)\\) for \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\), i.e. at most \\(c-1\\le n-1\\) points.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Suppose all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line can meet each horizontal row \\(R_c\\) in at most one point, and similarly each vertical column \\(C_c\\) in at most one point.  \n   There are \\(n\\) distinct rows (for \\(c=1,\\dots ,n\\)) and \\(n\\) distinct columns (for \\(c=1,\\dots ,n\\)).  \n   Since a point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to exactly one row and one column, the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most the total number of rows plus columns, i.e. \\(2n\\).  \n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n,\n   \\]  \n   so the covering requirement cannot be satisfied. Hence \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Assume exactly one line, say \\(\\ell\\), is non‑sunny and the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.  \n   By Lemma 2, \\(\\ell\\) covers at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Each sunny line can meet each row (or column) in at most one new point, so the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together can contribute at most \\(n-1\\) additional points.  \n   Consequently the total number of distinct points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n   \\]  \n   Since \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), the required covering is impossible. Therefore a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line (i.e. \\(k=n-1\\)) does not exist.\n\nFrom the two lemmas we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe construction of explicit families of lines that realize each integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\) has not been achieved; this constitutes the remaining part of the problem. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
