f the first column except possibly \\((1,1)\\).  \n   *A diagonal line* (parallel to \\(\\ell_{d}\\)) can cover at most one point of the first row and at most one point of the first column (the two points are symmetric with respect to the line \\(x=y\\)).  \n\n   Hence each non\u2011sunny line can cover **at most \\(n-1\\)** points of the set \u201cfirst row \u222a first column\u201d.  \n   To cover the required \\((2n-3)-k\\) points we need  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{(2n-3)-k}{\\,n-1\\,}.\n   \\]\n\n   Solving this inequality gives \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Therefore any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   *Step\u202f3.1 \u2013 Choose the sunny lines.*  \n   Pick distinct integers  \n\n   \\[\n   a_{1},a_{2},\\dots ,a_{k}\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   s_{i}:\\; y = -\\frac{a_{i}}{a_{i}+2}\\,x + \\frac{a_{i}+2}{a_{i}+2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\dfrac{a_{i}}{a_{i}+2}\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(s_{i}\\) is sunny.  \n   Substituting \\(x=a_{i}\\) in (2) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=a_{i}+2\\) gives \\(y=0\\), which lies outside \\(T_{n}\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   s_{i}\\cap T_{n}= \\{(a_{i},1)\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus each sunny line contributes **exactly one** point of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f3.2 \u2013 Remove the covered points.*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{k}= \\{(a_{i},1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\},\n   \\qquad\n   T_{n}' = T_{n}\\setminus P_{k}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f3.3 \u2013 Cover the remainder with non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   For every integer \\(c\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   c\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{a_{1},\\dots ,a_{k}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   introduce the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   v_{c}:\\; x=c .\n   \\]\n\n   There are exactly \\(n-k\\) such vertical lines;