            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.  \nA line is called **sunny** if it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_3:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_c\\) are parallel to \\(\\ell_3\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in the range \\([2,n+1]\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(L_c\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_0=\\{L_2,L_3,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nConsequently the integer  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Sunny lines intersecting a given diagonal  \n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n\\).  \nConsider the two points of the diagonal \\(D_t:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b=t\\}\\)  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,t-1),\\qquad P_2=(t,1).\n\\]\n\nThe line through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y- (t-1)=\\frac{1-(t-1)}{t-1}(x-1)\n      \\;=\\;-\\frac{t-2}{t-1}(x-1)+(t-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Verification that \\(S_t\\) is sunny.*  \nThe slope of \\(S_t\\) is \\(-\\dfrac{t-2}{t-1}\\).  \nSince \\(t\\ge 2\\),\n\n* the slope is not \\(0\\) (otherwise the line would be horizontal),  \n* the line is not vertical because the slope is finite,  \n* the slope is not \\(-1\\) because \\(-\\dfrac{t-2}{t-1}=-1\\) would imply \\(t-2=t-1\\), impossible.  \n\nHence \\(S_t\\) is not parallel to any of \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\); therefore \\(S_t\\) is sunny.  \n\nMoreover, by construction \\(S_t\\) passes through the two points \\(P_1,P_2\\in D_t\\).  \n\nThus for every diagonal \\(D_t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n\\)) we have exhibited a sunny line that contains at least two of its points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Remarks on extending the construction  \n\nThe lines \\(S_t\\) provide a mechanism for introducing sunny lines into a family that originally consisted solely of the non‑sunny diagonals \\(L_c\\).  \nIf one were to replace a diagonal \\(L_t\\) by the sunny line \\(S_t\\), the two points \\((1,t-1)\\) and \\((t,1)\\) would remain covered, but the other points of the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=t\\}\n\\]\n\nwould no longer lie on any line, because no other diagonal has the same sum \\(t\\).  \nConsequently a naïve replacement of a diagonal by \\(S_t\\) does **not** preserve the covering property.  \n\nTo obtain a configuration with a prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines one would need to devise a more elaborate scheme, for example by allowing several sunny lines to cooperate so that every point of each diagonal is covered by at least one of them, or by introducing additional sunny lines that intersect the diagonals in a way that restores coverage.  \n\nAt present such a scheme has not been constructed, and therefore the existence of configurations with an arbitrary number \\(k\\) of sunny lines (beyond the already proved case \\(k=0\\)) remains an open question.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\n* We have proved rigorously that \\(k=0\\) is always attainable by the family of diagonal lines \\(\\{x+y=c\\}\\).  \n* For each diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n\\)) we have exhibited a sunny line \\(S_t\\) that passes through two points of that diagonal.  \n\nThe remaining challenge—showing that by suitably combining such sunny lines one can obtain configurations with **any** prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines while still covering all points of \\(T_n\\)—has not been resolved.  \n\nHence the problem is only partially solved: the admissible values of \\(k\\) include at least \\(k=0\\); the full set of admissible \\(k\\) is still unknown.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
