nd at least one of them must be sunny in the mixed case we shall treat. ∎\n\n4. **Lemma 2.** *If a line contains a point of \\(D\\) and also another required point \\((x,y)\\) with \\(x+y\\le n\\), then the line is non‑sunny.*  \n   *Proof.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in D\\) so \\(a+b=n+1\\). Suppose the line also passes through \\((x,y)\\) with \\(x+y\\le n\\). Its slope is\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{y-b}{\\,x-a\\,}.\n   \\]\n   Because \\(y\\le n-x\\) and \\(b=n+1-a\\),\n   \\[\n   y-b\\le (n-x)-(n+1-a)=a-x-1.\n   \\]\n   Hence\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{y-b}{x-a}\\le\\frac{a-x-1}{x-a}= -1-\\frac{1}{x-a}.\n   \\]\n   Similarly, from \\(y\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\) we obtain\n   \\[\n   y-b\\ge 1-(n+1-a)=a-n\\ge -(n-1),\n   \\]\n   which together with \\(x-a\\ge -(n-1)\\) yields \\(m\\ge -1\\).  \n   The only rational numbers satisfying \\(-1\\le m\\le -1\\) are \\(m=-1\\). Thus the line has slope \\(-1\\) and is non‑sunny. ∎\n\n5. **Impossibility of a mixed family.**  \n   Assume that among the \\(n\\) required lines there are both sunny and non‑sunny ones; let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines with \\(0<k<n\\).  \n   By Lemma 1 each line can cover at most one point of \\(D\\); therefore the \\(n\\) lines together can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(D\\). Since \\(|D|=n\\), every line must contain **exactly one** point of \\(D\\).  \n\n   Take any sunny line \\(L\\). It contains a unique point \\((a,b)\\in D\\). Because \\(L\\) is sunny, by Lemma 2 it cannot contain any other required point \\((x,y)\\) with \\(x+y\\le n\\). Consequently the only lattice point of the whole set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) that lies on \\(L\\) is \\((a,b)\\) itself.  \n\n   Hence each sunny line accounts for **only one** of the \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) required points, while each non‑sunny line can contain at most two of them (a vertical or horizontal line can meet the diagonal in one point and can also meet the region \\(x+y\\le n\\) in at most one additional lattice point; a line of slope \\(-1\\) would coincide with the diagonal and would then contain all points of \\(D\\), contradicting the fact that we already have a sunny line).  \n\n   Counting the maximal number of covered points we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny lines}}\\cdot 1\n   \\;+\\;\n   \\underbrace{(n-k)}_{\\text{non‑sunny lines}}\\cdot 2\n   \\;<\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad\\text{for }0<k<n,\n   \\]\n   because the right‑hand side equals \\(n+ \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) and the left‑hand side equals \\(2n-k\\), which is strictly smaller for any \\(0<k<n\\).  \n   Thus the required set of lattice points cannot be covered when both types of lines are present.  \n\n   Therefore a feasible configuration must consist **either** entirely of non‑sunny lines (\\(k=0\\)) **or** entirely of sunny lines (\\(k=n\\)).\n\n6. **Realising the two possibilities.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(x=a\\). All these lines have slope \\(\\infty\\) and are non‑sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) lines of slope \\(1\\)  \n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = x + (i-1)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n   For a lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) set \\(i:=b-a+1\\).  \n   Because \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le n\\); then \\(b=a+(i-1)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).  \n   All \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The impossibility argument shows that a mixture of sunny and non‑sunny lines cannot satisfy the covering condition, and the explicit constructions above demonstrate that the two extreme cases are attainable. Consequently the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\text{ and }n}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate th