 in exactly one point, so the \\(n\\) lines together cover all \\(n\\) points of \\(D\\) and each line contains a *different* point of \\(D\\).\n\n   For a sunny line \\(L_{i}\\) Lemma 2 shows that the only point of \\(S\\) lying on \\(L_{i}\\) is its diagonal point; therefore each sunny line contributes **exactly one** point of \\(S\\).\n\n   Consider a non‑sunny line.  If it is vertical, say \\(x=a\\), then besides its diagonal point \\((a,n+1-a)\\) it can contain only points with \\(y\\le n-a\\); there are at most \\(n-a\\) such points.  If it is horizontal, say \\(y=b\\), then besides \\((n+1-b,b)\\) it can contain at most \\(n-b\\) points with \\(x\\le n-b\\).  In either case the number of *additional* points (those with \\(x+y\\le n\\)) that a non‑sunny line can cover is at most \\(n-a\\) (or \\(n-b\\)), i.e. at most the number of integers strictly smaller than the column (or row) index of its diagonal point.\n\n   Let the set of column indices of the non‑sunny lines be\n   \\[\n   A:=\\{a_{1},a_{2},\\dots ,a_{n-k}\\}\\subset\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\n   \\]\n   where each \\(a_{j}\\) is the \\(x\\)-coordinate of the diagonal point belonging to that line (for a horizontal line we use the corresponding column index \\(n+1-b\\)).  \n   The total number of points of \\(S\\) covered by the non‑sunny lines is at most\n   \\[\n   (n-k) \\;+\\; \\sum_{a\\in A}(n-a)\n   = (n-k) \\;+\\; (n-k)n-\\sum_{a\\in A}a .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   To maximise the right–hand side we choose the smallest possible elements for \\(A\\), namely \\(A=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\).  Substituting this choice into (3) gives the maximal possible coverage by the non‑sunny lines:\n   \\[\n   (n-k)+\\sum_{a=1}^{\\,n-k}(n-a)\n   = (n-k)+\\frac{(n-1)+(n-(n-k))}{2}\\,(n-k)\n   = (n-k)\\,\\frac{n+1+k}{2}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the contributions of the \\(k\\) sunny lines (each contributes exactly one point) we obtain an upper bound for the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered:\n   \\[\n   k \\;+\\; (n-k)\\,\\frac{n+1+k}{2}\n   = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\;-\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\ge1\\), the term \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\) is positive, and (5) is **strictly smaller** than \\(|S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  Consequently the required set \\(S\\) cannot be covered when both sunny and non‑sunny lines are present.\n\n   Therefore a feasible configuration must consist **either** entirely of non‑sunny lines (\\(k=0\\)) **or** entirely of sunny lines (\\(k=n\\)).\n\n7. **Realising the two extreme cases.**\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  All these lines have slope \\(\\infty\\) and are non‑sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) lines of slope \\(1\\)  \n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\; y = x + (i-1)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n   For a lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) set \\(i:=b-a+1\\).  \n   Because \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le n\\); then \\(b=a+(i-1)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_{i}\\).  \n   All \\(L_{i}\\) have slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The impossibility argument shows that a mixture of sunny and non‑sunny lines cannot satisfy the covering condition, while the explicit constructions above demonstrate that the two extreme possibilities are attainable.  Hence the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\text{ and }n}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
