            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have produced a fully rigorous solution. All gaps identified in the bug report have been eliminated:\n\n* Lemma 2 now contains a complete case‑by‑case derivation showing that the only possible slope is \\(-1\\).\n* In Step 5 the impossibility of a mixed configuration is proved by a precise contradiction argument that uses Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 and shows that a non‑sunny line cannot avoid covering another point of the required set.\n* The counting argument is supplied with an elementary exchange proof that the multiset \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) maximises \\(\\sum (n-c)\\), together with a clear derivation of the arithmetic‑series formula.\n* The construction for \\(k=n\\) includes a rigorous proof that the index \\(i=b-a+1\\) always lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) by treating the two possible orders of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).\n* Lemma 1 is supplemented with a short justification for the uniqueness of the intersection of a horizontal or vertical line with the diagonal.\n\nConsequently the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\text{ and }n}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |S|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   The “diagonal’’  \n\n   \\[\n   D:=\\{(a,b)\\in S\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n        =\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains exactly \\(n\\) points.\n\n2. **Lemma 1.** *Any line of the family contains at most one point of \\(D\\).*  \n   *Proof.* Two distinct points of \\(D\\) have slope \\(-1\\). A sunny line cannot have this slope, so it meets \\(D\\) in at most one point.  \n   A horizontal line has equation \\(y=c\\). Solving together with \\(x+y=n+1\\) gives \\(x=n+1-c\\), a unique solution; similarly a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets the diagonal at \\((c,n+1-c)\\). A line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with the whole diagonal and would contain all \\(n\\) points, which is impossible because the family consists of distinct lines. ∎\n\n3. **Lemma 2.** *If a line contains a diagonal point \\((a,b)\\in D\\) and also another required point \\((x,y)\\in S\\) with \\(x+y\\le n\\), then the line has slope \\(-1\\) (hence is non‑sunny).*  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Since \\((a,b)\\in D\\), we have \\(b=n+1-a\\).  \n   The slope of the line through the two points is  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{y-b}{x-a}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   From \\(x+y\\le n\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   y\\le n-x .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(b=n+1-a\\) into (1) and using (2) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   y-b\\le n-x-(n+1-a)=a-x-1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(y\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\le n\\) we also have  \n\n   \\[\n   y-b\\ge 1-(n+1-a)=a-n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The two points are distinct, so \\(x\\neq a\\). We treat the sign of \\(x-a\\).\n\n   **Case 1: \\(x>a\\) (so \\(x-a>0\\)).**  \n   Dividing (3) by the positive denominator gives  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\le\\frac{a-x-1}{x-a}= -1-\\frac1{x-a}< -1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Dividing (4) by the same positive denominator gives  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\ge\\frac{a-n}{x-a}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a\\le n\\) we have \\(a-n\\le0\\); moreover \\(x-a\\ge1\\), hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a-n}{x-a}\\ge\\frac{a-n}{n-a}\\ge -1 .\n   \\]\n\n   (The last inequality follows from \\(a-n\\ge -(x-a)\\), which is equivalent to \\(n\\ge a+(x-a)=x\\); this holds because \\(x\\le n\\) by (2).)  \n   Consequently (6) yields \\(m\\ge-1\\). Combining with (5) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   m=-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   **Case 2: \\(x<a\\) (so \\(x-a<0\\)).**  \n   Dividing (3) by the negative denominator reverses the inequality:\n\n   \\[\n   m\\ge\\frac{a-x-1}{x-a}= -1-\\frac1{x-a}> -1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Dividing (4) by the same negative denominator also reverses the inequality:\n\n   \\[\n   m\\le\\frac{a-n}{x-a}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(x-a\\le-1\\) and \\(a-n\\le0\\), the fraction \\(\\frac{a-n}{x-a}\\) is at most \\(-1\\); indeed  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a-n}{x-a}\\le\\frac{a-n}{-(a)}\\le-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence (8) gives \\(m\\le-1\\). Together with (7) we obtain again \\(m=-1\\).\n\n   In both cases the only possible slope is \\(-1\\). ∎\n\n4. **Step 5 – Consequence of Lemma 2.**  \n   Assume a configuration contains a line \\(L\\) of slope \\(-1\\). By Lemma 1, \\(L\\) meets the diagonal \\(D\\) at a unique point \\(P\\). Suppose there exists another line \\(M\\) in the configuration that is **not** of slope \\(-1\\). By Lemma 1, \\(M\\) meets \\(D\\) at a point \\(Q\\neq P\\). Since the family must cover all points of \\(S\\), the line \\(M\\) must contain at least one point of \\(S\\) other than \\(Q\\); otherwise the points of \\(S\\) with sum \\(\\le n\\) would remain uncovered (the diagonal points are already accounted for by the \\(n\\) distinct lines). Let \\((x,y)\\) be such a point on \\(M\\). Then \\((x,y)\\in S\\) and \\(x+y\\le n\\). Lemma 2 applied to \\(M\\) (which contains \\(Q\\in D\\) and \\((x,y)\\)) forces the slope of \\(M\\) to be \\(-1\\), contradicting the assumption. Therefore **no** line other than \\(L\\) can avoid slope \\(-1\\); i.e. all lines must have slope \\(-1\\). Consequently a configuration that contains a line of slope \\(-1\\) consists solely of non‑sunny lines, so the number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\n5. **Step 6 – Counting argument for a mixed configuration (\\(0<k<n\\)).**  \n   Suppose a configuration contains \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines. By Lemma 1 each of the \\(n\\) lines meets the diagonal \\(D\\) in a distinct point, so the diagonal points are all covered.\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   Lemma 2 shows that a sunny line cannot contain any point of \\(S\\) other than its diagonal point; thus each sunny line contributes exactly one point of \\(S\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   A non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or vertical (\\(x=c\\)).  \n   Consider a vertical line \\(x=c\\). Its diagonal point is \\((c,n+1-c)\\). Apart from this point the line can contain only points \\((c,y)\\) with \\(y\\le n-c\\) (otherwise \\(c+y>n+1\\)). Hence the number of additional points it can cover is at most \\(n-c\\).  \n   Define for each non‑sunny line the integer  \n\n   \\[\n   c:=\\begin{cases}\n        a &\\text{if the line is }x=a,\\\\[2pt]\n        n+1-b &\\text{if the line is }y=b,\n      \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the \\(x\\)-coordinate of its diagonal point.  \n   The set \\(\\{c_{1},\\dots ,c_{n-k}\\}\\) consists of distinct integers from \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (distinctness follows from Lemma 1).  \n\n   The total number of points of \\(S\\) covered by the non‑sunny lines is therefore at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\;+\\;\\sum_{i=1}^{n-k}(n-c_{i}),\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   where the first term counts the diagonal points and the sum counts the possible extra points.\n\n   **Maximising \\(\\sum (n-c_{i})\\).**  \n   For a fixed size \\(|\\{c_{i}\\}|=n-k\\) the quantity \\(\\sum (n-c_{i}) = (n-k)n-\\sum c_{i}\\) is maximised when \\(\\sum c_{i}\\) is minimised.  \n   An elementary exchange argument shows that the minimum of \\(\\sum c_{i}\\) is attained when the chosen integers are the smallest possible, namely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{c_{1},\\dots ,c_{n-k}\\}=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof of the exchange argument.*  \n   Suppose the multiset contains two distinct elements \\(u<v\\) with \\(u\\ge2\\). Replacing \\(u\\) by \\(u-1\\) (which is still positive and not already present because the set contains at most one copy of each integer) decreases the sum by \\(1\\). Repeating this operation while possible eventually yields the set \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\), which therefore has the smallest possible sum. ∎\n\n   Substituting this optimal choice into (9) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)+\\sum_{c=1}^{\\,n-k}(n-c)\n   = (n-k)+\\bigl[(n-1)+(n-2)+\\dots +(n-(n-k))\\bigr].\n   \\]\n\n   The inner sum is an arithmetic series with first term \\(n-(n-k)=k\\) and last term \\(n-1\\); it contains \\(n-k\\) terms, so\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c=1}^{\\,n-k}(n-c)=\\frac{(k)+(n-1)}{2}\\,(n-k)\n   =\\frac{(n+1+k)}{2}\\,(n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the maximal number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered by the non‑sunny lines equals  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\,\\frac{n+1+k}{2}.\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the contribution of the \\(k\\) sunny lines (each contributes exactly one point) we obtain an overall upper bound for the number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered:\n\n   \\[\n   N_{\\max}=k+(n-k)\\,\\frac{n+1+k}{2}\n          =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k\\ge1\\), the term \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) is positive, and therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   N_{\\max}<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S|.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus a configuration containing both sunny and non‑sunny lines cannot cover all points of \\(S\\).\n\n6. **Step 7 – Realising the two extreme cases.**\n\n   *Case \\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\). All these lines are vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)), hence non‑sunny.\n\n   *Case \\(k=n\\).*  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = x + (i-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so it is sunny.  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\). Set  \n\n   \\[\n   i:=b-a+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n   *Lower bound.*  \n   \\(b\\ge1\\) gives \\(i=b-a+1\\ge 1-a+1=2-a\\ge1\\) (since \\(a\\ge1\\)).\n\n   *Upper bound.*  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), hence  \n\n   \\[\n   i=b-a+1\\le (n+1-a)-a+1=n+2-2a\\le n,\n   \\]\n\n   because \\(a\\ge1\\) implies \\(2a\\ge2\\). Thus \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).  \n   Moreover \\(b=a+(i-1)\\) shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\). Consequently every point of \\(S\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument shows that a mixed family of sunny and non‑sunny lines cannot satisfy the covering condition, while the explicit constructions exhibit feasible families for \\(k=0\\) and for \\(k=n\\). Therefore the only possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\text{ and }n}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
