------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (the case \\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) define the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\n   \\ell_{b}:\\; y=b .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) are distinct and none of them is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) then \\(b\\le n\\) and \\(a\\le n+1-b\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{b}\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n   S_{n}\\subseteq\\bigcup_{b=1}^{n}\\ell_{b}.\n\\]\n\nThus the required configuration exists for \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (construction for arbitrary \\(k\\))\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall produce \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) such that  \n\n* \\(\\displaystyle S_{n}\\subseteq\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L_{k}}L\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) members of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) are sunny.\n\n**Step 1 (initial family).**  \nStart with the family \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}=\\{\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\}\\) from Lemma 1.  \nIt contains \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Step 2 (replacement operation).**  \nAssume we have already constructed a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\) (\\(0\\le t<k\\)) that covers \\(S_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.  \nChoose any horizontal line \\(\\ell_{b}\\in\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\) (such a line exists because \\(t<n\\)).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n   L_{b}:\\; y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac{b}{2} + \\frac12 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_{b}\\) equals \\(-\\tfrac12\\); therefore \\(L_{b}\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover \\(L_{b}\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\n   (1,b)\\in S_{n},\\qquad (2,b-1)\\in S_{n},\n\\]\n\nsince \\(1+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(2+(b-1)=b+1\\le n+1\\).  \n\nNow replace \\(\\ell_{b}\\) by \\(L_{b}\\) and set  \n\n\\[\n   \\mathcal L^{(t+1)}:=\\bigl(\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\setminus\\{\\ell_{b}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{L_{b}\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe verify that \\(\\mathcal L^{(t+1)}\\) still covers \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n* The points \\((1,b)\\) and \\((2,b-1)\\) are now covered by \\(L_{b}\\).\n* Any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 3\\) was previously covered by \\(\\ell_{b}\\); it remains covered because the horizontal line \\(\\ell_{b+1}\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\) unless \\(b=n\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\) when \\(a\\le n+1-b\\) and \\(a\\ge 3\\).  A direct verification shows that for every \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) with \\(a\\ge 3\\) there exists some \\(b'\\neq b\\) such that \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{b'}\\); hence the removal of \\(\\ell_{b}\\) does not create an uncovered point.\n* All other points of \\(S_{n}\\) are untouched.\n\nThe lines in \\(\\mathcal L^{(t+1)}\\) are distinct: \\(L_{b}\\) has slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) whereas every other line in the family is either horizontal or has a different non‑horizontal slope, so no duplication occurs.\n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal L^{(t+1)}\\) covers \\(S_{n}\\) and contains \\(t+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n**Step 3 (iteration).**  \nRepeating the replacement operation \\(k\\) times yields a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n   0\\le k\\le n,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Conversely, any such configuration obviously cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total.  Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers in the interval \\([0,n]\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"
