e is not \\(0\\), not \\(\\infty\\) (i.e. not vertical), and not \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with no sunny line\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i}:\\; y=i .\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) are distinct, none of them is sunny, and for any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and therefore \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{b}\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\ell_{i}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A sunny line that can replace a horizontal line\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{t}:\\; y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac{t}{2}+\\frac12 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny.  \nSubstituting \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\) in (4) gives  \n\n\\[\n(1,t)\\in L_{t},\\qquad (2,t-1)\\in L_{t}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1+t\\le n+1\\) and \\(2+(t-1)=t+1\\le n+1\\), both points belong to \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacement Lemma\n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(r\\) sunny lines.  Suppose \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains a horizontal line \\(y=t\\) for some \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F' := \\bigl(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{y=t\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{L_{t}\\}\n\\]\n\nalso consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, still covers \\(S_{n}\\), and contains exactly \\(r+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nDistinctness: every line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) different from \\(y=t\\) is either horizontal with a different \\(y\\)-value or has a slope different from \\(-\\tfrac12\\); therefore none coincides with \\(L_{t}\\).\n\nCoverage of the two points in (5): t