n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A first lower bound on the number of non‑sunny lines\n\nSuppose among the required \\(n\\) distinct lines exactly \\(t\\) are non‑sunny.  \nBy Lemma 1 the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by those \\(t\\) lines is at most \\(tn\\).  \nSince every point of \\(S_n\\) must lie on *at least* one of the \\(n\\) lines, we need  \n\n\\[\ntn\\ge |S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Intersections force all non‑sunny lines to be parallel\n\nAssume there are two non‑sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2\\) whose directions are different.  \nThere are three possibilities for the pair of directions; in each case the two lines intersect at a unique point \\(P\\).\n\nBecause the directions are among the three forbidden ones, the coordinates of \\(P\\) are integers:  \n\n* horizontal \\(\\cap\\) vertical gives a point with integer \\(x\\) and \\(y\\);\n* horizontal \\(\\cap\\) slope \\(-1\\) gives a point with integer \\(x\\) (from the horizontal line) and then integer \\(y\\) from \\(x+y=c\\);\n* vertical \\(\\cap\\) slope \\(-1\\) is analogous.\n\nMoreover the intersecting point satisfies \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); indeed each of the two lines already contains a point of \\(S_n\\), and the intersection lies on the segment joining those two points, which stays inside the triangle \\(\\{a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\). Hence \\(P\\in S_n\\).\n\nThus \\(P\\) belongs to **both** non‑sunny lines.  \nIf a third line (sunny or non‑sunny) also passed through \\(P\\) we would have a point of \\(S_n\\) covered more than once, which is allowed (the condition is “at least one”), but the crucial observation is that the two non‑sunny lines already *share* a point of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nNow count points contributed by the two non‑sunny lines