two of which are non\u2011sunny.\n\n   The three constructions work for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (3) together with the explicit constructions shows that the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Size of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has exactly \\(s-1\\) positive integer solutions. Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{(n+1)n}{2}=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  How many points can a non\u2011sunny line contain?\n\nA line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* vertical:   \\(x=c\\);\n* slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n*Horizontal.*  \nIf a line is \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge1\\), then a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on it iff \\(b=c\\).  \nSince \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); thus at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) lie on the line.\n\n*Vertical.*  \nAnalogous: a line \\(x=c\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\).*  \nA line \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge2\\) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\).  \nThe condition \\(a,b\\ge1\\) gives exactly \\(c-1\\) such points, and the extra condition \\(c\\le n+1\\) yields \\(c-1\\le n\\). Hence at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nThus **any non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).**  \nWe record this as\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{(Lemma 1) }|L\\cap S_n|\\le n\\text{