(P\\) we would have a point of \\(S_n\\) covered more than once, which is allowed (the condition is “at least one”), but the crucial observation is that the two non‑sunny lines already *share* a point of \\(S_n\\).  \n\nNow count points contributed by the two non‑sunny lines.  \nEach of them can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (Lemma 1), but because they share at least one point, together they cover **at most** \\(2n-1\\) distinct points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nIf we had three or more non‑sunny lines, at least two of them would have distinct directions (pigeonhole principle, there are only three directions). Those two would already waste at least one point, and the third line would add at most \\(n\\) new points, so the total number of distinct points covered by the non‑sunny lines would be at most \\(3n-2\\). Repeating this argument shows that with \\(t\\ge3\\) non‑sunny lines we can cover at most \\(tn-(t-2)\\) distinct points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nSince \\(|S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) and for \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n\\[\ntn-(t-2) < tn \\le \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\quad\\text{whenever }t\\ge3,\n\\]\n\nthe inequality \\(tn-(t-2)\\ge |S_n|\\) fails. Hence **\\(t\\) cannot be \\(3\\) or larger**.  \n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{t\\le 2.}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Determination of the possible values of \\(k\\)\n\nRecall \\(k=n-t\\) is the number of sunny lines.  \nFrom (6) we have \\(t\\ge\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\); from (7) we have \\(t\\le2\\).  \nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil\\le2\\) only when \\(n\\le3\\); however (7) already forces \\(t\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nConsequently the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\nk=n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Constructions showing attainability\n\nWe now exhibit, for each \\(n\\ge3\\), explicit families of 