tisfy \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); their number is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-(2n-1)=\\binom{n+1}{2}-(2n-1)=\\binom{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nAgain fix a sunny slope \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nFor each interior point \\((a,b)\\) define a line  \n\n\\[\nL_{(a,b)}:\\; y = m x + (b-m a).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are distinct, sunny, and each passes through exactly one of the interior points.  \nThus we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2+\\binom{n-1}{2}=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, of which precisely two (\\(L_1,L_2\\)) are non‑sunny. Hence \\(k=n-2\\).\n\nAll three constructions work for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Final conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition derived from counting and intersection arguments forces the number of sunny lines to be one of \\(n,\\,n-1,\\,n-2\\). The explicit constructions above show that each of these three values is indeed attainable. Therefore the complete answer to the problem is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
