\\) and \\(a+b<n+1\\).*  \n   Such a point is covered by the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), which is present in \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\).\n\n   *Points with \\(a+b=n+1\\).*  \n   All these points lie on the diagonal \\(\\ell\\), which is part of \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered, and \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) (\\(a=1,\\dots ,n\\)) all lie on the line \\(\\ell\\) of slope \\(-1\\), which is not sunny.  A sunny line can contain at most one of these points, otherwise its slope would be \\(-1\\).  Therefore covering all \\(n\\) of these points requires at least \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines, leaving no line to cover the remaining points of \\(S_{n}\\).  Consequently a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A covering with exactly one non‑sunny diagonal\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&h_{i}: y=i \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\\\\\n&\\ell : x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny (the \\(h_i\\) are horizontal, \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(-1\\)).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have two possibilities.\n\n*If \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\).  \n*If \\(a+b<n+1\\)*, then \\(b\\le n-1\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(h_{b}\\).\n\nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{0}= \\{h_{1},h_{2},\\dots ,h_{n-1},\\ell\\}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_{n}\\) and contains \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines that can replace horizontals\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{t}:\\; y=-\\frac12 x+\\frac{t+2}{2}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) is sunny.  Substituting \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(1,t+2)\\in L_{t},\\qquad (2,t+1)\\in L_{t}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1+(t+2)=t+3\\le n+1\\) and \\(2+(t+1)=t+3\\le n+1\\), both points belong to \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacement Lemma\n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n}\\) and containing the horizontal line \\(h_{t+2}\\) for some \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F'=\\bigl(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{h_{t+2}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{L_{t}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\mathcal F'\\) also consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, still covers \\(S_{n}\\), and has one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(L_{t}\\) is not horizontal, so it does not coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{h_{t+2}\\}\\); thus the lines remain distinct.  \n\nThe two points in (8) were on the removed horizontal line and are now on \\(L_{t}\\).  \n\nConsider any other point \\((a,t+2)\\in S_{n}\\) with \\(a\\ge3\\).  Since \\(a+(t+2)\\le n+1\\), we have \\((a-1,t+3)\\in S_{n}\\).  The point \\((a-1,t+3)\\) is covered by the horizontal line \\(h_{t+3}\\), which is still present because \\(t+3\\le n\\).  Hence \\((a,t+2)\\) remains covered.  \n\nAll points of \\(S_{n}\\) whose second coordinate is different from \\(t+2\\) are unaffected.  Therefore \\(\\mathcal F'\\) still covers \\(S_{n}\\).  \n\nFinally, \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny while the removed line \\(h_{t+2}\\) is not, so the number of sunny lines increases by one. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nStart from the base family \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) in (6).  \nFor \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) apply the Replacement Lemma to the horizontal line \\(h_{i+2}\\).  \nAfter performing the \\(k\\) replacements we obtain the family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{k}= \\bigl\\{L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{h_{1},h_{k+2},h_{k+3},\\dots ,h_{n-1}\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\ell\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nCounting the lines:\n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_{0},\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\);\n* \\((n-1)-k\\) remaining horizontals;\n* the diagonal \\(\\ell\\).\n\nHence \\(|\\mathcal F_{k}| = k+(n-1-k)+1 = n\\).\n\n**Coverage.**  \n\n*Points with second coordinate \\(b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\).*  \nWrite \\(b=i+2\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le k-1\\).  The points \\((1,b)\\) and \\((2,b-1)\\) lie on \\(L_{i}\\) by (8).  Any other point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge3\\) is covered by the horizontal line \\(h_{b+1}\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) because \\(b+1\\le k+2\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Points with second coordinate \\(b\\notin\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) and \\(a+b<n+1\\).*  \nSuch a point is covered by the horizontal line \\(h_{b}\\), which is present in \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\).\n\n*Points with \\(a+b=n+1\\).*  \nAll these points lie on \\(\\ell\\), which is part of \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered, and \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_{a}=(a,n+1-a),\\qquad a=1,2,\\dots ,n,\n\\]\n\nall satisfy \\(a+(n+1-a)=n+1\\); hence they lie on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;x+y=n+1,\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nwhose slope is \\(-1\\) and therefore is not sunny.  Any sunny line has slope different from \\(-1\\); consequently it can contain at most one of the points \\(P_{a}\\) (two distinct points would force the slope to be \\(-1\\)).  To cover all \\(n\\) points of (10) we need at least \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines, leaving no