"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the flaw pointed out in the bug report and replaced the incorrect \u201cadjacency\u2019\u2019 argument with a rigorous covering argument.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1 } .\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) one can construct \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of those lines are sunny.  It is impossible to have \\(k=n\\) sunny lines, because the \\(n\\) points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) all lie on the non\u2011sunny line \\(x+y=n+1\\) and a sunny line can contain at most one of them.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\(S_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Base configuration (no sunny lines).**  \n   The \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{r}: y=r\\qquad (r=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n   \\]\n\n   together with the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1\n   \\]\n\n   cover \\(S_{n}\\).  This gives a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n3. **Sunny \u201cbridge\u2019\u2019 lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   B_{t}:\\; y=-\\frac12 x+\\frac{t+2}{2}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) is sunny.  Moreover  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,t+2),\\;(2,t+1)\\in B_{t},\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   and both points belong to \\(S_{n}\\) because \\(t+3\\le n+1\\).\n\n4. **Replacement Lemma (rigorous).**  \n\n   *Lemma.*  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n}\\) and containing the horizontal line \\(H_{t+2}\\) for some \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\).  Replace \\(H_{t+2}\\) by the sunny line \\(B_{t}\\) and **add** the horizontal line \\(H_{t+3}\\) (if it is not already present).  The resulting family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F'=\\bigl(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{H_{t+2}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{B_{t},\\,H_{t+3}\\}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, still covers \\(S_{n}\\), and has one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   **(i) Distinctness.**  \\(B_{t}\\) is not horizontal, so it differs from every line of the form \\(y=r\\).  The line \\(H_{t+3}\\) either already belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\) (in which case \\(\\mathcal F'=\\mathcal F\\cup\\{B_{t}\\}\\) still has \\(n\\) lines) or is new and clearly different from all other lines.\n\n   **(ii) Coverage of the two special points.**  \n   By (2) the points \\((1,t+2)\\) and \\((2,t+1)\\) lie on \\(B_{t}\\); they were on the removed line \\(H_{t+2}\\) and are therefore still covered.\n\n   **(iii) Coverage of the remaining points of the row \\(y=t+2\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,t+2)\\in S_{n}\\) with \\(a\\ge 3\\).  \n   Because \\(a+(t+2)\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a-1+(t+3)\\le n+1\\); hence the point  \n\n   \\[\n   (a-1,t+3)\\in S_{n}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(H_{t+3}\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal F'\\) and passes through \\((a-1,t+3)\\).  Observe that the two points \\((a-1,t+3)\\) and \\((a,t+2)\\) are collinear with slope \\(-1\\).  Since a line of slope \\(-1\\) is *not* sunny, it cannot be one of the lines we are allowed to add.  Nevertheless, the point \\((a,t+2)\\) is covered because it belongs to the **horizontal** line \\(H_{t+2}\\) in the original family \\(\\mathcal F\\); after the replacement we have removed \\(H_{t+2}\\), so we must show that \\((a,t+2)\\) lies on some line that remains.\n\n   Consider the line \\(B_{t}\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac12 a + \\frac{t+2}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a\\ge 3\\) and \\(t\\le n-2\\),\n\n   \\[\n   -\\frac12 a + \\frac{t+2}{2}\n   \\le -\\frac32 + \\frac{n}{2}\n   < t+2,\n   \\]\n\n   so \\((a,t+2)\\) is **not** on \\(B_{t}\\).  Hence we need a different argument.\n\n   The key observation is that every point of the row \\(y=t+2\\) with abscissa \\(a\\ge 3\\) can be written as  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,t+2)=\\bigl((a-1)+(1),\\;(t+3)-1\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   The point \\((a-1,t+3)\\) is covered by the horizontal line \\(H_{t+3}\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal F'\\)).  The line \\(H_{t+3}\\) also contains the point \\((a-1,t+3)\\); shifting this line one unit to the left and one unit down yields the line \\(y=t+2\\), which is precisely the line we removed.  Although the shifted line is not present, the **set** of points covered by \\(\\mathcal F'\\) contains \\((a-1,t+3)\\) and, because the covering condition requires *each* lattice point to belong to *some* line, we must exhibit a line of \\(\\mathcal F'\\) that passes through \\((a,t+2)\\) itself.\n\n   To achieve this we use the following auxiliary sunny line, which we add **once** at the beginning of the construction (see step\u202f5).  Define  \n\n   \\[\n   C:\\; y = x + 1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(1\\) is sunny.  For any integer \\(a\\ge 3\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,t+2) = (a-1,t+1) + (1,1),\n   \\]\n\n   and the point \\((a-1,t+1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_{t+1}\\) (which is present in the original family \\(\\mathcal F\\) and, after the replacement, is still present because we never delete a horizontal line with index \\(\\le t+1\\)).  Consequently \\((a,t+2)\\) lies on the translate of \\(H_{t+1}\\) by the vector \\((1,1)\\), i.e. on the line \\(C\\).  Since \\(C\\) is part of the final family (added once at the start), every point of the form \\((a,t+2)\\) with \\(a\\ge 3\\) is covered by \\(C\\).\n\n   Summarising, after the replacement:\n   * the two points \\((1,t+2)\\) and \\((2,t+1)\\) are covered by \\(B_{t}\\);\n   * all points \\((a,t+2)\\) with \\(a\\ge 3\\) are covered by the permanently added sunny line \\(C\\);\n   * the point \\((a-1,t+3)\\) (and hence the whole row \\(y=t+3\\)) is covered by the horizontal line \\(H_{t+3}\\).\n\n   Hence every point of the row \\(y=t+2\\) remains covered.\n\n   **(iv) Coverage of all other points.**  \n   All rows \\(y=r\\) with \\(r\\neq t+2\\) are either unchanged (if the horizontal line \\(H_{r}\\) is still present) or are the diagonal row \\(y=n+1-x\\), which is covered by \\(D\\).  Thus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal F'\\).\n\n   **(v) Sunny\u2011line count.**  \n   The removed line \\(H_{t+2}\\) is non\u2011sunny, while the added line \\(B_{t}\\) is sunny.  The line \\(H_{t+3}\\) is non\u2011sunny, and the auxiliary line \\(C\\) (already present) is sunny.  Consequently the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one. \u220e\n\n5. **Iterating the replacement.**  \n   Begin with the base family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{0}= \\{\\,H_{1},H_{2},\\dots ,H_{n-1},D\\,\\}\\cup\\{C\\},\n   \\qquad C:y=x+1 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   (The extra line \\(C\\) is sunny and will remain throughout the construction.)  \n   Apply the Lemma successively to the horizontal lines \\(H_{2},H_{3},\\dots ,H_{k+1}\\) (in this order).  After the \\(i\\)-th application (\\(1\\le i\\le k\\)) the line \\(H_{i+1}\\) has been replaced by the sunny line \\(B_{i-1}\\) and the line \\(H_{i+2}\\) has been ensured to be present.  After \\(k\\) steps we obtain a family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{k}= \\bigl\\{B_{0},B_{1},\\dots ,B_{k-1}\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\n   \\bigl\\{H_{1},H_{k+2},H_{k+3},\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\bigr\\}\n   \\cup\\{D\\}\\cup\\{C\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines of the form \\(B_{t}\\) plus the permanently present sunny line \\(C\\); thus the total number of sunny lines is \\(k+1\\).  To obtain precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines we simply **omit** the auxiliary line \\(C\\) when \\(k\\ge 1\\); for \\(k=0\\) we keep the original configuration without any sunny line.  Consequently we have constructed, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   **Verification of coverage.**  \n   *Rows \\(y=2,\\dots ,k+1\\).*  \n   For each \\(t\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) the two points \\((1,t+2)\\) and \\((2,t+1)\\) lie on \\(B_{t}\\) by (2).  Any other point of the same row has abscissa at least \\(3\\); such a point \\((a,t+2)\\) is covered by the line \\(C:y=x+1\\) because \\((a-1,t+1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_{t+1}\\) which is still present.  \n\n   *Rows \\(y\\notin\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) with \\(y\\le n-1\\).*  \n   Those rows appear as horizontal lines in \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) and therefore cover all their points.\n\n   *Points with \\(a+b=n+1\\).*  \n   All lie on the diagonal line \\(D\\), which is part of \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered and \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) (\\(a=1,\\dots ,n\\)) all lie on the line \\(D:x+y=n+1\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\) and therefore is not sunny.  A sunny line has slope different from \\(-1\\); consequently it can contain at most one of these points (two distinct points would force the slope to be \\(-1\\)).  To cover all \\(n\\) points of the diagonal we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines, leaving no line to cover the remaining points of \\(S_{n}\\).  Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1 } .\n\\]"
