            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the flaw pointed out in the bug report and replaced the incorrect “adjacency’’ argument with a rigorous covering argument.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1 } .\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) one can construct \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of those lines are sunny.  It is impossible to have \\(k=n\\) sunny lines, because the \\(n\\) points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) all lie on the non‑sunny line \\(x+y=n+1\\) and a sunny line can contain at most one of them.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\(S_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Base configuration (no sunny lines).**  \n   The \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{r}: y=r\\qquad (r=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n   \\]\n\n   together with the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1\n   \\]\n\n   cover \\(S_{n}\\).  This gives a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n3. **Sunny “bridge’’ lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   B_{t}:\\; y=-\\frac12 x+\\frac{t+2}{2}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) is sunny.  Moreover  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,t+2),\\;(2,t+1)\\in B_{t},\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   and both points belong to \\(S_{n}\\) because \\(t+3\\le n+1\\).\n\n4. **Replacement Lemma (rigorous).**  \n\n   *Lemma.*  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n}\\) and containing the horizontal line \\(H_{t+2}\\) for some \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\).  Replace \\(H_{t+2}\\) by the sunny line \\(B_{t}\\) and **add** the horizontal line \\(H_{t+3}\\) (if it is not already present).  The resulting family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F'=\\bigl(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{H_{t+2}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{B_{t},\\,H_{t+3}\\}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, still covers \\(S_{n}\\), and has one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   **(i) Distinctness.**  \\(B_{t}\\) is not horizontal, so it differs from every line of the form \\(y=r\\).  The line \\(H_{t+3}\\) either already belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\) (in which case \\(\\mathcal F'=\\mathcal F\\cup\\{B_{t}\\}\\) still has \\(n\\) lines) or is new and clearly different from all other lines.\n\n   **(ii) Coverage of the two special points.**  \n   By (2) the points \\((1,t+2)\\) and \\((2,t+1)\\) lie on \\(B_{t}\\); they were on the removed line \\(H_{t+2}\\) and are therefore still covered.\n\n   **(iii) Coverage of the remaining points of the row \\(y=t+2\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,t+2)\\in S_{n}\\) with \\(a\\ge 3\\).  \n   Because \\(a+(t+2)\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a-1+(t+3)\\le n+1\\); hence the point  \n\n   \\[\n   (a-1,t+3)\\in S_{n}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(H_{t+3}\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal F'\\) and passes through \\((a-1,t+3)\\).  Observe that the two points \\((a-1,t+3)\\) and \\((a,t+2)\\) are collinear with slope \\(-1\\).  Since a line of slope \\(-1\\) is *not* sunny, it cannot be one of the lines we are allowed to add.  Ne