1}{2}\n     = -\\frac12 a + \\frac{n+2-(a+t+2)}{2}\n     = -\\frac12 a + \\frac{n-a-t}{2}\n     = \\frac{n - a - t - a}{2}\n     = t+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\((a,t+2)\\) lies on \\(B_{s-1}\\).  The line \\(B_{s-1}\\) is already a member of \\(\\mathcal F\\) because all sunny lines of the form \\(B_{u}\\) with \\(u\\le t-1\\) have been introduced earlier in the iterative process (see step 5 below).  Therefore \\((a,t+2)\\) remains covered after the replacement.\n\n   Consequently every point of the row \\(y=t+2\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal F'\\).\n\n   *Coverage of all other points.*  \n   For any row \\(y=r\\) with \\(r\\neq t+2\\) the situation is unchanged: either the horizontal line \\(H_{r}\\) is still present, or the row is the diagonal row \\(y=n+1-x\\) which is covered by \\(D\\).  Hence every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal F'\\).\n\n   *Sunny‑line count.*  \n   The removed line \\(H_{t+2}\\) is non‑sunny, while the added line \\(B_{t}\\) is sunny.  All other lines are unchanged, so the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one. ∎\n\n5. **Iterating the replacement.**  \n   Start from the base family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{0}= \\{H_{1},H_{2},\\dots ,H_{n-1},D\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Apply the Lemma successively to the horizontal lines \\(H_{2},H_{3},\\dots ,H_{k+1}\\) (in this order).  \n   After the \\(i\\)-th application (\\(1\\le i\\le k\\)) the line \\(H_{i+1}\\) has been replaced by the sunny line \\(B_{i-1}\\); all horizontal lines with index \\(\\ge i+2\\) remain untouched.  \n   After \\(k\\) steps we obtain the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{k}= \\bigl\\{B_{0},B_{1},\\dots ,B_{k-1}\\bigr\\}\n                \\cup\\bigl\\{H_{1},H_{k+2},H_{k+3},\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\bigr\\}\n                \\cup\\{D\\}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(B_{0},\\dots ,B_{k-1}\\);\n   * \\(n-1-k\\) horizontal lines \\(H_{1},H_{k+2},\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\);\n   * the diagonal line \\(D\\).\n\n   **Verification of coverage.**  \n   *Rows \\(y=r\\) with \\(r\\ge k+2\\).*  They are covered by the horizontal lines \\(H_{r}\\) that belong to \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\).  \n   *Rows \\(y=r\\) with \\(2\\le r\\le k+1\\).*  For such a row the two points \\((1,r)\\) and \\((2,r-1)\\) lie on the sunny line \\(B_{r-2}\\) by (2).  Any other point \\((a,r)\\) of the same row satisfies \\(a\\ge 3\\) and \\(a+r\\le n+1\\).  As shown in the proof of the Lemma, such a point belongs to the sunny line \\(B_{s-1}\\) where \\(s=n+1-(a+r)\\); because \\(a+r\\le n+1\\) we have \\(0\\le s-1\\le k-1\\), so this line is indeed present in \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\).  \n   *Row \\(y=1\\).*  The only point is \\((1,1)\\), which lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\).  \n   *Diagonal points.*  All points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on \\(D\\), which is part of \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\), and \\(\\mathcal F_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) (\\(a=1,\\dots ,n\\)) all lie on the line \\(D:x+y=n+1\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\) and therefore is not sunny.  A sunny line has slope different from \\(-1\\); consequently it can contain at most one of these diagonal points (otherwise the line through two of them would have slope \\(-1\\)).  To cover all \\(n\\) diagonal points we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  Since we are allowed only \\(n\\) lines in total, no line would remain to cover any other point of \\(S_{n}\\).  Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1 } .\n\\]"
