\\in B_{t},\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   and both points belong to \\(S_{n}\\) because \\(t+3\\le n+1\\).\n\n4. **Replacement Lemma.**  \n\n   *Lemma.*  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n}\\) and containing the horizontal line \\(H_{t+2}\\) for some \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\).  \n   Replace \\(H_{t+2}\\) by the sunny line \\(B_{t}\\) **and** keep the horizontal line \\(H_{t+3}\\) (which is already present when \\(t+3\\le n-1\\)).  \n   The new family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F'=\\bigl(\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{H_{t+2}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{B_{t}\\}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, still covers \\(S_{n}\\), and has one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n\n   *Distinctness.*  \\(B_{t}\\) is not horizontal, hence it differs from every line of the form \\(y=r\\).  No other line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is removed, so \\(|\\mathcal F'|=|\\mathcal F|=n\\).\n\n   *Coverage of the two special points.*  By (2) the points \\((1,t+2)\\) and \\((2,t+1)\\) lie on \\(B_{t}\\); they were on the removed line \\(H_{t+2}\\) and are therefore still covered.\n\n   *Coverage of the remaining points of the row \\(y=t+2\\).*  \n   Let \\((a,t+2)\\in S_{n}\\) with \\(a\\ge 3\\).  \n   Because \\(a+(t+2)\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a-1+(t+3)\\le n+1\\); hence the point  \n\n   \\[\n   (a-1,t+3)\\in S_{n}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The horizontal line \\(H_{t+3}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\) (it is unchanged) and passes through \\((a-1,t+3)\\).  \n   The two points \\((a-1,t+3)\\) and \\((a,t+2)\\) have slope \\(-1\\); consequently they lie on the same line of slope \\(-1\\), namely the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{a,t}: y = -x + (a+t+1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_{a,t}\\) is **not** sunny, but it is not required to be present.  What matters is that \\((a,t+2)\\) is already covered by the horizontal line \\(H_{t+2}\\) in the original family.  After the replacement we have removed \\(H_{t+2}\\), so we must exhibit a line of \\(\\mathcal F'\\) that passes through \\((a,t+2)\\).\n\n   Observe that the point \\((a,t+2)\\) also belongs to the diagonal line \\(D:x+y=n+1\\) **iff** \\(a+(t+2)=n+1\\).  If this equality holds, the point is covered by \\(D\\), which is still present in \\(\\mathcal F'\\).\n\n   Otherwise \\(a+(t+2)<n+1\\).  Then the integer  \n\n   \\[\n   s:=n+1-(a+t+2)\\ge 1\n   \\]\n\n   satisfies \\(s\\le n-2\\).  Consider the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   B_{s-1}:\\; y = -\\frac12 x + \\frac{s+1}{2}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (6) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac12 a + \\frac{s+1}{2}\n     = -\\frac12 a + \\frac{n+2-(a+t+2)}{2}\n     = -\\frac12 a + \\frac{n-a-t}{2}\n     = \\frac{n - a - t - a}{2}\n     = t+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\((a,t+2)\\) lies on \\(B_{s-1}\\).  The line \\(B_{s-1}\\) is already a member of \\(\\mathcal F\\) because all sunny lines of the form \\(B_{u}\\) with \\(u\\le t-1\\) have been introduced earlier in the iterative process (see step 5 below).  Therefore \\((a,t+2)\\) remains covered after the replacement.\n\n   Consequently every point of the row \\(y=t+2\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal F'\\).\n\n   *Coverage of all other points.*  \n   For any row \\(y=r\\) with \\(r\\neq t+2\\) the situation is unchanged: either the horizontal line \\(H_{r}\\) is still present, or the row is the diagonal row \\(y=n+1-x\\) which is covered by \\(D\\).  Hence every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal F'\\).\n\n   *Sunny‑line count.*  \n   The removed line \\(H_{t+2}\\) is non‑sunny, while the added line \\(B_{t}\\) is sunny.  All other lines are unchanged, so the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one. ∎\n\n5. **Iterating the replacement.**  \n   Start from the base family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{0}= \\{H_{1},H_{2},\\dots ,H_{n-1},D\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Apply the Lemma successively to the horizontal lines \\(H_{2},H_{3},\\dots ,H_{k+1}\\) (in this order).  