)). The new family \\(\\mathcal F' = (\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{H_{t+2}\\})\\cup\\{B_t\\}\\) still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, still covers \\(S_n\\), and has one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal F\\).*\n\n*The statement is plausible, but the proof must establish that every point formerly covered by \\(H_{t+2}\\) remains covered after the replacement.*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Proof of Lemma \u2013 distinctness\n> **\u201c\\(B_t\\) is not horizontal, hence it differs from every line of the form \\(y=r\\). No other line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is removed, so \\(|\\mathcal F'|=|\\mathcal F|=n\\).\u201d**\n\n*Correct.* Distinctness is clear.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Proof of Lemma \u2013 coverage of the two special points\n> **\u201cBy (2) the points \\((1,t+2)\\) and \\((2,t+1)\\) lie on \\(B_t\\); they were on the removed line \\(H_{t+2}\\) and are therefore still covered.\u201d**\n\n*Correct.* Direct substitution shows the points belong to \\(B_t\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7. Proof of Lemma \u2013 coverage of the remaining points of the row \\(y=t+2\\)\n> **\u201cLet \\((a,t+2)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\ge 3\\). Because \\(a+(t+2)\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a-1+(t+3)\\le n+1\\); hence the point \\((a-1,t+3)\\in S_n\\).\u201d**  \n> **\u201cThe horizontal line \\(H_{t+3}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F\\) (it is unchanged) and passes through \\((a-1,t+3)\\). The two points \\((a-1,t+3)\\) and \\((a,t+2)\\) have slope \\(-1\\); consequently they lie on the same line of slope \\(-1\\), namely the line \\(L_{a,t}: y = -x + (a+t+1).\\)\u201d**  \n> **\u201cThe line \\(L_{a,t}\\) is **not** sunny, but it is not required to be present. What matters is that \\((a,t+2)\\) is already covered by the horizontal line \\(H_{t+2}\\) in the original family. After the replacement we have removed \\(H_{t+2}\\), so we must exhibit a line of \\(\\mathcal F'\\) that passes through \\((a,t+2)\\).\u201d**  \n> **\u201cObserve that the point \\((a,t+2)\\) also belongs to the diagonal line \\(D:x+y=n+1\\) **iff** \\(a+(t+2