o be present,\u201d but does not explain why the existence of a non\u2011sunny line through \\((a,t+2)\\) does not help. The goal is to find a line **in** \\(\\mathcal F'\\) covering the point; merely noting another line exists is insufficient.\n\nBecause of these gaps, the claim that every point of the row \\(y=t+2\\) remains covered after the replacement is not rigorously established.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Proof of Lemma \u2013 coverage of all other rows\n> **\u201cFor any row \\(y=r\\) with \\(r\\neq t+2\\) the situation is unchanged: either the horizontal line \\(H_{r}\\) is still present, or the row is the diagonal row \\(y=n+1-x\\) which is covered by \\(D\\).\u201d**\n\n*Correct, provided that \\(D\\) indeed remains in the family (which is true, but an explicit statement would improve rigor). No gap here.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Proof of Lemma \u2013 sunny\u2011line count\n> **\u201cThe removed line \\(H_{t+2}\\) is non\u2011sunny, while the added line \\(B_{t}\\) is sunny. All other lines are unchanged, so the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one.\u201d**\n\n*Correct.*\n\n---\n\n### 10. Iterating the replacement\n> **Construction of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) (equation\u202f(8)) and the claim that it contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covers \\(S_n\\).**\n\nThe description of the iterative process relies on the Lemma being valid for each replacement. Since the Lemma\u2019s coverage argument has the gaps identified above, the induction that all rows \\(2\\le r\\le k+1\\) are covered is not fully justified. In particular, the statement:\n\n> **\u201cAny other point \\((a,r)\\) of the same row satisfies \\(a\\ge 3\\) and \\(a+r\\le n+1\\). As shown in the proof of the Lemma, such a point belongs to the sunny line \\(B_{s-1}\\) where \\(s=n+1-(a+r)\\); because \\(a+r\\le n+1\\) we have \\(0\\le s-1\\le k-1\\), so this line is indeed present in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\u201d**\n\nrepeats the unproved claim that \\((a,r)\\) lies on \\(B_{s-1}\\). The algebraic error noted earlier makes this claim doubtful, and the argument that the required \\(B_{s-1}\\) is present assumes the same ordering property that was not established.\n\nThus the construction of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) for \\(k\\ge 1\\) rests on an unproved Lemma.\n\n---\n\n### 11. Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n> **\u201cThe \\(n\\) points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) all lie on the line \\(D\\)\u2026 a sunny line can contain at most one of these diagonal points\u2026 to cover all \\(n\\) diagonal points we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines\u2026 Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\u201d**\n\nThe reasoning that a sunny line can contain at most one diagonal point is correct (since any two would give slope \\(-1\\)). However, the deduction that we need at least \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines assumes that *every* non\u2011sunny line can contain at most one diagonal point, which is false: the line \\(D\\) itself (non\u2011sunny) contains all \\(n\\) diagonal points. The argument should instead note that we already have one non\u2011sunny line \\(D\\) covering all diagonal points, leaving no room for any sunny line (since we would then have more than \\(n\\) lines). The current wording is vague and leaves a justification gap.\n\n---\n\n### 12. Final answer\n> **\u201cThe possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly \\(0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).\u201d**\n\nGiven the gaps identified in the Lemma and the construction for \\(k\\ge 1\\), the conclusion is not rigorously established.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:**  \nThe solution presents a plausible construction and a reasonable impossibility argument, but several critical steps\u2014most notably the coverage proof in the Replacement Lemma and the algebraic verification that points lie on the newly introduced sunny lines\u2014are insufficiently justified. These constitute **Justification Gaps** that must be filled for the proof to be accepted as a complete IMO\u2011level solution."

