et of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Existence of a covering with no sunny line)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines whose union contains \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;1\\le c\\le n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;1\\le c\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;2\\le c\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence they are non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently it lies simultaneously on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\).\n\nChoose the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\quad (1\\le i\\le n-2),\\qquad\nL_{n-1}:\\;y=n-1,\\qquad\nL_n:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have three possibilities:\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\); the point lies on \\(L_{n-1}\\) when \\(b=n-1\\) (which cannot happen) or on \\(L_n\\) because \\(a+b=n+1\\) or \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). In any case it is covered.\n* If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L_n\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and none of them is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Redundant non‑sunny line)\n\nIn the configuration of Lemma 1 the diagonal line \\(L_n\\colon x+y=n+1\\) is redundant; i.e. each of its points already lies on another line of the configuration.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points of \\(L_n\\) are \\((1,n), (2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \nFor the first \\(n-2\\) of them, namely \\((i,n+1-i)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\), the vertical line \\(x=i\\) (which is one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-2}\\)) also contains the point.  \nHence every point of \\(L_n\\) is covered by a line different from \\(L_n\\); therefore \\(L_n\\) is redundant. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Replacing a redundant line by a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal C\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and suppose \\(\\mathcal C\\) contains a redundant line \\(\\ell\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell'\\) is any sunny line that is distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\), then  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C'=(\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\n\\]\n\nstill covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is redundant, removing it does not uncover any point of \\(T_n\\).  \nAdding \\(\\ell'\\) cannot destroy the covering property, and distinctness is preserved by the hypothesis on \\(\\ell'\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (After a replacement a redundant non‑sunny line remains)\n\nAssume we start from the configuration of Lemma 1 and apply Lemma 3 once, removing a redundant non‑sunny line and inserting a sunny line.  \nThen the resulting configuration still contains a redundant non‑sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo cases.\n\n1. **The diagonal line \\(L_n\\) is not the line removed.**  \n   Then \\(L_n\\) is still present and, as in Lemma 2, remains redundant because the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) are unchanged.\n\n2. **The diagonal line \\(L_n\\) is the line removed.**  \n   After its removal the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) still cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except possibly those that were covered only by \\(L_n\\).  \n   Those points are exactly \\((n-1,2)\\) 