uad\nL_n:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have three possibilities:\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\); the point lies on \\(L_{n-1}\\) when \\(b=n-1\\) (which cannot happen) or on \\(L_n\\) because \\(a+b=n+1\\) or \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). In any case it is covered.\n* If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L_n\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and none of them is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Redundant non‑sunny line)\n\nIn the configuration of Lemma 1 the diagonal line \\(L_n\\colon x+y=n+1\\) is redundant; i.e. each of its points already lies on another line of the configuration.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points of \\(L_n\\) are \\((1,n), (2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \nFor the first \\(n-2\\) of them, namely \\((i,n+1-i)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\), the vertical line \\(x=i\\) (which is one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-2}\\)) also contains the point.  \nHence every point of \\(L_n\\) is covered by a line different from \\(L_n\\); therefore \\(L_n\\) is redundant. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Replacing a redundant line by a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal C\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and suppose \\(\\mathcal C\\) contains a redundant line \\(\\ell\\).  \nIf \\(\\ell'\\) is any sunny line that is distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\), then  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C'=(\\mathcal C\\setminus\\{\\ell\\})\\cup\\{\\ell'\\}\n\\]\n\nstill covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is redundant, removing it does not uncover any point of \\(T_n\\).  \nAdding \\(\\ell'\\) cannot destroy the covering property, and distinctness is preserved by the hypothesis on \\(\\ell'\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (After a replacement a redundant non‑sunny line remains)\n\nAssume we start from the configuration of Lemma 1 and apply Lemma 3 once, removing a redundant non‑sunny line and inserting a sunny line.  \nThen the resulting configuration still contains a redundant non‑sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo cases.\n\n1. **The diagonal line \\(L_n\\) is not the line removed.**  \n   Then \\(L_n\\) is still present and, as in Lemma 2, remains redundant because the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) are unchanged.\n\n2. **The diagonal line \\(L_n\\) is the line removed.**  \n   After its removal the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) still cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except possibly those that were covered only by \\(L_n\\).  \n   Those points are exactly \\((n-1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \n   The point \\((n-1,2)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=n-1\\) (which is \\(L_{n-1}\\)), and \\((n,1)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=n\\) (which is not among the original vertical lines but can be added without affecting distinctness).  \n   Consequently at least one of the vertical lines \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\)) becomes redundant after the removal of \\(L_n\\).  \n\nThus in either case a redundant non‑sunny line persists. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\(k=0\\)*, Lemma 1 provides a suitable configuration.\n\n*If \\(1\\le k\\le n\\)*, proceed inductively.\n\n* Base step (\\(k=1\\)):  \n  Start with the configuration of Lemma 1.  \n  By Lemma 2 the diagonal line is redundant; replace it with any sunny line (for instance \\(y=x+1\\)) using Lemma 3.  \n  The new configuration has exactly one sunny line.\n\n* Inductive step:  \n  Suppose we have constructed a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{t}\\) with exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines (\\(t<k\\)) that still covers \\(T_n\\).  \n  By Lemma 4 \\(\\mathcal C_{t}\\) contains a redundant non‑sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  \n  Apply Lemma 3 to replace \\(\\ell\\) by a new sunny line distinct from all existing ones, obtaining \\(\\mathcal C_{t+1}\\).  \n  Then \\(\\mathcal C_{t+1}\\) has \\(t+1\\) sunny lines and still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\nRepeating the step \\(k\\) times yields a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{k}\\) consisting of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, all distinct, that covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required set of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nSince exactly \\(n\\) lines are chosen, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence no \\(k>n\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
