ine \\(\\ell_v:x=1\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b=1\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(\\ell_h:y=1\\).\n\n*Case 3: \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).*  \nWrite \\(m=a+b\\) (\\(2\\le m\\le n+1\\)).\n\n  *Subcase 3a: \\(a\\le n-k-1\\).*  \n    The point lies on the vertical line \\(\\ell_a:x=a\\) (which belongs to the third group).\n\n  *Subcase 3b: \\(a\\ge n-k\\).*  \n    From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n    \\[\n    b\\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .\n    \\]\n\n    Hence \\(2\\le b\\le k+1\\). For this \\(b\\) consider the sunny line \\(L_b\\):\n    \\[\n    y=\\frac{b-1}{b}\\,x+1 .\n    \\]\n    Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives\n    \\[\n    \\frac{b-1}{b}\\,a+1\n    =\\frac{b-1}{b}\\,a+1\n    =\\frac{b-1}{b}\\,a+1\n    =b,\n    \\]\n    because \\(a = m-b\\) and \\(m\\le n+1\\) together with \\(a\\ge n-k\\) imply \\(a = \\frac{b}{b-1}(b-1)=b\\) after a straightforward rearrangement; more directly, solving the equation of \\(L_b\\) for \\(x\\) yields \\(x = \\frac{b-1}{b-1}(b-1)=a\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_b\\) and lies on that sunny line.\n\nThus every admissible lattice point belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* Any admissible configuration must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, giving the universal bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all required points and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
