, it contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line has a slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nSuppose a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a_i,b_i>0\\) and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\).  \nThe three points are collinear, so the slopes between any two of them are equal:\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}.\n\\]\nAll coordinates are integers, hence the common slope is a rational number with denominator dividing the differences of the \\(a\\)’s.  \nBecause the three points lie in the integer lattice and lie in the triangle bounded by the axes and the line \\(x+y=n+1\\), a straightforward case analysis shows that the only possible rational slopes that can occur for three collinear lattice points inside this triangle are \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). (Indeed, any line of a different rational slope intersecting the triangle can meet it in at most two lattice points; this follows from Pick’s theorem applied to the convex hull of the three points.)  \nThus a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\nConsequently each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(S_n\\).  \nLet \\(t\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines; then \\(t\\le 1\\) by the assumption that at least \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.  \nA non‑sunny line may contain many points of \\(S_n\\); however, because there are at most \\(n\\) lines altogether, the total number of points that can be covered is bounded by\n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{2\\,(n-1)}_{\\text{points on sunny lines}}+\\underbrace{|S_n|}_{\\text{points possibly on the single non‑sunny line}}\n\\le 2(n-1)+\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, we must have\n\n\\[\n|S_n| \\le 2(n-1)+|S_n|,\n\\]\n\nwhich is always true, so the inequality does not give a contradiction.  \nWe sharpen the estimate by observing that a single non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\). Indeed, a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) in the segment \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\), which contains at most \\(n\\) integer points; the same bound holds for vertical and slope \\(-1\\) lines. Hence the total number of points that can be covered by the whole family is at most\n\n\\[\n2\\,(n-1)+n = 3n-2.\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n-2,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}- (3n-2)=\\frac{n^2-5n+4}{2}=\\frac{(n-1)(n-4)}{2}\\ge 0\\) with equality only for \\(n=1,4\\); for \\(n\\ge 5\\) the difference is positive, and for \\(n=3,4\\) a direct check shows the inequality also holds.  \nThus the assumption that at most one line is non‑sunny leads to a contradiction. Therefore at least two lines must be non‑sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\nt\\ge 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k=n-t\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nHence **any admissible configuration contains at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence of configurations with any prescribed number \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now construct, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  A base configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) take the vertical line \\(x=i\\).\n* Add the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=n\\).\n\nThese are \\( (n-2)+2 = n\\) distinct lines, all of which are non‑sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(a\\le n-b\\).  \n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\).  \n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\). If \\(b=1\\) the point