of sunny lines by one, while preserving the covering property and the distinctness of the lines. After performing the operation exactly \\(r\\) times we obtain a configuration with\n\n\\[\nt=n-r,\\qquad k=r .\n\\]\n\nSince the operation can be applied as long as at least one non‑sunny line remains, we may continue until \\(t=2\\); i.e. we can achieve any \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le n-2\\). The resulting configuration contains exactly \\(k=r\\) sunny lines and satisfies all the required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* **Upper bound.** Any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) contains at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n* **Realisation.** For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is contained in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) and contains the whole interval \\([0,n-2]\\). The cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) remain unresolved by the present argument. ∎"
