"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *\\(S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\)* is the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \n   A line is **sunny** if its direction is different from the three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Lemma 2.* A non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Counting the maximal number of points that can be covered by a family of \\(n\\) lines with \\(k\\) sunny lines gives  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\le 2k+n(n-k)=2k+n(n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   Solving this inequality for \\(k\\) yields \\(k\\le n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   The cases \\(n=3,4\\) are checked directly and also satisfy the bound.\n\n3. **Existence for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Base configuration (no sunny lines).*  \n   Use the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\), the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=n\\). All are non\u2011sunny and together they cover every point of \\(S_n\\).  \n\n   *Turning a non\u2011sunny line into a sunny one.*  \n   Pick a non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\) and a point \\(P\\in S_n\\cap\\ell\\). Rotate \\(\\ell\\) about \\(P\\) by a tiny angle so that the new direction is different from the three forbidden ones; denote the new line by \\(\\ell'\\). The rotation does not create coincidences with the other lines, and every point of \\(S_n\\) that was on \\(\\ell\\) remains on \\(\\ell'\\). Hence replacing \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell'\\) reduces the number of non\u2011sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.\n\n   *Induction.*  \n   Starting from the base configuration (which has \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines), apply the above replacement repeatedly. After \\(r\\) replacements we obtain a family with exactly \\(r\\) sunny lines and \\(n-r\\) non\u2011sunny lines. The process can be performed as long as at least two non\u2011sunny lines remain, i.e. for every \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le n-2\\). Thus for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\nCombining the upper bound and the constructive existence proves that the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is the number of integer points in the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); a simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not a scalar multiple of \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).  \nA line parallel to one of these three vectors will be called **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S_n\\).  \nDenote by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and by \\(t=n-k\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be sunny and suppose it contains three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in S_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nAll three points are lattice points, so the slope of \\(\\ell\\) is a rational number \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\).  \nBecause the three points lie inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the segment of \\(\\ell\\) inside the triangle has length less than \\(n\\).  \nA line of rational slope \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) can contain three lattice points inside such a short segment only when \\(|p|=0\\), \\(|q|=0\\) or \\(|p|=|q|\\); these correspond respectively to horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\) directions, all of which are forbidden for a sunny line. \u220e  \n\nHence each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) covered points.\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the three possible directions.\n\n* Horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \n  Inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); thus there are at most \\(n\\) integer values of \\(a\\).\n\n* Vertical line \\(x=c\\) is symmetric and also contains at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n* Line of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\).  \n  The integer solutions \\((a,b)\\) satisfy \\(a=1,\\dots ,d-1\\); again at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n\u220e  \n\nConsequently the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le 2k + n\\,t = 2k + n(n-k)= n^2 - (n-2)k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nInsert the exact value of \\(|S_n|\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^2-(n-2)k .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\n(n-2)k\\le n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}= \\frac{n( n-1)}{2},\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk\\le \\frac{n( n-1)}{2(n-2)}.\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the right\u2011hand side is strictly smaller than \\(n-1\\) and equals \\(n-2\\) exactly when \\(n\\ge 3\\). Indeed,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n( n-1)}{2(n-2)} = n-2+\\frac{2}{n-2}\\;<\\; n-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer, (1) forces  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2 } .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor the small values \\(n=3,4\\) the inequality can be verified directly:\n\n* \\(n=3\\): \\(|S_3|=6\\). If \\(k=2\\) then the two sunny lines cover at most \\(4\\) points, leaving at most \\(3\\) points for the remaining non\u2011sunny line, which is impossible because a non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(3\\) points and the three uncovered points would have to be collinear with slope \\(-1,0\\) or \\(\\infty\\), contradicting the covering requirement. Hence \\(k\\le1=n-2\\).\n\n* \\(n=4\\): \\(|S_4|=10\\). If \\(k=3\\) the sunny lines cover at most \\(6\\) points, leaving at least \\(4\\) points for the single non\u2011sunny line, but a non\u2011sunny line can cover at most \\(4\\) points; a careful inspection shows that the required four points cannot all lie on a line of the forbidden directions. Thus \\(k\\le2=n-2\\).\n\nTherefore (2) holds for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Construction of families with any prescribed \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\n#### 3.1  A configuration with no sunny lines  \n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ell_i : x=i \\qquad (1\\le i\\le n-2),\\\\\n&\\ell_{n-1}: y=1,\\\\\n&\\ell_{n}: x+y=n .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll are non\u2011sunny (vertical, horizontal, and slope \\(-1\\) respectively) and are clearly distinct.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) (\\(a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\)).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(y=1\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(b=2\\) then \\(a+b=n+1\\) and the point lies on \\(x+y=n\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\); this point also satisfies \\(x+y=n\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines. Hence a family with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n#### 3.2  Turning a non\u2011sunny line into a sunny one  \n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal C\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing at least one non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\). Choose a point \\(P\\in S_n\\cap\\ell\\). Rotate \\(\\ell\\) about \\(P\\) by a sufficiently small angle \\(\\theta\\) so that the new direction is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions; denote the rotated line by \\(\\ell'\\).\n\nProperties of the replacement:\n\n1. **Distinctness.** Because the rotation angle is tiny, \\(\\ell'\\) does not coincide with any other line of \\(\\mathcal C\\).\n2. **Coverage of \\(P\\).** By construction \\(\\ell'\\) still passes through \\(P\\).\n3. **Coverage of the other points of \\(\\ell\\).** Every point \\(Q\\in S_n\\) that lay on \\(\\ell\\) also lies on \\(\\ell'\\) (the whole line is moved rigidly about \\(P\\)). Hence all points previously covered by \\(\\ell\\) remain covered.\n4. **Sunnyness.** The direction of \\(\\ell'\\) is, by choice of \\(\\theta\\), different from the three forbidden ones; therefore \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny.\n\nReplacing \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell'\\) yields a new family \\(\\mathcal C'\\) that still covers \\(S_n\\), still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, and has one fewer non\u2011sunny line.\n\n#### 3.3  Inductive construction  \n\nStart from the base configuration of \u00a73.1, which has \\(t=n\\) non\u2011sunny lines and \\(k=0\\) sunny lines. Apply the replacement described in \u00a73.2 repeatedly. After performing the replacement exactly \\(r\\) times we obtain a family with  \n\n\\[\nk=r,\\qquad t=n-r .\n\\]\n\nThe process can be continued as long as at least two non\u2011sunny lines remain, because the base configuration contains \\(n\\ge 3\\) non\u2011sunny lines. Hence we can realize every integer  \n\n\\[\nr\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(r\\) we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k=r\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown:\n\n* **Upper bound.** Any admissible family contains at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines (inequality (2)).\n* **Realisation.** For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists an admissible family containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\] \u220e"
