\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.1. Two fixed non\u2011sunny lines\n\\[\n\\ell_{v}: x=1 ,\\qquad \\ell_{h}: y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are parallel to the coordinate axes, hence non\u2011sunny.\n\n#### 2.2. Additional non\u2011sunny vertical lines\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with  \n\n\\[\n2\\le j\\le n-k-1,\n\\]\n\nintroduce the vertical line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{j}: x=j .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, thus non\u2011sunny, and they are distinct from each other and from \\(\\ell_{v}\\).  \nThe number of such lines is \\(n-2-k\\).\n\n#### 2.3. Sunny lines\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with  \n\n\\[\n2\\le t\\le k+1,\n\\]\n\nconsider the lattice point  \n\n\\[\nP_{t}=\\bigl(n-t+1,\\;t\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t\\ge 2\\) and \\(n-t+1\\ge 1\\), we have \\(P_{t}\\) in the required region:\n\\[\n(n-t+1)+t = n+1\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThrough \\(P_{t}\\) draw a line with slope \\(\\frac12\\) (any slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) would work). Its equation is\n\n\\[\nS_{t}:\\; y-t=\\frac12\\bigl(x-(n-t+1)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac12\\) guarantees that each \\(S_{t}\\) is sunny. Since the points \\(P_{t}\\) are distinct, the lines \\(S_{t}\\) are distinct as well. The number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\).\n\n#### 2.4. Counting the lines\n\\[\n\\underbrace{2}_{\\ell_{v},\\ell_{h}}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{(n-2-k)}_{\\text{extra vertical}}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;=\\;n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Verification that all required points are covered\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a=1\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(\\ell_{v}:x=1\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b=1\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(\\ell_{h}:y=1\\).\n\n*