ine \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) are taken.  \n* If \\(k<n-2\\) (i.e. if more than two non‑sunny lines are needed), we also take the vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=2,\\;x=3,\\dots ,x=n-k-1 .\n  \\]\n\nAltogether we have  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{2}_{x=1,\\;y=1}\\;+\\;(n-k-3)=n-k\n\\]\n\ndistinct non‑sunny lines (the count is correct also when \\(k=n-2\\), because then the list \\(x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k-1\\) is empty).\n\n#### 2.2. The sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le k+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{t}:\\; y-1=\\frac{t-1}{k}\\,(x-1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_{t}\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{t-1}{k}\\).  \nBecause \\(1\\le t-1\\le k\\), this slope is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(S_{t}\\) is sunny.  \nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{2},S_{3},\\dots ,S_{k+1}\\) are distinct, since they have distinct slopes.\n\nThus we have constructed exactly \\(n\\) lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n#### 2.3. Verification of the covering property\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-k-1\\).*  \nThe vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to the set of non‑sunny lines, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on a chosen line.\n\n*If \\(a\\ge n-k\\).*  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(b\\) belongs to \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\).\n\n  *If \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n  *If \\(b\\ge 2\\)*, then \\(b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) and we consider the sunny line \\(S_{b}\\) from (3).  \n    Substituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(S_{b}\\) gives  \n\n    \\[\n    y-1=\\frac{b-1}{k}\\,(a-1).\n    \\tag{5}\n    \\]\n\n    Because \\(a\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n    \\[\n    a-1 = (n-k)-1 + (a-(n-k)) = k + (a-(n-k)).\n    \\]\n\n    Moreover \\(a-(n-k)= (n+1-b)-(n-k)=k+1-b\\).  \n    Hence  \n\n    \\[\n    a-1 = k + (k+1-b)=2k+1-b .\n    \\]\n\n    Multiplying (5) by \\(k\\) and using the above expression for \\(a-1\\) we obtain  \n\n    \\[\n    k(y-1) = (b-1)(2k+1-b).\n    \\]\n\n    Since \\(y=b\\), the left‑hand side equals \\(k(b-1)\\); the right‑hand side simplifies to  \n\n    \\[\n    (b-1)(2k+1-b)= (b-1)(k+(k+1-b)) = k(b-1)+(b-1)(k+1-b).\n    \\]\n\n    The term \\((b-1)(k+1-b)\\) vanishes because \\(b\\le k+1\\).  \n    Consequently equality holds, showing that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (5) and therefore lies on \\(S_{b}\\).\n\nIn every possible case the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.  \n\nThus the family of \\(n\\) lines we have built indeed covers all lattice points with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) follows from the existence of the two forced non‑sunny lines.  \nThe construction above shows that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.  \n\nHence the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
