on that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\) satisfy the above conditions.\n\n   *Case 1.* \\(a\\le n-k-1\\).  \n   Then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to the set of non‑sunny lines) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   *Case 2.* \\(a\\ge n-k\\).  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1 .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(b\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\).\n\n   – If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n   – If \\(b\\ge 2\\) we claim that \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{b}\\) defined in (1).  \n     Substituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(S_{b}\\) gives  \n\n     \\[\n     b-1=\\frac{b-1}{\\,n-k\\,}(a-1). \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n     Because \\(b-1\\neq 0\\) we may cancel it, obtaining the equivalent condition  \n\n     \\[\n     a-1=n-k .\\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n     But (4) holds precisely because of the inequalities that define Case 2:\n     we have \\(a\\ge n-k\\) and, using (2),\n\n     \\[\n     a = n+1-b \\le n+1-2 = n-1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Since \\(a\\) is an integer and \\(a\\ge n-k\\), the only possible values of \\(a\\) are  \n\n     \\[\n     a=n-k\\quad\\text{or}\\quad a=n-k+1,\\dots ,n-1 .\n     \\]\n\n     If \\(a>n-k\\) then (3) would give  \n\n     \\[\n     b-1=\\frac{b-1}{\\,n-k\\,}(a-1) > b-1,\n     \\]\n\n     a contradiction. Hence the only admissible value is \\(a=n-k\\), which indeed satisfies (4).  \n     Consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_{b}\\) and lies on that sunny line.\n\n   In every possible case the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion** – the inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\) is necessary, and the explicit construction above shows that it is also sufficient. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
