\(\\ell_i\\).  \n     Moreover, for every intermediate \\(b\\) the equality\n     \\[\n        b = (n-i-1)(x-i)+1\n     \\]\n     holds for the unique \\(x\\) with \\(i\\le x\\le i+1\\); that \\(x\\) is an integer because the slope is integral, so the corresponding lattice point also lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n     Consequently every point formerly covered by the vertical line \\(x=i\\) is still covered after the replacement.\n\n     All other lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\) are left unchanged, so the new family \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1}\\) still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers the whole required set of points, and now contains one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal L_{m}\\).  \n\n     Repeating the replacement up to \\(n\\) times yields \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) with all \\(n\\) lines sunny.\n\n   By induction we have produced, for each \\(m\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a configuration with exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines. Hence every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable.\n\nSince no configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), undefined (vertical), and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The trivial lower bound \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound \\(k\\le n\\)\n\nWe are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; consequently at most