(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\).  \nWriting the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\) as  \n\n\\[\ny-1=(n-i-1)(x-i),\n\\]\n\nand substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=1\\); thus the only point of column \\(i\\) that lies on \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\((i,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points of column \\(i\\) are covered by the line \\(x=i+1\\), which is still present in \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) because we removed only the line \\(x=i\\).  \n\nHence every point of \\(S_n\\) that was covered by the removed vertical line \\(x=i\\) remains covered after the replacement: the point \\((i,1)\\) is now on \\(\\ell_i\\), and the points \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) are on the still\u2011existing line \\(x=i+1\\) (note that for such \\(b\\) we have \\(i+1+b\\le n+2\\), i.e. \\(b\\le n+1-(i+1)\\), so \\((i+1,b)\\) indeed belongs to \\(S_n\\)).  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_m\\setminus\\{x=i\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_i\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(S_n\\), and contains exactly one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal L_m\\).  \n\nThe only exceptional situation occurs when \\(i=n-1\\); then the slope in (1) equals \\(0\\) and \\(\\ell_{n-1}\\) would be horizontal, which is not sunny. In this case we replace the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) by the sunny line through the points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,2)\\):\n\\[\n\\ell_{n-1}':\\; y = (1)(x-(n-1))+1 = x-n+2 .\n\\]\nIts slope is \\(1\\neq 0,-1\\), so \\(\\ell_{n-1}'\\) is sunny, and the same covering argument as above applies.\n\nThus the inductive step is valid for every \\(i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBy induction we obtain, for each \\(m=0,1,\\dots ,n\\), a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) with exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines.  \n\nSince no configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\, } .\n\\]"
