ted because the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) are left unchanged.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_m\\setminus\\{x=i\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_i\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, still covers every point of \\(S_n\\), and now contains exactly one more sunny line than \\(\\mathcal L_m\\).\n\nThus, starting from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) and applying the above replacement repeatedly, we obtain families \\(\\mathcal L_1,\\mathcal L_2,\\dots ,\\mathcal L_n\\) with respectively \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(m\\) with \\(0\\le m\\le n\\), a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines.  \nSince no configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
