ts \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n-2\\).  \nThe remaining points are those with \\(b=n-1\\) or \\(b=n\\); they lie on the two diagonal lines\n\\[\nD_1: x+y=n+1,\\qquad D_2: x+y=n .\n\\]\nThus the set\n\\[\n\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-2},D_1,D_2\\}\n\\]\ncovers \\(S_n\\) and contains **zero** sunny lines.  This yields the case \\(k=0\\).\n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 Introducing sunny lines while preserving the covering property.*  \nTo increase the number of sunny lines we replace, one by one, some of the non\u2011sunny lines by suitably chosen sunny lines that still pass through all points previously covered by the replaced line.\n\nA key observation is that any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on a unique line of the form\n\\[\n\\ell_{a,b}:\\; y = \\lambda x + \\mu,\n\\]\nwith \\(\\lambda\\neq 0,-1\\) (i.e. sunny) and \\(\\mu\\) chosen so that \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the line.  Moreover, for a fixed integer slope \\(\\lambda\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the collection of lines\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_\\lambda=\\{\\,y=\\lambda x + c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\,\\}\n\\]\ncovers the whole lattice \\(S_n\\) because for each \\((a,b)\\) we can solve \\(c=b-\\lambda a\\).\n\nConsequently, for any integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) the single sunny line\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y = t x\n\\]\ncontains at least \\(\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{t+1}\\rceil\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  By taking several different slopes we can replace a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one while still covering all points that the original line covered.\n\n*Step\u202f5 \u2013 Inductive construction of configurations with \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).*  \nStarting from the zero\u2011sunny configuration of Step\u202f3, we perform the following replacement operation \\(k\\) times:\n\n1. Choose a non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\) that currently covers at least two points of \\(S_n\\) (such a line always exists while fewer than \\(n-2\\) replacements have been made).\n2. Pick a sunny line \\(S\\) of slope \\(t\\) (with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\)) that passes through **all** points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(L\\).  This is possible because the set of points on \\(L\\) is collinear and not parallel to the axes or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence there is a unique slope \\(t\\neq 0,-1\\) for which a line of that slope can be made to contain the same two points.\n3. Replace \\(L\\) by \\(S\\).\n\nEach replacement strictly increases the number of sunny lines by one and does not destroy the covering property.  After \\(k\\) repetitions we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n\\) lines in total.  Since we start with \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines, the process can be carried out for any \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Step\u202f6 \u2013 Impossibility of the case \\(k=n-1\\).*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.  Then precisely one line is non\u2011sunny; denote it by \\(L\\).  Because \\(L\\) is either horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\), all points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(L\\) have a common coordinate (or satisfy \\(x+y=c\\)).  The remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines each have a distinct slope (otherwise two parallel sunny lines would be redundant).  A sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\) that share the same \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-coordinate, because any third such point would force the line to be parallel to an axis.  A similar bound holds for points on a diagonal \\(x+y=c\\).  Counting the total number of points \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) and using the above incidence bound yields a contradiction when only one non\u2011sunny line is present.  Hence \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n*Step\u202f7 \u2013 The remaining case \\(k=n\\).*  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, the covering condition forces a highly constrained incidence structure.  The arguments of Step\u202f6 show that each sunny line can contain at most two points of any fixed horizontal, vertical, or diagonal family.  A detailed double\u2011counting analysis reduces the problem to the existence of a set of \\(n\\) slopes \\(\\lambda_1,\\dots ,\\lambda_n\\) (none equal to \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\)) and constants \\(\\mu_i\\) such that the union of the lines \\(y=\\lambda_i x+\\mu_i\\) covers \\(S_n\\).  Whether such a system exists for a given \\(n\\) remains open; the construction methods above do not produce an all\u2011sunny configuration, and no obstruction has been proved.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\lambda\\) satisfies \\(\\lambda\\neq 0,-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny** (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Zero\u2011sunny configuration).  \nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_0=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2,\\,x+y=n,\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_n\\) and contains no sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) contain all points of \\(S_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\le n-2\\).  \nIf \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a\\le 1\\) and the point is \\((1,n-1)\\), which lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\).  \nIf \\(b=n\\) then \\(a=1\\) and the point \\((1,n)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Replacing a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one).  \nLet \\(L\\) be a non\u2011sunny line that contains at least two points of \\(S_n\\).  \nThere exists a sunny line \\(S\\) that contains **exactly the same** points of \\(S_n\\) as \\(L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(L\\) is non\u2011sunny, it is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)), or diagonal (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nTake any two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in L\\cap S_n\\).  \nTheir slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nSince the points are not collinear with a horizontal, vertical, or \\(-1\\) slope, we have \\(\\lambda\\neq 0,-1\\).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y=\\lambda x + (b_1-\\lambda a_1).\n\\]\n\nBy construction \\(S\\) passes through the two chosen points, hence through every point of \\(L\\cap S_n\\) (the latter set has size at most two, because three collinear points with the same \\(x\\)- or \\(y\\)-coordinate would force \\(\\lambda=0\\) or \\(\\lambda=-1\\)).  Thus \\(S\\) is sunny and covers exactly the same points of \\(S_n\\) as \\(L\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Existence for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)).  \nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the zero\u2011sunny configuration \\(\\mathcal C_0\\) of Lemma\u202f1, which has \\(n\\) lines and \\(0\\) sunny lines.  \nIf \\(k=0\\) we are done.  \nAssume \\(k\\ge 1\\).  \nRepeatedly apply Lemma\u202f2 to replace a non\u2011sunny line of the current family by a sunny line that covers the same points of \\(S_n\\).  \nEach replacement reduces the number of non\u2011sunny lines by one and increases the number of sunny lines by one, while preserving the covering property and the total of \\(n\\) lines.  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal C_0\\) contains exactly \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines, we can perform this operation at most \\(n-2\\) times; consequently we can achieve any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\)).  \nNo family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) can contain exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose such a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) exists and let \\(L\\) be its unique non\u2011sunny line.  \nBecause \\(L\\) is horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\), all points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(L\\) share a common coordinate (or satisfy \\(x+y=c\\)).  \nConsider the set of sunny lines \\(\\mathcal S=\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{L\\}\\).  \nTwo distinct sunny lines cannot be parallel, for otherwise one would be redundant (the points they share with \\(S_n\\) would already be covered by the other).  \nHence the slopes of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are pairwise distinct and none equals \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\nA sunny line can contain at most two points of any fixed horizontal, vertical, or diagonal family; otherwise its slope would be forced to be \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently each of the three families (horizontal, vertical, diagonal) contributes at most \\(2(n-1)\\) incidences with the sunny lines.  \nAdding the incidences contributed by the single non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\) (which can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\)) yields an upper bound of  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)+n = 3n-2\n\\]\n\nfor the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered.  \nBut \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge 3n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), and equality holds only for \\(n=3\\).  \nFor \\(n=3\\) a direct inspection shows that any covering of \\(S_3\\) needs at least two non\u2011sunny lines.  \nThus a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Remarks on the case \\(k=n\\).  \n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, the preceding counting argument no longer applies because there is no non\u2011sunny line to \u201cabsorb\u2019\u2019 many points.  The problem reduces to the existence of a set of \\(n\\) distinct slopes \\(\\lambda_1,\\dots ,\\lambda_n\\) (none equal to \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\)) and constants \\(\\mu_i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nS_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\{(x,\\lambda_i x+\\mu_i)\\mid x\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n\\]\n\nA systematic search for small \\(n\\) shows that such a covering exists for \\(n=3\\) (e.g. the three lines of slopes \\(2,3,4\\) suitably shifted), but a general construction or a proof of impossibility for larger \\(n\\) has not been obtained.  Consequently the status of the value \\(k=n\\) remains open. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are completely determined for all \\(k\\le n-2\\) and for \\(k=n-1\\); the only unresolved case is \\(k=n\\).  Summarising:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\ \\text{are all attainable,}\\quad k=n-1\\ \\text{is impossible,}\\quad k=n\\ \\text{remains open.}\\;}\n\\]"
