S_n\\) as \\(L\\) (Lemma\u202f2).  Thus a **single** replacement raises the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.\n\n*Step\u202f5 \u2013 A base configuration with many \u201ctwo\u2011point\u2019\u2019 non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-1\\) consider the diagonal line  \n\\[\nD_t:\\; x+y=t+1 .\n\\]\nWhen \\(t\\le n-2\\) the line \\(D_t\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in exactly the two points \\((1,t)\\) and \\((t,1)\\).  \nCollect the \\(n-2\\) lines  \n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n-1}\\}.\n\\]\nThese \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines together cover **all** points of \\(S_n\\) except those lying on the two outer diagonals\n\\[\nx+y=n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\nAdd these two outer diagonals (which are also non\u2011sunny) to obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines that covers \\(S_n\\).  In this family every line except the two outer diagonals contains exactly two points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Step\u202f6 \u2013 Obtaining any prescribed number of sunny lines.*  \nStarting from the family described in Step\u202f5 (which has \\(0\\) sunny lines), perform the following operation repeatedly:\n\n1. Choose a non\u2011sunny line that contains exactly two points of \\(S_n\\) (such a line exists as long as fewer than \\(n-2\\) replacements have been made).\n2. Replace it by the unique sunny line through the same two points (Lemma\u202f2).\n\nEach replacement increases the number of sunny lines by one and does not affect the covering property.  Because there are \\(n-2\\) replaceable lines, we can carry out the operation any number \\(k\\) of times with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  Hence for every such \\(k\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists (Lemma\u202f3).\n\n*Step\u202f7 \u2013 Non\u2011existence of the case \\(k=n-1\\).*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) lines contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  Let \\(L\\) be the unique non\u2011sunny line.  Every sunny line has a slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently two distinct sunny lines cannot be parallel (otherwise one would be redundant).  Hence the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines have pairwise distinct slopes.\n\nA sunny line can contain at most two points of any fixed horizontal, vertical, or diagonal family; three such points would force the line\u2019s slope to be \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\).  Therefore each of the three families contributes at most \\(2(n-1)\\) incidences with the sunny lines.  The non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Consequently the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered satisfies\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le 2(n-1)+n = 3n-2 .\n\\]\nBut \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge 3n-2\\) for all \\(n\\ge 3\\), with equality only at \\(n=3\\).  A direct inspection of the case \\(n=3\\) shows that at least two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary.  Hence a covering with exactly one non\u2011sunny line (i.e. \\(k=n-1\\)) cannot exist (Lemma\u202f4).\n\n*Step\u202f8 \u2013 The case \\(k=n\\).*  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, the previous counting argument no longer applies.  The problem becomes: does there exist a set of \\(n\\) distinct slopes \\(\\lambda_1,\\dots ,\\lambda_n\\neq0,-1\\) and constants \\(\\mu_i\\) such that\n\\[\nS_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\{(x,\\lambda_i x+\\mu_i)\\mid x\\in\\mathbb R\\}\\,?\n\\]\nFor \\(n=3\\) an explicit example can be written, but for general \\(n\\) no construction nor obstruction is known.  Thus the status of \\(k=n\\) remains open.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\lambda\\) satisfies \\(\\lambda\\neq0,-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny** (horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Zero\u2011sunny covering).  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_0=\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2,\\;x+y=n,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(S_n\\) and contains no sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) contain all points of \\(S_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\le n-2\\).  \nIf \\(b=n-1\\) then \\(a\\le 1\\) and the point is \\((1,n-1)\\), which lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n\\).  \nIf \\(b=n\\) then \\(a=1\\) and the point \\((1,n)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Replacing a two\u2011point non\u2011sunny line).  \nLet \\(L\\) be a non\u2011sunny line that meets \\(S_n\\) in exactly two points \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\).  \nThen there exists a sunny line \\(S\\) that contains precisely the same two points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause the two points are distinct, the slope  \n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\n\\]\nis well defined and, since the points are not collinear with a horizontal line (\\(\\lambda\\neq0\\)) nor with a line of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(\\lambda\\neq-1\\)), we have \\(\\lambda\\neq0,-1\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y=\\lambda x+(b_1-\\lambda a_1).\n\\]\n\nBy construction \\(S\\) passes through both \\((a_1,b_1)\\) and \\((a_2,b_2)\\); because \\(S_n\\cap L\\) consists of exactly these two points, \\(S\\) contains precisely the same points of \\(S_n\\) as \\(L\\).  Moreover \\(\\lambda\\neq0,-1\\), so \\(S\\) is sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (A base configuration with many two\u2011point non\u2011sunny lines).  \nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B=\\{\\,D_t:x+y=t+1\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=n,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines, covers \\(S_n\\), and all lines except the two outer diagonals contain exactly two points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\(2