es.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a contrary family \\(\\mathcal F\\) exists and let \\(L\\) be its unique non\u2011sunny line.  \nAll sunny lines have slopes different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore two distinct sunny lines cannot be parallel, for parallel sunny lines would have the same slope and one would be redundant in covering \\(S_n\\).  Hence the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines have pairwise distinct slopes.\n\nConsider the three families of non\u2011sunny directions:\nhorizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), and diagonal (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \nA sunny line can intersect any one of these families in at most two points of \\(S_n\\); three points with a common \\(x\\)-coordinate (or \\(y\\)-coordinate, or constant sum) would force the line\u2019s slope to be \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\).  Consequently each of the three families contributes at most \\(2(n-1)\\) incidences with the sunny lines.  \n\nThe single non\u2011sunny line \\(L\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the longest possible horizontal, vertical, or diagonal segment inside the triangle has length \\(n\\)).  \n\nThus the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered satisfies\n\\[\n|S_n|\\le 2(n-1)+n = 3n-2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  For \\(n\\ge4\\) we have \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2>3n-2\\); for \\(n=3\\) a direct check shows that at least two non\u2011sunny lines are required.  Hence the assumed configuration cannot exist. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Remarks on the case \\(k=n\\).  \n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, the incidence bound used in Lemma\u202f5 no longer applies.  The problem reduces to the existence of slopes \\(\\lambda_1,\\dots ,\\lambda_n\\neq0,-1\\) and intercepts \\(\\mu_i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nS_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\{(x,\\lambda_i x+\\mu_i)\\mid x\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) an explicit construction (e.g. the three lines of slopes \\(2,3,4\\) suitably shifted) works, but for general \\(n\\) no construction nor impossibility proof is known.  Hence the value \\(k=n\\) remains an open problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\ \\text{(all attainable)},\\qquad k=n-1\\ \\text{(impossible)},\\qquad k=n\\ \\text{(status open)}\\; } .\n\\]"
