the triangle, and by  \n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge 1\\},\\qquad b=1,\\dots ,n,\n\\]\n\nthe *rows*.  \nExplicitly  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\\qquad\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nA line that is **not** vertical meets a column \\(C_a\\) in at most one point of \\(C_a\\).  \nA line that is **not** horizontal meets a row \\(R_b\\) in at most one point of \\(R_b\\).\n\n*Proof.*  If a line is not vertical it has an equation of the form \\(x=mx_0+y_0\\) with non‑zero slope, hence for a fixed \\(x=a\\) it can intersect the line in at most one point. The same reasoning applies to rows. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nIf a column \\(C_a\\) does **not** contain its vertical line \\(x=a\\) among the chosen lines, then at least \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\) distinct lines are required to cover all points of \\(C_a\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma 1 every line that is not \\(x=a\\) contributes at most one point of \\(C_a\\). Since \\(|C_a|\\) points have to be covered, we need at least \\(|C_a|\\) different lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nNow suppose that for some \\(a\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is missing from \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThen, by Lemma 2, the set \\(\\mathcal L\\) must contain at least \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\) lines distinct from \\(x=a\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\le n\\), we have \\(|C_a|\\ge 2\\); consequently the total number of lines would be at least  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{the missing vertical line})\\;+\\;(n+1-a)\\;\\ge\\;n+1,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting the hypothesis that \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).\n\nHence **every** column \\(C_a\\) must contain its vertical line \\(x=a\\) in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L\\supset\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n\\) lines already account for all \\(n\\) lines required, so no other line can be present. In particular, \\(\\mathcal S=\\varnothing\\) and \\(s=0\\).\n\nThus a configuration with a sunny line cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe covering condition can be satisfied with \\(n\\) lines, and any such family must consist solely of the \\(n\\) vertical lines (or, symmetrically, of the \\(n\\) horizontal lines).  \nConsequently the number of sunny lines is forced to be zero.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]\n\nThis completes the determination of all admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
