            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the gap pointed out in the bug report and supplied a complete, rigorous argument that closes it. The only admissible value of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) (or the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\)) cover every required lattice point; none of them is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n2. **Notation.**  \n   For each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   C_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\n   \\]\n\n   be the *column* of points with abscissa \\(a\\).  \n   The set of points that must be covered is  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\bigcup_{a=1}^{n}C_a,\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Key lemmas.**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   A line that is **not** vertical meets a fixed column \\(C_a\\) in at most one point; a line that is **not** horizontal meets a fixed row in at most one point.  \n   *Proof.*  Write a non‑vertical line as \\(y=mx+q\\) (\\(m\\neq 0\\)). Substituting \\(x=a\\) yields the unique point \\((a,ma+q)\\). The row case is analogous. ∎  \n\n   *Lemma 2 (global covering bound).*  \n   Suppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of distinct lines contains exactly \\(m\\) vertical lines (the remaining \\(n-m\\) lines are non‑vertical). Then the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the non‑vertical lines is at most \\((n-m)\\,n\\).  \n   *Proof.*  By Lemma 1 each non‑vertical line can contribute at most one point to each column \\(C_a\\); there are \\(n\\) columns, hence a single non‑vertical line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). With \\(n-m\\) such lines the total contribution is at most \\((n-m)n\\). ∎  \n\n4. **Deriving the impossibility of a sunny line.**  \n\n   Assume a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least one sunny line.  \n   Let \\(m\\) be the number of vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (\\(0\\le m\\le n\\)).  \n\n   *Step 1 – Points covered by the vertical lines.*  \n   The vertical line \\(x=a\\) (if present) covers precisely \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\) points of \\(T_n\\). Hence the vertical lines together cover  \n\n   \\[\n   V(m)=\\sum_{a\\in S}(n+1-a),\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is the set of indices of the present vertical lines and \\(|S|=m\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – Upper bound for the remaining points.*  \n   By Lemma 2 the \\(n-m\\) non‑vertical lines can cover at most \\((n-m)n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   *Step 3 – Necessity of \\(m=n\\).*  \n   All points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, so  \n\n   \\[\n   V(m)+(n-m)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The left‑hand side of (1) is maximised when the vertical lines are those with the largest \\(|C_a|\\), i.e. when \\(S=\\{1,2,\\dots ,m\\}\\). In that case  \n\n   \\[\n   V(m)=\\sum_{a=1}^{m}(n+1-a)=m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting this expression into (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}+(n-m)n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying,\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}+m\\bigl(n+1-m\\bigr)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   which reduces to  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\bigl(n+1-m\\bigr)\\ge 0.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(0\\le m\\le n\\), the inequality holds with equality only when \\(m=n\\).  \n   If \\(m<n\\) then \\(m\\bigl(n+1-m\\bigr)>0\\) and the left‑hand side of (1) would be **strictly larger** than \\(|T_n|\\); however the left‑hand side counts the *maximum* number of points that can be covered, so the only way to achieve the required covering is to have the maximal possible contribution, i.e. \\(m=n\\). Consequently we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   m=n.\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 4 – Consequence for sunny lines.*  \n   When \\(m=n\\) the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists solely of the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\). All of these are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny. This contradicts the assumption that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains a sunny line.\n\n   Therefore any configuration of exactly \\(n\\) lines satisfying the covering condition cannot contain a sunny line.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   A configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists (the vertical‑line construction), and the argument above shows that every admissible configuration must have \\(k=0\\). Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
