e \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) into (4) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-V(m)\\le (n-m)n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging,\n\n   \\[\n   V(m)\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-m)n\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n^{2}+mn\n   =\\frac{n}{2}+mn .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 4 – Maximising the left‑hand side of (6).*  \n   For a fixed \\(m\\) the sum \\(V(m)\\) in (2) is largest when the vertical lines are taken for the smallest abscissae, i.e. when  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{1,2,\\dots ,m\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   In that case  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{\\max }(m)=\\sum_{a=1}^{m}(n+1-a)\n   =m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore a necessary condition for the existence of a covering family with exactly \\(m\\) vertical lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n}{2}+mn .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying (8),\n\n   \\[\n   m(n+1)-\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}-mn-\\frac{n}{2}\\ge 0\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   \\frac{m( n+1-m)}{2}\\ge \\frac{n}{2}.\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Multiplying by \\(2\\),\n\n   \\[\n   m\\bigl(n+1-m\\bigr)\\ge n .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 5 – Solving inequality (10).*  \n   The quadratic function \\(f(m)=m\\bigl(n+1-m\\bigr)= -m^{2}+(n+1)m\\) attains its maximum at \\(m=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\), where  \n\n   \\[\n   f_{\\max }=\\frac{(n+1)^{2}}{4}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n   \\[\n   f_{\\max }=\\frac{(n+1)^{2}}{4}< n(n+1)/2 =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   but we only need \\(f(m)\\ge n\\). Direct inspection shows that the inequality \\(m\\bigl(n+1-m\\bigr)\\ge n\\) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\ge n .\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, for \\(0\\le m\\le n-1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\bigl(n+1-m\\bigr)\\le (n-1)\\bigl(n+1-(n-1)\\bigr)=(n-1)2=2n-2<n\n   \\]\n\n   (the last inequality holds because \\(n\\ge3\\)). Hence the only possible integer solution of (10) is  \n\n   \\[\n   m=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 6 – Consequence for sunny lines.*  \n   The equality \\(m=n\\) means that **all** \\(n\\) lines are vertical. Vertical lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny. This contradicts the assumption that the family contains a sunny line.\n\n   Consequently any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition cannot contain a sunny line.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction with the \\(n\\) vertical lines shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable. The counting argument above proves that every admissible configuration must have \\(k=0\\). Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
