\\), the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-1=\\alpha_i (x-i)\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nis sunny and passes through \\(P_i\\).  \nThe slopes \\(\\alpha_i\\) are distinct, hence the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are\npairwise distinct.\n\nNow discard from the family \\(\\{L_s\\}\\) the \\(k\\) lines whose constants equal\n\\(i+1\\) (i.e. the lines \\(L_{i+1}\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).  \nThe remaining set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_s\\mid s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{i+1\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct lines, all with slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered  \n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf \\(a+b\\neq i+1\\) for all \\(i\\) (i.e. the sum \\(a+b\\) is not among\n\\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\)), then the line \\(L_{a+b}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) and\ncontains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nIf \\(a+b=i+1\\) for some \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\), then \\(a=i\\) and \\(b=1\\); hence\n\\((a,b)=P_i\\), which lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\) by construction (1).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines\n\\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Counting sunny lines  \n\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are sunny, while all lines in\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore not sunny.  \nConsequently the constructed configuration contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Conclusion  \n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nConversely, the example with the \\(n\\) lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\) shows that\n\\(k=0\\) is attainable, and taking all \\(n\\) lines sunny is possible by the\nconstruction above with \\(k=n\\). Hence **all** values in the interval are\nrealizable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]"
