            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) lie on one of the lines \\(D_s\\); indeed, for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have\n\\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \n\nFix \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is **not** parallel to \\(D_s\\) (i.e. its slope is different from \\(-1\\)), then \\(\\ell\\) contains at most one point of the set  \n\n\\[\nT_s:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}=D_s\\cap T_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of \\(T_s\\) have the same sum \\(a+b=s\\); the line joining them therefore has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{(s-a_2)-(s-a_1)}{a_2-a_1}= -1 .\n\\]\n\nHence any line that contains two different points of \\(T_s\\) must have slope \\(-1\\), i.e. must be parallel to \\(D_s\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Main argument  \n\nAssume that we have \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) such that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{i\\mid \\ell_i\\text{ is sunny}\\}.\n\\]\n\nRecall that a line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nConsider a fixed diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \nThe set \\(T_s\\) contains at least two points because \\(n\\ge 3\\) implies \\(s\\ge 2\\) and \\(s\\le n+1\\).  \nBy Lemma 1, any line that is **not** parallel to \\(D_s\\) can cover at most one point of \\(T_s\\).  \nTherefore, to cover **all** points of \\(T_s\\) we must have at least one line among \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) that is parallel to \\(D_s\\); such a line necessarily has slope \\(-1\\).\n\nSince the diagonals \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) are pairwise distinct (they have different intercepts), the \\(n\\) covering lines must contain **exactly one** line parallel to each diagonal. Consequently the covering set is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(-1\\); none of them is sunny. Hence  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) are distinct, none is sunny, and, as observed at the beginning, they together contain every point of \\(T_n\\). Thus the required configuration exists for \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility for \\(k\\ge 1\\)\n\nIf \\(k\\ge 1\\), at least one of the covering lines would be sunny, i.e. would have slope different from \\(-1\\). By Lemma 1 such a line could intersect a given diagonal \\(D_s\\) in at most one point, leaving the other points of that diagonal uncovered, contradicting the covering requirement. Hence no configuration with \\(k\\ge 1\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nThe only non‑negative integer \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
