            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(S_{n}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_{n}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is **not** parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}:y=0,\\qquad \\ell_{y}:x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{d}:x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 1  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is a sunny line, then \\(|\\ell\\cap S_{n}|\\le 1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the equation of \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nAssume that \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2})\\in S_{n}\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nb_{2}-b_{1}=m(a_{2}-a_{1}). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBoth differences on the left‑hand side are integers, hence \\(m\\) is rational; write \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>0\\).  \nEquation (2) becomes \\(q(b_{2}-b_{1})=p(a_{2}-a_{1})\\).  \nSince \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), we must have \\(q\\mid (a_{2}-a_{1})\\).  \nBecause \\(1\\le a_{i}\\le n\\), \\(|a_{2}-a_{1}|\\le n-1\\); therefore \\(q\\le n-1\\).\n\nIf \\(q\\ge2\\) then \\(|a_{2}-a_{1}|\\ge q\\ge2\\), and (2) forces \\(|b_{2}-b_{1}|\\ge2\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n(a_{2}+b_{2})-(a_{1}+b_{1})= (a_{2}-a_{1})+(b_{2}-b_{1})\\ge 4,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the fact that both sums are at most \\(n+1\\) (recall \\(n\\ge3\\)).  \nHence \\(q=1\\) and \\(m\\) is an integer.\n\nIf \\(m\\neq -1,0\\) (the sunny condition) then (2) gives \\(b_{2}-b_{1}=m(a_{2}-a_{1})\\) with \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{-1,0\\}\\).  \nThus \\(a_{2}-a_{1}=b_{2}-b_{1}\\).  \nAdding the two equalities yields  \n\n\\[\n(a_{2}+b_{2})-(a_{1}+b_{1})=2(a_{2}-a_{1}) .\n\\]\n\nSince both sides are bounded by \\(n+1\\), the only possibility is \\(a_{2}=a_{1}\\), contradicting distinctness. ∎\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we obtain two immediate consequences.\n\n* Each sunny line contributes **at most one** point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n* A non‑sunny line (parallel to \\(\\ell_{x},\\ell_{y}\\) or \\(\\ell_{d}\\)) contains at least two points of \\(S_{n}\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\) never meets \\(S_{n}\\) because all its points have non‑positive coordinates).\n\nLet a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nDenote by \\(t\\) the total number of points of \\(S_{n}\\) covered by the non‑sunny lines.  \nSince each non‑sunny line covers at least two points,  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge 2\\,(n-k). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_{n}\\) must be covered, so  \n\n\\[\n|S_{n}|\\le t+k\\le 2\\,(n-k)+k = 2n-k. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\le 2n-k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus **no admissible family can contain more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Existence of families for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now show that each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can actually be realised.\n\n#### Lemma 2 (grid covering)  \nFor any integer \\(m\\ge1\\) the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_{m}:=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=m\\}\\cup\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le 2m+1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\) satisfy the hypotheses.  \nIf \\(a\\le m\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal G_{m}\\)) contains the point.  \nOtherwise \\(a>m\\); the inequality \\(a+b\\le 2m+1\\) then forces \\(b\\le m\\), and the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (also in \\(\\mathcal G_{m}\\)) contains the point. ∎\n\n\n\nTake \\(m=n-2\\).  \nLemma 2 tells us that the \\(2(n-2)\\) lines of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal G_{n-2}= \\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2\\}\\cup\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nalready cover **all** points of \\(S_{n}\\) because for any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1 = 2(n-2)+1\\).\n\nThese \\(2(n-2)\\) lines are all non‑sunny (they are horizontal or vertical).  \nFrom them we shall keep exactly \\(n\\) lines, delete \\(n-2\\) of them, and replace precisely \\(k\\) of the deleted lines by sunny ones.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1 Choosing the base non‑sunny lines  \n\nSelect the following \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B:=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y_{h}=k+1\\}\\cup\\{x=1,\\dots ,x_{v}=n-k-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(h=k+1\\) and \\(v=n-k-1\\).  \nClearly \\(h+v=n\\) and every line of \\(\\mathcal B\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal G_{n-2}\\); hence all are non‑sunny.\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal B\\subseteq\\mathcal G_{n-2}\\), the points of \\(S_{n}\\) that are **not** covered by \\(\\mathcal B\\) are exactly those that were covered only by the \\(n-2\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal G_{n-2}\\) that we omitted.  \nThere are at most \\(n-2\\) such points, and in fact at most \\(k\\) of them will be needed later (see below).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2 Inserting sunny lines  \n\nWe now replace \\(k\\) of the omitted lines by \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nProceed as follows.\n\n*Step 1.* List the points of \\(S_{n}\\) that are not covered by \\(\\mathcal B\\).  \nBecause \\(\\mathcal B\\) already contains \\(k+1\\) horizontal and \\(n-k-1\\) vertical lines, the uncovered points are precisely those with  \n\n\\[\na\\in\\{k+2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b\\in\\{k+2,\\dots ,n-2\\},\n\\]\n\nsubject to \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThe total number of such points is at most \\(n-2\\); we select any \\(k\\) of them and denote them by \\(P_{1},\\dots ,P_{k}\\).\n\n*Step 2.* For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) draw a line \\(\\ell_{i}\\) through \\(P_{i}\\) whose slope is **neither** \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).  \nThis is always possible: choose an arbitrary real number \\(\\alpha_{i}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i}:\\; y-\\!y_{i}= \\alpha_{i}\\,(x-\\!x_{i}),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(P_{i}=(x_{i},y_{i})\\).  \nEach \\(\\ell_{i}\\) is sunny, distinct from the others (choose distinct \\(\\alpha_{i}\\)), and passes through a point of \\(S_{n}\\) that was previously uncovered.\n\n*Step 3.* Form the final family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F:=\\mathcal B\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists of \\(h+v+k=n+k\\) lines, but we have **replaced** exactly \\(k\\) of the omitted lines of \\(\\mathcal G_{n-2}\\) by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i}\\); therefore the total number of lines is precisely \\(n\\).  \nBy construction every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is either already covered by \\(\\mathcal B\\) or is one of the points \\(P_{i}\\) and hence lies on the corresponding sunny line \\(\\ell_{i}\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal F\\) satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince the above procedure works for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), all these values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n* **Upper bound:** any admissible configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines can have at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n* **Realisation:** for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
