\\[\n   a\\ge n-k\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b\\ge n-k .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, if \\(a\\le n-k-1\\) then \\(a\\) equals one of the numbers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1\\) and the point lies on the corresponding vertical line; similarly for \\(b\\).  \n   Under condition (6) the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   a=n-k+r,\\qquad b=n-k+1-r\\qquad\\text{for some }r\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the set of uncovered points is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{r}=(\\,n-k+r,\\;n-k+1-r\\,),\\qquad r=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f4 \u2013 sunny lines covering the remaining points.*  \n   For each \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{r}\\colon y-x = (n-2k+1)+2(r-1) .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of every \\(S_{r}\\) is \\(+1\\); hence each \\(S_{r}\\) is sunny (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   Substituting the coordinates from (8) into (9) shows that \\(P_{r}\\) lies on \\(S_{r}\\).  \n   Because the right\u2011hand sides of (9) are distinct for different values of \\(r\\), the lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) are pairwise distinct and none of them coincides with any of the non\u2011sunny lines introduced earlier.\n\n   *Step\u202f5 \u2013 counting.*  \n   We have produced  \n\n   * \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (2)\u2013(4);  \n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines (9).\n\n   Altogether there are \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs to at least one of them (the points on the non\u2011sunny lines are covered in Steps\u202f1\u20132, the points in (8) are covered in Step\u202f4).\n\n   Since the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1), the condition \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is both necessary and sufficient.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]"
