ll** points of that diagonal.  \n   If a diagonal \\(D_s\\) is covered by a slope\u2011\\(-1\\) line, it contributes \\(s-1\\) points.\n\n   Consequently the total number of points that can be covered by the whole family is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k n + h n + \\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1),\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   where \\(S\\subset\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) is the set of diagonals that are represented among the \\(m\\) slope\u2011\\(-1\\) lines.\n\n   The set \\(T_n\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   points, so (2) must be at least (3).  Using \\(k+m+h=n\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   kn+hn+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(kn+hn=(k+h)n=(n-m)n\\), inequality (4) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-m)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\ge\\frac{n}{2}\\bigl(m+1\\bigr).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The left\u2011hand side is the sum of the sizes of the diagonals that are taken whole.  \n   To **minimise** the left\u2011hand side for a given \\(m\\) we should choose the *smallest* \\(m\\) diagonals, i.e. \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{m+1}\\). Their total size equals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s=2}^{m+1}(s-1)=\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore a necessary condition for the existence of a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{m(m+1)}{2}\\ge\\frac{n}{2}\\bigl(m+1\\bigr)\n   \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n   m\\ge n-1 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Recall that \\(m=n-k-h\\le n-k\\).  Hence (8) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge n-1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **no** configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Sufficiency: construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   *Base family (no sunny lines).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(\\{D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\). All have slope \\(-1\\); they cover \\(T_n\\) and give \\(k=0\\).\n\n   *Adding sunny lines.*  \n   Fix any integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   - Choose the \\(k\\) points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,k)\\) (all lie on the first column).  \n   - Through each of these points draw a distinct sunny line of slope \\(1\\) (for instance \\(y=x+(b-a)\\) passing through \\((a,b)\\)).  \n     These \\(k\\) lines are pairwise distinct and none is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are sunny.  \n   - The points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,k)\\) are now covered.  \n   - Remove from the original diagonal family the first \\(k\\) diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\); each of those diagonals consisted exactly of the points \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,k)\\) together with some points on higher rows.  \n   - Replace them by the following \\(n-k\\) lines:  \n     * the remaining diagonals \\(D_{k+2},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) (still slope \\(-1\\));  \n     * the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (slope \\(\\infty\\)).  \n\n   Count of lines: \\(k\\) sunny + \\((n-k-1)\\) diagonals + 1 vertical = \\(n\\).  \n   Coverage check:  \n   - Every point with \\(a\\ge 2\\) lies on a diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\ge k+2\\) and is therefore covered.  \n   - Points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\le k\\) are on the sunny lines.  \n   - Points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge k+1\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n\n   Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly \\(k\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   This construction works for every \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary inequality (9) shows that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-2\\); the explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) are exactly the points of the diagonals \\(D_s\\); the diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains \\(s-1\\) points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \n\nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) has slope different from \\(-1\\) (i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, horizontal or vertical), then \\(\\ell\\) meets each diagonal \\(D_s\\) in **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of the same diagonal satisfy \\(x_1+y_1=x_2+y_2\\); the line through them therefore has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}= \\frac{(s-x_2)-(s-x_1)}{x_2-x_1}= -1 .\n\\]\n\nHence a line whose slope is not \\(-1\\) cannot contain two different points of the same diagonal. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A necessary bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) covering \\(T_n\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nk=\\#\\{i\\mid \\ell_i\\text{ is sunny}\\},\\qquad\nm=\\#\\{i\\mid \\ell_i\\text{ has slope }-1\\},\\qquad\nh=\\#\\{i\\mid \\ell_i\\text{ is horizontal or vertical}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(k+m+h=n\\).\n\n*Contribution of the different types of lines.*\n\n* A sunny line can meet each diagonal in at most one point (Lemma\u202f1); therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\cdot n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* A horizontal or vertical line also meets each diagonal in at most one point, so the \\(h\\) such lines together cover at most \\(h\\cdot n\\) points.\n\n* A line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with a diagonal \\(D_s\\) and covers all its \\(s-1\\) points.\n\nLet \\(S\\subset\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) be the set of indices of the diagonals that appear among the \\(m\\) slope\u2011\\(-1\\) lines.  \nThe total number of points that can be covered is therefore at most  \n\n\\[\nk n + h n + \\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, (2) must be at least  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(k+m+h=n\\) we rewrite (2) as  \n\n\\[\n(k+h)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)=(n-m)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1).\n\\]\n\nHence (3) yields  \n\n\\[\n(n-m)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\n\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\ge\\frac{n}{2}(m+1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor a given \\(m\\) the left\u2011hand side of (4) is smallest when we choose the *smallest* \\(m\\) diagonals, i.e. \\(S=\\{2,3,\\dots ,m+1\\}\\). Their total size equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s=2}^{m+1}(s-1)=\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus a necessary condition for the existence of a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}\\ge\\frac{n}{2}(m+1)\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nm\\ge n-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\le n-k\\) (the \\(k\\) sunny lines are not among the \\(m\\) slope\u2011\\(-1\\) lines), (6) gives  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge n-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently **no** configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now show that each integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can actually be realised.\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\). All have slope \\(-1\\); they cover \\(T_n\\) and contain no sunny line.\n\n*Case \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) consider the point \\((1,i)\\).  \n   Through \\((1,i)\\) draw the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = x + (i-1) .\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is sunny.  \n   The lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n2. **Remaining lines.**  \n   - Keep the diagonals \\(D_{k+2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) (there are \\(n-k-1\\) of them).  \n   - Add the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (slope \\(\\infty\\), not sunny).\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-k-1)\\;(\\text{diagonals})\\;+\\;1\\;(\\text{vertical}) = n .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Verification of coverage.**  \n\n   - Any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\); since \\(a+b\\ge 2+(1)=3\\) and \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\) (because \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(k\\le n-2\\)), this diagonal is among the retained ones, so the point is covered.\n\n   - Points of the form \\((1,i)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) lie on the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n   - Points \\((1,i)\\) with \\(i\\ge k+1\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny.\n\nThus for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Final conclusion  \n\nThe necessary inequality derived from Lemma\u202f1 forces \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\) is attainable.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
