ost  \n\n   \\[\n   kn+hn+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)=(k+h)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)=(n-m)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Since every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, (1) must be at least  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-m)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\Longrightarrow\n   \\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\ge\\frac{n}{2}\\bigl(2m-n+1\\bigr).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   (The algebra: move \\((n-m)n\\) to the right\u2011hand side and simplify.)\n\n   For a given \\(m\\) the left\u2011hand side of (3) is **minimal** when we choose the smallest \\(m\\) diagonals, i.e. \\(S=\\{2,3,\\dots ,m+1\\}\\). Their total size equals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s=2}^{m+1}(s-1)=\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (3) and (4) yields the necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{m(m+1)}{2}\\ge\\frac{n}{2}\\bigl(2m-n+1\\bigr)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   m^{2}-(2n-1)m+n(n-1)\\le0.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The quadratic on the left has discriminant \\(\\Delta=1\\) and roots \\(m=n-1\\) and \\(m=n\\). Therefore (5) holds only for  \n\n   \\[\n   n-1\\le m\\le n .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(m\\le n-k\\) (the \\(k\\) sunny lines are not among the slope\u2011\\(-1\\) lines), (6) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge n-1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines**.\n\n4. **Sufficiency \u2013 construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   *Case \\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\). All have slope \\(-1\\); they cover \\(T_n\\) and contain no sunny line.\n\n   *Case \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \n\n   - For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) draw the sunny line  \n     \\[\n     \\ell_i:\\;y=x+(i-1),\n     \\]\n     which passes through the point \\((1,i)\\) and has slope \\(1\\) (hence is sunny). The \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct.\n\n   - Keep the diagonals \\(D_{k+2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) (there are \\(n-k-1\\) of them).\n\n   - Add the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (slope \\(\\infty\\), not sunny).\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-k-1)\\;(\\text{diagonals})\\;+\\;1\\;(\\text{vertical})=n .\n   \\]\n\n   **Coverage check.**  \n   * Points with first coordinate \\(\\ge2\\) lie on a diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\) with \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\); such a diagonal is retained, so the point is covered.  \n   * Points \\((1,i)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) lie on the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n   * Points \\((1,i)\\) with \\(i\\ge k+1\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (7) shows that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-2\\); the explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable. Therefore the set of all admissible non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
