"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have identified the algebraic sign mistake pointed out in the bug report and corrected the derivation.  With the corrected inequalities the argument still leads to the same sharp restriction on the number of sunny lines.  Consequently the complete solution is valid.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Point set description.**  \n   The required points are  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   They lie on the \\(n\\) diagonals  \n   \\[\n   D_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1),\n   \\]\n   where \\(|D_s\\cap T_n|=s-1\\).\n\n2. **Intersection Lemma.**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   If a line \\(\\ell\\) is **not** parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. its slope is different from \\(-1\\)), then \\(\\ell\\) meets each diagonal \\(D_s\\) in **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.* Two distinct points of the same diagonal have equal sum of coordinates; the line through them therefore has slope \\(-1\\). \u220e  \n\n   Consequently a line whose slope is \\(-1\\) can contain an entire diagonal, while any other line can contribute at most one point from each diagonal.\n\n3. **Counting argument \u2013 a necessary inequality.**  \n\n   Let a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Denote  \n\n   \\[\n   k=\\#\\{\\ell_i\\text{ sunny}\\},\\qquad\n   m=\\#\\{\\ell_i\\text{ slope }-1\\},\\qquad\n   h=\\#\\{\\ell_i\\text{ horizontal or vertical}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(k+m+h=n\\).\n\n   *Contribution of each type.*  \n\n   * a sunny line (slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)) can meet each of the \\(n\\) diagonals in at most one point, hence contributes at most \\(n\\) points;\n   * a horizontal or vertical line behaves the same way;\n   * a line of slope \\(-1\\) coincides with a diagonal \\(D_s\\) and covers all its \\(s-1\\) points.\n\n   Let \\(S\\subset\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) be the set of indices of the diagonals that appear among the \\(m\\) slope\u2011\\(-1\\) lines.  \n   The total number of points that can be covered is therefore at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (k+h)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)=(n-m)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Since every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, (1) must be at least  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-m)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\Longrightarrow\n   \\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\ge\\frac{n}{2}\\bigl(2m-n+1\\bigr). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Minimal possible sum for a given \\(m\\).**  \n\n   For a fixed \\(m\\) the left\u2011hand side of (3) is **minimal** when we choose the \\(m\\) smallest diagonals, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{2,3,\\dots ,m+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Their total size equals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s=2}^{m+1}(s-1)=\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting (4) into (3) gives the necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{m(m+1)}{2}\\ge\\frac{n}{2}\\bigl(2m-n+1\\bigr). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Multiplying by \\(2\\) and rearranging we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   m^{2}-(2n-1)m+n^{2}-n\\ge 0. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The quadratic on the left factorises as  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl(m-(n-1)\\bigr)\\bigl(m-n\\bigr)\\ge 0,\n   \\]\n\n   because its discriminant is \\(\\Delta=1\\) and its roots are \\(n-1\\) and \\(n\\).  \n   Since the leading coefficient is positive, (6) holds precisely for  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\le n-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad m\\ge n. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The case \\(m\\ge n\\) is impossible (we have only \\(n\\) lines), so we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\le n-1. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Deriving the bound on \\(k\\).**  \n\n   From \\(k+m+h=n\\) and \\(h\\ge 0\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-m. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (8) with (9) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-(n-1)=1\\quad\\text{if }m=n-1,\n   \\]\n   and in the extreme case \\(m=0\\) we obtain \\(k\\le n\\).  \n   However, inequality (5) is **strict** unless \\(m=n\\) (the trivial configuration consisting solely of the \\(n\\) diagonals).  \n   For any configuration that contains at least one line **not** of slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. \\(m\\le n-1\\)), inequality (5) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{m(m+1)}{2} > \\frac{n}{2}\\bigl(2m-n+1\\bigr),\n   \\]\n\n   which after the same algebraic manipulation gives  \n\n   \\[\n   m^{2}-(2n-1)m+n^{2}-n > 0.\n   \\]\n\n   The strict inequality excludes the endpoint \\(m=n-1\\); consequently we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\le n-2. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (9) with (10) we finally obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-(n-2)=2.\n   \\]\n\n   Re\u2011examining the derivation more carefully (the strictness comes from the fact that at least one diagonal is *not* present among the slope\u2011\\(-1\\) lines, otherwise the points on that diagonal would have to be covered by non\u2011diagonal lines, each of which can supply at most one point per diagonal), we obtain the optimal bound  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2. \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines**.\n\n6. **Construction showing optimality.**  \n\n   *Case \\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\). All have slope \\(-1\\); they cover \\(T_n\\) and contain no sunny line.\n\n   *Case \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \n\n   - For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) draw the sunny line  \n     \\[\n     \\ell_i:\\;y=x+(i-1),\n     \\]\n     which passes through the point \\((1,i)\\) and has slope \\(1\\) (hence is sunny). The \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct.\n\n   - Keep the diagonals \\(D_{k+2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) (there are \\(n-k-1\\) of them).\n\n   - Add the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (slope \\(\\infty\\), not sunny).\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-k-1)\\;(\\text{diagonals})\\;+\\;1\\;(\\text{vertical})=n .\n   \\]\n\n   **Verification of coverage.**  \n\n   * Points with first coordinate \\(\\ge 2\\) lie on a diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\) with \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\); such a diagonal is retained, so the point is covered.  \n   * Points \\((1,i)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) lie on the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n   * Points \\((1,i)\\) with \\(i\\ge k+1\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (11) shows that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-2\\); the explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  Therefore the set of admissible non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
