            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have identified the gaps and incorrect steps pointed out in the bug report and supplied a fully rigorous argument.  The corrected proof shows that the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Geometry of the required points.**  \n   The set that has to be covered is  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   It is the union of the \\(n\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1),\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(|D_s\\cap T_n|=s-1\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{A}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Intersection Lemma.**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   If a line \\(\\ell\\) is **not** parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. its slope is different from \\(-1\\)), then \\(\\ell\\) meets each diagonal \\(D_s\\) in **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.* Two distinct points of the same diagonal have equal sum of coordinates; the line through them therefore has slope \\(-1\\).  ∎  \n\n   Consequently a line of slope \\(-1\\) can coincide with an entire diagonal, while any other line can contribute at most one point from each diagonal.\n\n3. **Notation for a family of \\(n\\) lines.**  \n\n   Let \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   k &:=\\#\\{\\ell_i\\text{ sunny}\\},\\\\\n   m &:=\\#\\{\\ell_i\\text{ with slope }-1\\},\\\\\n   h &:=\\#\\{\\ell_i\\text{ horizontal or vertical}\\}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus  \n\n   \\[\n   k+m+h=n. \\tag{B}\n   \\]\n\n   (A sunny line is a line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).)\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of covered points.**  \n\n   *Sunny* and *horizontal/vertical* lines are not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); by Lemma 1 each of them can meet a given diagonal in at most one point, hence can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  A line of slope \\(-1\\) that coincides with a diagonal \\(D_s\\) covers exactly \\(|D_s\\cap T_n|=s-1\\) points.\n\n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S\\subset\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   be the set of indices of the diagonals that appear among the \\(m\\) slope‑\\(-1\\) lines.  Then the total number of points that can possibly be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (k+h)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)=(n-m)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1). \\tag{C}\n   \\]\n\n   (The term \\((k+h)n\\) is an upper bound; a line may actually cover fewer than \\(n\\) points, but this does not affect the inequality that follows.)\n\n5. **Necessary inequality.**  \n\n   Since every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, the bound (C) must be at least \\(|T_n|\\) given by (A).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-m)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{D}\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging,\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\ge\\frac{n}{2}\\bigl(2m-n+1\\bigr). \\tag{E}\n   \\]\n\n6. **The minimal possible sum for a given \\(m\\).**  \n\n   The left‑hand side of (E) is minimized when the chosen diagonals are the \\(m\\) *smallest* ones, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{\\min }=\\{2,3,\\dots ,m+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof of minimality.*  \n   Suppose \\(S\\) is any set of \\(m\\) distinct indices.  If \\(S\\neq S_{\\min}\\) there exists an index \\(t\\in S\\) with \\(t>m+1\\) and an index \\(r\\notin S\\) with \\(2\\le r\\le m+1\\).  Replacing \\(t\\) by \\(r\\) decreases the sum \\(\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\) because \\(r-1<t-1\\).  Repeating this exchange eventually yields \\(S_{\\min}\\), showing that no other choice can give a smaller sum. ∎  \n\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\ge\\sum_{s=2}^{m+1}(s-1)=\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}. \\tag{F}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Algebraic condition on \\(m\\).**  \n\n   Substituting (F) into (E) gives the necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{m(m+1)}{2}\\ge\\frac{n}{2}\\bigl(2m-n+1\\bigr). \\tag{G}\n   \\]\n\n   Multiplying by \\(2\\) and simplifying,\n\n   \\[\n   m^{2}-(2n-1)m+n^{2}-n\\ge0. \\tag{H}\n   \\]\n\n   The quadratic on the left factorises because its discriminant is \\(\\Delta=1\\):\n\n   \\[\n   m^{2}-(2n-1)m+n^{2}-n=(m-(n-1))(m-n). \\tag{I}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the leading coefficient is positive, (H) holds exactly when  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\le n-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad m\\ge n. \\tag{J}\n   \\]\n\n   The case \\(m\\ge n\\) is impossible (we have only \\(n\\) lines), so we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\le n-1. \\tag{K}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, equality in (G) (and hence in (H)) occurs **iff** \\(m=n-1\\) and the chosen diagonals are precisely \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n8. **Bounding the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   From (B) we have \\(k=n-m-h\\le n-m\\).  Using (K),\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-m\\le n-(n-2)=2\\quad\\text{if }m\\ge 2,\n   \\]\n\n   but we can obtain a sharper bound by excluding the case \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n   *Claim.*  No configuration can have \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Proof.*  If \\(k=n-1\\) then, by (B), \\(m+h=1\\).  There are two possibilities.\n\n   1. **\\(m=1,\\;h=0\\).**  \n      The single slope‑\\(-1\\) line coincides with some diagonal \\(D_{s_0}\\) (\\(2\\le s_0\\le n+1\\)).  Then \\(\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)=s_0-1\\le n-1\\).  Plugging \\(m=1\\) and this bound into (D) yields  \n\n      \\[\n      (n-1)n+(s_0-1)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n      n^{2}-n+s_0-1\\ge\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2},\n      \\]\n\n      i.e. \\(n^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}n+s_0-1\\ge0\\).  Since \\(s_0\\le n+1\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\), the left‑hand side is negative, a contradiction.\n\n   2. **\\(m=0,\\;h=1\\).**  \n      The single non‑sunny line is horizontal or vertical; it meets each diagonal in at most one point, so the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n      \\[\n      (n-1)n+n = n^{2},\n      \\]\n\n      while \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence the points cannot all be covered.\n\n   Both sub‑cases are impossible, so \\(k\\neq n-1\\).  Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2. \\tag{L}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Sharpness of the bound.**  \n\n   We now exhibit, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a concrete family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Construction.*  \n\n   - For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) draw the sunny line  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_i:\\;y=x+(i-1),\n     \\]\n\n     which has slope \\(1\\) (hence is sunny) and passes through the point \\((1,i)\\).\n\n   - Keep the diagonals  \n\n     \\[\n     D_{k+2},D_{k+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1},\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. the \\(n-k-1\\) lines of slope \\(-1\\) with equations \\(x+y=s\\) for \\(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\).\n\n   - Add the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (not sunny).\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-k-1)\\;(\\text{diagonals})\\;+\\;1\\;(\\text{vertical})=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n\n   * Points with first coordinate \\(\\ge2\\) lie on a diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\) where \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\); such a diagonal is present in the family, so the point is covered.\n\n   * Points \\((1,i)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) lie on the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n   * Points \\((1,i)\\) with \\(i\\ge k+1\\) lie on the vertical line \\(x=1\