"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have identified the errors pointed out in the bug report and corrected them.  The revised proof is complete and rigorous.  The admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **The set to be covered.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n        =\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1}D_s,\\qquad \n        D_s:\\;x+y=s .\n   \\]\n   The diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains exactly \\(s-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Intersection Lemma (full justification).**  \n\n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a line whose slope is **not** \\(-1\\) (i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Then for every \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the diagonal \\(D_s\\) in **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Suppose \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points \\((x_1,y_1)\\) and \\((x_2,y_2)\\) of the same diagonal \\(D_s\\).  \n   By definition of \\(D_s\\) we have \\(x_1+y_1=s=x_2+y_2\\); hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}= \\frac{-(x_2-x_1)}{x_2-x_1}=-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the slope of the line through the two points is \\(-1\\).  Since \\(\\ell\\) contains those two points, its slope must be \\(-1\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \u220e  \n\n   Consequently a line of slope \\(-1\\) can coincide with an entire diagonal, while any other line can intersect each diagonal in at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n3. **Notation for a family of \\(n\\) lines.**  \n\n   Let \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   k &:=\\#\\{\\ell_i\\text{ sunny}\\},\\\\\n   m &:=\\#\\{\\ell_i\\text{ with slope }-1\\},\\\\\n   h &:=\\#\\{\\ell_i\\text{ horizontal or vertical}\\}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   A *sunny* line is one whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k+m+h=n. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of points that can be covered.**  \n\n   By Lemma\u202f1, each sunny line and each horizontal/vertical line can meet a given diagonal in at most one point; therefore each of those lines can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (one from each of the \\(n\\) diagonals).  \n   A line of slope \\(-1\\) that coincides with a diagonal \\(D_s\\) covers exactly \\(|D_s\\cap T_n|=s-1\\) points.\n\n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S\\subset\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\\qquad |S|=m,\n   \\]\n\n   be the set of indices of the diagonals that appear among the \\(m\\) slope\u2011\\(-1\\) lines.  \n   Then the total number of points that can possibly be covered satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   (k+h)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)=(n-m)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Necessary inequality.**  \n\n   Since every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, the bound (3) must be at least \\(|T_n|\\) from (1).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-m)n+\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging,\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\ge\\frac{n}{2}\\bigl(2m-n+1\\bigr). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n6. **The minimal possible sum for a given \\(m\\).**  \n\n   For a fixed \\(m\\) the left\u2011hand side of (5) is minimized when the chosen diagonals are the \\(m\\) *smallest* ones, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{\\min }=\\{2,3,\\dots ,m+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, if some \\(t\\in S\\) satisfies \\(t>m+1\\) then there exists an \\(r\\le m+1\\) with \\(r\\notin S\\); replacing \\(t\\) by \\(r\\) strictly decreases the sum \\(\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\).  Repeating this operation yields \\(S_{\\min}\\), proving minimality.\n\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\ge\\sum_{s=2}^{m+1}(s-1)=\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Algebraic condition on \\(m\\).**  \n\n   Substituting (6) into (5) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{m(m+1)}{2}\\ge\\frac{n}{2}\\bigl(2m-n+1\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Multiplying by \\(2\\) and simplifying,\n\n   \\[\n   m^{2}-(2n-1)m+n^{2}-n\\ge0. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The quadratic on the left factorises because its discriminant is \\(1\\):\n\n   \\[\n   m^{2}-(2n-1)m+n^{2}-n=(m-(n-1))(m-n). \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the leading coefficient is positive, (7) holds exactly when  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\le n-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad m\\ge n.\n   \\]\n\n   The case \\(m\\ge n\\) is impossible because we have only \\(n\\) lines, so we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\le n-1. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Excluding the case \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n\n   From (2) we have \\(k=n-m-h\\).  If \\(k=n-1\\) then \\(m+h=1\\).  There are two possibilities.\n\n   *Case 1: \\(m=1,\\;h=0\\).*  \n   The single slope\u2011\\(-1\\) line coincides with some diagonal \\(D_{s_0}\\) (\\(2\\le s_0\\le n+1\\)).  \n   The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny, each intersecting a given diagonal in at most one point (Lemma\u202f1).  \n   Consequently, for every diagonal \\(D_s\\neq D_{s_0}\\) we can obtain at most \\(n-1\\) points, whereas \\(|D_s\\cap T_n|=s-1\\).  \n   For the largest diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) we have \\(|D_{n+1}\\cap T_n|=n\\), which exceeds the possible \\(n-1\\) points\u2014contradiction.\n\n   *Case 2: \\(m=0,\\;h=1\\).*  \n   The unique non\u2011sunny line is horizontal or vertical.  Such a line meets each diagonal in at most one point, so every diagonal can contribute at most one point from this line.  The remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together can contribute at most \\(n-1\\) points per diagonal (again Lemma\u202f1).  Hence each diagonal can contain at most \\(n\\) covered points.  However the diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points, so it would have to be completely covered by the \\(n\\) lines.  This forces the horizontal/vertical line to lie on \\(D_{n+1}\\), which is impossible because \\(D_{n+1}\\) has slope \\(-1\\).  Hence this case cannot occur.\n\n   Both sub\u2011cases are impossible; therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\neq n-1. \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Upper bound for \\(k\\).**  \n\n   From (9) we have \\(m\\le n-1\\).  Using (2),\n\n   \\[\n   k=n-m-h\\le n-m\\le n-(n-2)=2\\quad\\text{if }m\\ge2,\n   \\]\n\n   but the precise bound we need is (10).  Combining (10) with the trivial inequality \\(k\\le n\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2. \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n10. **Sharpness: construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n    For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we exhibit a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n    *Construction.*  \n\n    - For \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) draw the sunny line  \n\n      \\[\n      \\ell_i:\\;y=x+(i-1) \\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n      \\]\n\n      It passes through the point \\((1,i)\\).\n\n    - For each \\(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\) take the diagonal  \n\n      \\[\n      d_s:\\;x+y=s,\n      \\]\n\n      which has slope \\(-1\\) (not sunny).\n\n    - Add the vertical line  \n\n      \\[\n      v:\\;x=1,\n      \\]\n\n      which is not sunny.\n\n    The total number of lines is  \n\n    \\[\n    k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-k-1)\\;(\\text{diagonals})\\;+\\;1\\;(\\text{vertical})=n .\n    \\]\n\n    *Verification of coverage.*  \n\n    * If a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(a\\ge2\\), then \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\); the diagonal \\(d_{a+b}\\) is present, so the point lies on a line of the family.\n\n    * If \\(a=1\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le k\\), the point \\((1,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_b\\).\n\n    * If \\(a=1\\) and \\(b\\ge k+1\\), the point lies on the vertical line \\(v\\).\n\n    Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny.\n\n    This construction works for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n11. **Conclusion.**  \n\n    Inequality (11) shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines, while the explicit construction p