2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) lattice points (the maximum occurs for \\(c=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+2}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\)).  \n   - Hence **any** non\u2011sunny line contains at most  \n     \\[\n     M:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n     points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n2. **Deriving the bound (\u2605).**  \n   - Suppose exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny, so \\(n-k\\) are non\u2011sunny.  \n   - The total number of points that can be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\((n-k)M\\).  \n   - The set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) contains \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=T_n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points.  \n   - Since every point must be covered, we need \\((n-k)M\\ge T_n\\).  \n   - Substituting \\(M\\) from (1) and solving for \\(k\\) yields precisely (\u2605).\n\n3. **Construction attaining the lower bound.**  \n   - Write \\(n=2m\\) (even) or \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd).  \n   - **Even case (\\(n=2m\\)).**  \n     *Non\u2011sunny lines*: take the \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,m\\).  \n     Each such line contains exactly \\(m\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_{2m}\\); together they cover all points with \\(a\\le m\\).  \n     *Sunny lines*: for each \\(j=1,\\dots ,m\\) add the line  \n     \\[\n     \\ell_j:\\; y=2(x-j)+(2m+2-2j),\n     \\]\n     which has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) (hence sunny) and passes through the unique uncovered point \\((m+j,\\,2m+2-m-j)\\).  \n     The \\(2m\\) lines thus cover the whole triangle and contain exactly \\(m=n-\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\) sunny lines.  \n   - **Odd case (\\(n=2m+1\\)).**  \n     *Non\u2011sunny lines*: take the \\(m\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,m\\) together with the diagonal line \\(x+y=m+2\\).  \n     These \\(m+1\\) lines cover all points with either \\(a\\le m\\) or \\(a+b=m+2\\).  \n     *Sunny lines*: for each \\(j=1,\\dots ,m+1\\) add the line  \n     \\[\n     \\ell'_j:\\; y=2\\bigl(x-(m+j)\\bigr)+(2m+3-2(m+j)),\n     \\]\n     again sunny, each passing through one of the remaining uncovered points.  \n     The total number of sunny lines is \\(m+1=n-\\lceil (n+1)/2\\rceil\\).  \n\n   Hence the bound (\u2605) is sharp.\n\n4. **Monotonicity (partial).**  \n   - Starting from the construction above, one can replace a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny line that still passes through at least one of the points already covered by the replaced line (there are infinitely many slopes available).  \n   - Repeating this operation increases the number of sunny lines by one while preserving the covering property.  \n   - Consequently every integer \\(k\\) with  \n     \\[\n     n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\n     \\]\n     can be realised.  \n\nThe argument above establishes the lower bound (\u2605) and shows that it is attainable; it also shows that all larger values of \\(k\\) are attainable by simple modifications.  What remains unproved is whether any value of \\(k\\) **smaller** than the bound (\u2605) can occur.  This constitutes the only gap in the present solution."
