nct non\u2011sunny lines and covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Turning non\u2011sunny lines into sunny ones.**  \n   Starting from \\(\\mathcal N_n\\) we perform the following replacement operation repeatedly:  \n\n   *Pick a line \\(\\ell\\) that is non\u2011sunny. Choose two points \\(P,Q\\in S_n\\) such that the line \\(\\ell'\\) through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (i.e. \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny). Replace \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell'\\).*\n\n   Because among the finitely many slopes determined by pairs of points of \\(S_n\\) only three are forbidden, a sunny line through any prescribed point \\(P\\) can be found. Moreover, when \\(\\ell\\) is removed, every point that was covered **only** by \\(\\ell\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\); such a point cannot be the second point \\(Q\\) (otherwise \\(\\ell'\\) would coincide with \\(\\ell\\) and be non\u2011sunny). Hence after the replacement each point of \\(S_n\\) is still covered by at least one line. The lines remain distinct because a sunny line cannot coincide with any of the already existing lines (all of which are either vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n   Starting with \\(0\\) sunny lines we can apply the operation exactly \\(k\\) times, obtaining a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(S_n\\) and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. This works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\).**  \n   As shown in step\u202f1 any feasible configuration must consist of exactly \\(n\\) lines. Since a line is either sunny or non\u2011sunny, the number of sunny lines can only be an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\). The construction of step\u202f3 shows that every integer in this interval is attainable. Hence no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\ b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Size of \\(S_n\\) and a lower bound for the number of lines\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=c\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\). Therefore  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=\\binom{n+1}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line can intersect the region \\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) in at most one point for each integer value of \\(a+b\\); consequently a line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\). Hence any family of lines covering \\(S_n\\) must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|S_n|}{n}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{\\binom{n+1}{2}}{n}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =n \\qquad (n\\ge3). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus a feasible configuration necessarily consists of **exactly** \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Existence of a covering by \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A=\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal B=\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-2\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal A\\cup\\mathcal B\\cup\\mathcal D\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore they are **non\u2011sunny**.  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_0=\\mathcal A\\cup\\mathcal B\\cup\\mathcal D\n\\]\n\ncontains \\((n-2)+(n-2)+1=2n-3\\) lines and covers \\(S_n\\): a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on a vertical line \\(x=a\\) if \\(a\\le n-2\\), on a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) if \\(b\\le n-2\\), and otherwise \\(a\\ge n-1,\\ b\\ge n-1\\) forces \\(a+b=n+1\\), so the point lies on the diagonal.\n\nWe now delete lines from \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) while preserving the covering, until only \\(n\\) lines remain.\n\n*Step 1.* Keep the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:x=1,\\qquad \\ell_2:y=1,\\qquad \\ell_3:x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nand discard all other lines of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\).  \nThe points of \\(S_n\\) not covered by \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\}\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge2,\\ b\\ge2\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the set \\(U\\) consists of the points with \\(a+b\\le n\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|U|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n}(c-1)=\\binom{n}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Step 2.* For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-2\\) we add **one** of the lines \\(x=t\\) or \\(y=t\\) (whichever has not yet been used) that passes through at least one point of \\(U\\).  \nBecause each point of \\(U\\) has either its \\(x\\)-coordinate or its \\(y\\)-coordinate in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\), we can select a distinct line for each of the first \\(n-3\\) points of \\(U\\). After adding these \\(n-3\\) lines every point of \\(U\\) is covered (the remaining points of \\(U\\) are already covered by \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) or \\(\\ell_3\\)).\n\nThus we obtain a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_n=\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\}\\cup\\{\\text{the }n-3\\text{ added lines}\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of exactly  \n\n\\[\n3+(n-3)=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. From a non\u2011sunny covering to any prescribed number of sunny lines\n\nRecall that a line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nWe prove the following claim.\n\n> **Claim.**  \n> Starting from the covering \\(\\mathcal N_n\\) we can, for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), produce a covering \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(S_n\\) consisting of \\(n\\) distinct lines of which exactly \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(k\\).*\n\n*Base \\(k=0\\).* \\(\\mathcal N_n\\) itself contains no sunny lines, so the claim holds.\n\n*Inductive step.* Assume we have a covering \\(\\mathcal L\\) with exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le t<k\\)).  \nIf every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\), we may simply delete any non\u2011sunny line and replace it by any sunny line (for instance the line through two points of \\(S_n\\) whose slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)). The covering property is preserved because each point still has at least one incident line.\n\nOtherwise there exists a point \\(P\\in S_n\\) that is covered **exactly once**; let \\(\\ell\\) be the unique line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) containing \\(P\\). By construction \\(\\ell\\) is non\u2011sunny (otherwise \\(P\\) would already be covered by a sunny line).  \n\nChoose a second point \\(Q\\in S_n\\) with \\(Q\\neq P\\) such that the line \\(\\ell'\\) through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) is sunny. Among the \\(\\binom{|S_n|}{2}\\) lines determined by pairs of points of \\(S_n\\) only three have forbidden slopes, so such a \\(Q\\) always exists.\n\nReplace \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell'\\). The new family still has \\(n\\) lines.  \n\n- The point \\(P\\) remains covered (now by the sunny line \\(\\ell'\\)).  \n- Any other point that was covered only by \\(\\ell\\) must lie on \\(\\ell\\) but not on \\(\\ell'\\). Because \\(\\ell'\\) shares the point \\(P\\) with \\(\\ell\\) and has a different slope, the two lines intersect only at \\(P\\); consequently no other point loses its unique covering.  \n\nAll other points keep at least one incident line, so the covering condition is maintained. Moreover \\(\\ell'\\) cannot coincide with any pre\u2011existing line, because all pre\u2011existing lines are either vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\), whereas \\(\\ell'\\) has a different slope. Hence the lines remain distinct.\n\nThus we have produced a covering with \\(t+1\\) sunny lines. By induction we obtain a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Completion of the proof\n\nFrom (2) any feasible configuration must consist of exactly \\(n\\) lines, so the number of sunny lines can only be an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  \nThe construction of Sections\u202f2 and\u202f3 shows that **every** integer in this interval is attainable. Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the answer to the problem is that all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are possible, and no other values of \\(k\\) can occur.**"
