y three\n   have forbidden slopes).  \n   Replace \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell'\\).*\n\n   The replacement preserves the covering:\n   * \\(P\\) remains covered (now by the sunny line \\(\\ell'\\));\n   * any point that was covered **only** by \\(\\ell\\) must be \\(P\\), because two\n     distinct non\u2011parallel lines intersect in at most one point; hence no other\n     point loses its unique covering;\n   * \\(\\ell'\\) cannot coincide with any existing line, because all existing lines\n     are vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\), whereas \\(\\ell'\\) has a\n     different slope.  \n   Consequently the family still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   Starting with \\(0\\) sunny lines we may apply the operation exactly \\(k\\) times,\n   obtaining a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers\n   \\(S_n\\) and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  This works for every\n   integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Any configuration required by the problem uses exactly \\(n\\) lines (the\n   statement of the problem fixes the number of lines).  Since each line is\n   either sunny or non\u2011sunny, the number of sunny lines is an integer between\n   \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  The construction of step\u202f3 shows that every integer in this\n   interval is attainable.  Hence no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\ b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Size of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=c\\) contains\nexactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.