at\nis non\u2011sunny.  Because \\(\\ell\\) meets the finite set \\(S_n\\) in at least two\npoints, we can select a point  \n\n\\[\nP\\in\\ell\\cap S_n\n\\]\n\nthat also lies on another line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (such a point exists unless\n\\(\\ell\\) is the only line through every point of \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\); in that\ndegenerate situation we simply delete \\(\\ell\\) and replace it by any sunny\nline through any point of \\(S_n\\), which clearly preserves the covering).\n\nNow pick a second point \\(Q\\in S_n\\), \\(Q\\neq P\\), such that the line\n\\(\\ell'\\) through \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) is sunny.  Among the \\(\\binom{|S_n|}{2}\\)\nlines determined by pairs of points of \\(S_n\\) only three have forbidden\nslopes (\\(0,\\infty,-1\\)); therefore such a \\(Q\\) always exists.\n\nReplace \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell'\\) and denote the new family by \\(\\mathcal L'\\).\n\n*Why \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still covers \\(S_n\\).*  \n\n* The point \\(P\\) remains covered (now by the sunny line \\(\\ell'\\)).\n* Any point that was covered **only** by \\(\\ell\\) must be \\(P\\), because two\n  distinct non\u2011parallel lines intersect in at most one point; hence no other\n  point loses its unique covering.\n* All other points keep at least one incident line from the original family.\n\n*Why the lines stay distinct.*  Every line already present in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is\nvertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); the new line \\(\\ell'\\) has a slope\ndifferent from these three values, so \\(\\ell'\\) cannot coincide with any\nexisting line.\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L'\\) is a covering of \\(S_n\\) consisting of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines and contains \\(t+1\\) sunny lines.  By the induction hypothesis we can\nrepeat the replacement exactly \\(k-t\\) times and obtain a covering with\nprecisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Determination of all possible \\(k\\)\n\nThe problem statement fixes the number of lines to be exactly \\(n\\).  Hence the\nnumber of sunny lines can only be an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  The\nconstruction of the claim shows that **every** integer in this interval is\nrealizable.  Consequently no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}.\n\\]\n\nThus the problem is completely solved."
