g with exactly\n   two sunny lines.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \n   As argued in step\u202f3, each vertical line \\(x=i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\)) contains at\n   least one point that is covered **only** by that line.  To keep those points\n   covered, the line \\(x=i\\) itself must remain in the family, and its slope\n   \\(\\infty\\) is non\u2011sunny.  Hence at most the two non\u2011vertical lines of the\n   canonical covering can be turned sunny.  Therefore no configuration with\n   three or more sunny lines exists.\n\nCombining steps\u202f2\u20135 we obtain the complete classification of admissible values\nof \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\ b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Size of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=c\\) contributes\n\\(c-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A covering with no sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=1\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D\\) are parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore they are **non\u2011sunny**.\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_n=\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal D .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal N_n\\) contains \\((n-2)+1+1=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(x=1\\in\\mathcal V\\).\n* If \\(2\\le a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal V\\).\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le2\\).  \n  \u2013 If \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\