\(n-2\\) points of \\(T\\) (one from each of the \\(n-2\\) columns).\n\n   *Proof.*  Write \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\neq\\infty\\).  \n   For a fixed integer \\(i\\) the system \\(\\{x=i,\\;y=mx+b\\}\\) has the unique solution \\((i,mi+b)\\); therefore at most one point of the vertical line \\(x=i\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\).  Since the columns that contain points of \\(T\\) are exactly \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\), the claim follows. ∎\n\n4. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   The points of \\(T\\) must be covered either by a vertical line \\(x=i\\) (which is non‑sunny) or by a sunny line.  \n\n   *Each sunny line can take at most one column* (by the Lemma).  \n   Therefore the \\(k\\) sunny lines can “save’’ at most \\(k\\) columns from being represented by a vertical line.  \n   There are \\(n-2\\) columns altogether, so at least \\((n-2)-k\\) vertical lines are necessary.  \n\n   In addition we always need at least one line to cover the horizontal set \\(H\\) and at least one line to cover the diagonal set \\(D\\).  \n   Those two lines may be sunny or non‑sunny; they do not affect the count above.  \n\n   Consequently the total number of lines satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\;\\ge\\; \\bigl[(n-2)-k\\bigr]\\;+\\;2\\;+\\;k \\;=\\; n .\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality is an equality, so the only way it can hold is when  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2)-k\\ge 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le 2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **no configuration can contain three or more sunny lines**.\n\n5. **Realising the three admissible values of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\) (no sunny line).*  \n   Use the following \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal N_n=\n   \\underbrace{\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\}}_{\\text{vertical}} \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{y=1\\}}_{\\text{horizontal}} \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{x+y=n+1\\}}_{\\text{diagonal}} .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(H\\) lies on \\(y=1\\); every point of \\(D\\) lies on \\(x+y=n+1\\); and each point of \\(T\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=i\\) with its first coordinate \\(i\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal N_n\\) covers \\(S_n\\) and contains no sunny line.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line).*  \n   Keep all vertical lines \\(x=i\\;(1\\le i\\le n-2)\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n   Replace the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\; y=-\\frac12\\,x+\\frac32 .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_1\\) passes through the points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), both belonging to \\(S_n\\).  \n   The point \\((1,2)\\) is already covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\); the point \\((3,1)\\) is now covered by \\(\\ell_1\\).  \n   All other points of \\(H\\) (i.e. \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\neq 3\\)) lie on some vertical line \\(x=a\\); all points of \\(D\\) and of \\(T\\) are covered as before.  \n   Thus the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{1}= \\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{x+y=n+1\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\ell_1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\) (exactly two sunny lines).*  \n   Start from the configuration for \\(k=1\\).  \n   Replace the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_2:\\; y=-\\frac12\\,x+\\frac{n+3}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_2\\) contains the points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((3,n-2)\\) of \\(S_n\\).  \n   The point \\((1,n)\\) was previously covered only by the diagonal line, now it is covered by \\(\\ell_2\\); the point \\((3,n-2)\\) remains covered by the vertical line \\(x=3\\).  \n   All points of \\(H\\) are still covered by the vertical lines (except \\((3,1)\\) which is covered by \\(\\ell_1\\)), all points of \\(T\\) are covered by the vertical lines, and the remaining points of the former diagonal are either on \\(