e two single‑point columns \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) by sunny lines.  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_{a}&:\\; y=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-2\\,}x+\\frac{n+1}{\\,n-2\\,}\n            &&\\text{through }(n-1,1)\\text{ and }(1,2),\\\\[2mm]\n   \\ell_{b}&:\\; y=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}x+\\frac{n}{\\,n-1\\,}\n            &&\\text{through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(1,n).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge4\\) the slopes \\(-\\frac1{n-2}\\) and \\(-\\frac1{n-1}\\) are different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); thus both \\(\\ell_a,\\ell_b\\) are sunny.  \n   The points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) already belong to the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\); the points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) are covered by \\(\\ell_a,\\ell_b\\) respectively.  \n   All remaining points of \\(H\\) lie on the vertical lines \\(x=i\\) (for \\(i\\neq n-1,n\\)), and all interior points \\(T\\) are covered by the vertical lines \\(x=i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\).  \n\n   Hence the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{2}= \\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_a,\\ell_b\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, two of which are sunny, and covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Small values of \\(n\\).*  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the constructions above specialise to:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   k=0 &: \\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=4\\},\\\\\n   k=1 &: \\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=4\\}\\cup\\{y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32\\},\\\\\n   k=2 &: \\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32\\}\\cup\\{y=-x+4\\},\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   which satisfy the required properties.  The case \\(n=4\\) is handled similarly; the formulas (3)–(4) remain valid.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument proves that no configuration can contain more than two sunny lines, and the explicit families above show that \\(k=0,1,2\\) are indeed attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  Therefore the classification is complete.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible numbers of sunny lines are }k