            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have repaired the gaps pointed out in the bug report.  \nThe complete, rigorous classification is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,2.}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **The set to be covered.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Decomposition of \\(S_n\\).**  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   H&=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\quad\\text{(bottom row)},\\\\\n   D&=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\quad\\text{(diagonal)},\\\\\n   T&=S_n\\setminus(H\\cup D)\n      =\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-2,\\;2\\le b\\le n-a\\}\\quad\\text{(interior)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   The three sets are pairwise disjoint and  \n   \\[\n   |T|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n-2}(n-a-1)=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Lemma (how many points of a column a sunny line can cover).**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a *sunny* line, i.e. its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).  \n   Then for each integer \\(i\\) the vertical line \\(x=i\\) meets \\(\\ell\\) in at most one point; consequently \\(\\ell\\) contains **at most one** point of the column \\(\\{(i,b)\\mid b\\in\\mathbb Z\\}\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  Write \\(\\ell\\)