ll_2\\)) are non\u2011sunny, so the number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nk=n-2 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Obtaining all intermediate values of \\(k\\)\n\nStarting from the configuration of Section\u202f4 (which has \\(k=n-2\\)), we may replace any sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) (\\(i\\ge3\\)) by a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) where \\(c\\) equals the \\(y\\)-intercept of \\(\\ell_i\\) (that is, \\(c=i\\)).  \nThe horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains **all** points of \\(T_n\\) that were previously on \\(\\ell_i\\) because those points all have second coordinate \\(c\\).  \nHence the covering property is preserved while the number of sunny lines decreases by one.  \n\nRepeating this replacement \\(r\\) times yields a configuration with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=(n-2)-r\n\\]\n\nsunny lines, for any integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le n-2\\).  \nWhen \\(r=n-2\\) we obtain the configuration of Section\u202f2 with \\(k=0\\).  \n\nTherefore every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (5) is realizable.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe necessary condition \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is also sufficient.  \nHence for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
