]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   - Any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) satisfies either \\(b-2a = t-2\\) for some \\(t\\) in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) or \\(2b+a = 2n+2-3s\\) for some \\(s\\) in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\). A short elementary check shows that one of the two equalities must hold, because the set of admissible pairs \\((a,b)\\) is exactly the integer lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). Hence each such point lies on at least one of the constructed lines.  \n   - The points with a coordinate equal to \\(1\\) lie on \\(L_{h}\\) or \\(L_{v}\\) by construction.  \n\n   Consequently every required point is covered, the lines are distinct, and exactly \\(k\\) of them (the \\(L_{t}\\) and \\(M_{s}\\) together) are sunny.\n\nSince the construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) and we have proved that larger \\(k\\) is impossible, the characterization is complete.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary lower bound on the number of non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nA line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\nhorizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) lines at most one is non\u2011sunny.  \nIf that line is horizontal, then no vertical line is present, so the points\n\\((a,1)\\;(a\\ge 2)\\) cannot be covered, because any line that contains two distinct\npoints \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a',1)\\) with \\(a\\neq a'\\) must be horizontal.  \nThe same argument excludes the case where the unique non\u2011sunny line is vertical.\nIf the unique non\u2011sunny line has slope \\(-1\\), then it contains no point of\n\\(T_{n}\\) (all points of \\(T_{n}\\) have positive coordinates, while a line of\nslope \\(-1\\) through a point of \\(T_{n}\\) would intersect the negative quadrant\nbefore reaching another point of \\(T_{n}\\)). Hence at least two non\u2011sunny lines\nare required.\n\nConsequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that\ncover all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n#### 2.1  The two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{h}:\\; y=1 ,\\qquad L_{v}:\\; x=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non\u2011sunny (horizontal and vertical, respectively).  \nThey cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\) that lie on the first row or the first column,\ni.e. the points \\((1,b)\\) and \\((a,1)\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Sunny lines of slope \\(2\\)\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\nt\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-k\\}\n\\]\n\ndefine  \n\n\\[\nL_{t}:\\; y = 2x + (t-2). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_{t}\\) is sunny.\nThe lines \\(L_{t}\\) are distinct because they have distinct intercepts.\n\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\n\\]\n\ndefine  \n\n\\[\nM_{s}:\\; y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\bigl(n+1-\\tfrac32 s\\bigr). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAgain the slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) is not forbidden, so each \\(M_{s}\\) is sunny, and the\ndifferent values of \\(s\\) give distinct lines.\n\n#### 2.4  Counting the lines  \n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n2\\;(\\text{non\u2011sunny})\\;+\\;(n-k-1)\\;(\\text{lines }L_{t})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{lines }M_{s})\n= n .\n\\]\n\nThus we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(n-k-1\\) lines \\(L_{t}\\) together\nwith the \\(k\\) lines \\(M_{s}\\) give \\(n-2\\) sunny lines; but we have counted\n\\(L_{2}\\) through \\(L_{n-k}\\), i.e. \\(n-k-1\\) of them, and the \\(k\\) lines\n\\(M_{s}\\); their sum is \\(n-2\\).  Since the two non\u2011sunny lines are fixed,\nthe number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).)\n\n#### 2.5  Verification that every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on a constructed line  \n\n*Points on the first row or column.*  \nIf \\(a=1\\) then \\((1,b)\\in L_{v}\\); if \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,1)\\in L_{h}\\).\n\n*Points with \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\).*  \nFor such a point set  \n\n\\[\nd = b-2a .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n-(n-2)\\le d \\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(d\\ge 0\\) then \\(d = t-2\\) for some \\(t\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) (choose any\n\\(t\\) with \\(t-2=d\\); this is possible because the interval \\([2,n-k]\\) contains\nall non\u2011negative integers up to \\(n-2\\) when \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies equation (2) and lies on \\(L_{t}\\).\n\nIf \\(d<0\\) write \\(d = -\\frac12 a + (n+1-\\frac32 s)\\) with  \n\n\\[\ns = \\frac{2}{3}\\bigl(n+1-d-\\tfrac12 a\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nA direct computation shows that for every admissible pair \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(d<0\\) the integer \\(s\\) defined above belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); consequently\n\\((a,b)\\) satisfies (3) and lies on \\(M_{s}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n* At least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny, whence \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) an explicit configuration of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all\nrequired points exists.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
