            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the two conditions of the problem and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(1,1),\\qquad P_{2}=(n,0),\\qquad P_{3}=(0,n),\n\\]\n\nall of which belong to \\(T_{n}\\) because \\(1+1\\le n+1\\) and \\(n+0\\le n+1\\).\n\nIf a line is sunny, its slope is not \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently it cannot be simultaneously horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\). Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(P_{1},P_{2},P_{3}\\).\n\nTherefore at least two of the three points must be covered by non‑sunny lines.  \nSince the lines are distinct, we obtain at least two distinct non‑sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k\\ (\\ge2).\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) choose a line \\(S_{i}\\) whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and which passes through the point \\((i,i)\\).  \nThe slopes may be chosen arbitrarily, provided the resulting lines are distinct (there are infinitely many admissible slopes, so this is always possible).\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S:=\\{S_{1},S_{2},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny and covers the point \\((i,i)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The non‑sunny lines\n\nConsider the following three families of non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal V &:=\\{\\,x=0,\\,x=1,\\dots ,x=m-2\\,\\}\\quad\\text{(vertical, slope }\\infty),\\\\\n\\mathcal H &:=\\{\\,y=0\\,\\}\\quad\\text{(horizontal, slope }0),\\\\\n\\mathcal D &:=\\{\\,x+y=2,\\,x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=m\\,\\}\\quad\\text{(diagonal, slope }-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe three families are pairwise disjoint, and  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal V|+|\\mathcal H|+|\\mathcal D|=(m-1)+1+(m-1)=2m-1.\n\\]\n\nFrom this pool we select exactly \\(m\\) lines, namely all the vertical lines of \\(\\mathcal V\\) together with the horizontal line \\(y=0\\). Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N:=\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|\\mathcal N|= (m-1)+1 = m = n-k\\), and every line in \\(\\mathcal N\\) is non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The whole family\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\mathcal S\\cup\\mathcal N .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal L|=k+(n-k)=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of its members are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Verification that all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1=k+m+1\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le m-2\\).*  \nThe point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal V\\subset\\mathcal N\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a=m-1\\).*  \nThen \\(b\\le n+1-(m-1)=k+2\\).\n\n  *Subcase 2.1: \\(b=0\\).*  \n  The point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=0\\in\\mathcal H\\subset\\mathcal N\\).\n\n  *Subcase 2.2: \\(1\\le b\\le k\\).*  \n  The point \\((a,b)=(m-1,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le k+m+1\\).  \n  If \\(a+b\\le m\\) then \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,m\\}\\) and the point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\in\\mathcal D\\).  \n  If \\(a+b>m\\) then \\(b\\le k\\) and the sunny line \\(S_{b}\\) (which passes through \\((b,b)\\)) also passes through \\((a,b)\\): indeed \\(S_{b}\\) has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and was chosen arbitrarily, so we may select it to be the unique line through the two distinct points \\((b,b)\\) and \\((a,b)\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\in S_{b}\\subset\\mathcal S\\).\n\n  *Subcase 2.3: \\(b=k+1\\) or \\(b=k+2\\).*  \n  Because \\(a=m-1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+m+1\\), we have \\(b\\le k+2\\).  \n  If \\(b=k+1\\) then \\(a+b=m+k\\le m+k\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=m+k\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal D\\) (since \\(m+k\\le m+(m-2)=2m-2\\) and \\(2\\le m+k\\le m\\) when \\(k\\le m-2\\); the inequality holds because \\(k\\le n-2=m-2\\)).  \n  If \\(b=k+2\\) then \\(a+b=m+k+1\\le m+(m-2)+1=2m-1\\); the line \\(x+y=m+k+1\\) is also among the diagonal lines \\(\\mathcal D\\).  \n  Thus the point is covered by a diagonal line.\n\n*Case 3: \\(a\\ge m\\).*  \nThen \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le k+1\\).  \nIf \\(b=0\\) we are in Case 2.1.  \nIf \\(1\\le b\\le k\\) we are in Subcase 2.2, which shows the point lies either on a diagonal line (when \\(a+b\\le m\\)) or on the sunny line \\(S_{b}\\) (when \\(a+b>m\\)).  \nIf \\(b=k+1\\) then \\(a+b\\le k+m+1\\) gives \\(a\\le m\\); the only possibilities are \\(a=m\\) or \\(a=m-1\\), 