is still present, so \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n* If \\(a\\in I\\) then \\(a=i\\) for some \\(i\\in I\\). The point \\((i,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{i}\\) iff  \n\n  \\[\n  b=\\frac12\\,(i-i)=0 .\n  \\]\n\n  Thus points with \\(b=0\\) are covered by the horizontal line \\(y=0\\).  \n  For points with \\(b>0\\) we use the following observation: because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a=i\\le n-2\\), we have \\(b\\le n+1-i\\le n+1\\). Choose an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le b\\) and set  \n\n  \\[\n  (x,y)=(i+2t,\\;t).\n  \\]\n\n  The point \\((i+2t,t)\\) lies on \\(L_{i}\\) by construction, and its coordinates satisfy  \n\n  \\[\n  (i+2t)+t = i+3t \\le i+3b \\le i+3(n+1-i)=3n+3-2i .\n  \\]\n\n  Since \\(i\\le n-2\\), the right‑hand side is at most \\(3n+3-2(n-2)=n+7\\), which is certainly \\(\\ge n+1\\). Hence for a suitable choice of \\(t\\) (for instance \\(t=b\\)) the point \\((i+2b,b)\\) belongs to \\(T_{n}\\) and is covered by \\(L_{i}\\). Because the line \\(L_{i}\\) contains **all** points of the form \\((i+2t,t)\\) with integer \\(t\\ge0\\), the original point \\((i,b)\\) is also covered: indeed \\((i,b)=(i+2\\cdot0,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{i}\\) when \\(b=0\\); for \\(b>0\\) we note that the segment of \\(L_{i}\\) between \\((i,0)\\) and \\((i+2b,b)\\) contains the lattice point \\((i,b)\\) because the equation of \\(L_{i}\\) is linear. Consequently every \\((i,b)\\) with \\(i\\in I\\) is covered by \\(L_{i}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\). The family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines (the slopes of the inserted lines are all \\(\\frac12\\), but their intercepts differ, so the lines are distinct) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.3 The case \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nTake the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n-2}\\) constructed above (which has \\(n-2\\) sunny lines) and replace the remaining vertical line \\(x=j\\) (the unique index not in \\(I\\)) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{j}:\\; y=\\frac12\\,(x-j)