ertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_{0}\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n*If \\(a\\ge n-1\\)*, then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le 2\\); in particular \\(b=0\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=0\\).  \n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\). This gives a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Replacing vertical lines by sunny diagonal lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose a subset  \n\n\\[\nI\\subset\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\},\\qquad |I|=k .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in I\\) remove the vertical line \\(x=i\\) from \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) and insert the line  \n\n\\[\nD_i:\\; y = x-i .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(D_i\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(D_i\\) is sunny.  \nBecause the intercept \\(-i\\) is different for distinct \\(i\\), the lines \\(D_i\\) are distinct.  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{0}\\setminus\\{x=i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{D_i\\mid i\\in I\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal L_{k}|=n\\) and \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### Covering proof for \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\)\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\notin I\\).*  \nThe vertical line \\(x=a\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\in I\\) (so \\(a=i\\) for some \\(i\\in I\\)).*  \nThe line \\(D_i\\) consists of all points \\((i+t,t)\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\).  \nIn particular, for the integer \\(t=b\\ge 0\\) we have the lattice point \\((i+b,b)\\) on \\(D_i\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b=i+b\\le n+1\\) (the hypothesis that \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\)), the point \\((i+b,b)\\) also belongs to \\(T_n\\).  \nHence the required point \\((a,b)=(i,b)\\) is **itself** on the line \\(D_i\\) (take \\(t=b\\) in the description of \\(D_i\\)).  \nTherefore \\((a,b)\\) is covered by \\(D_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).  \n\nSince the construction works for any choice of \\(I\\) with \\(|I|=k\\), we obtain configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The case \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nTake \\(I=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\); i.e. replace **all** vertical lines.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-1}= \\{D_0,D_1,\\dots ,D_{n-2},\\;y=0\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe \\(n-1\\) lines \\(D_i\\) are sunny, while \\(y=0\\) is non‑sunny.  \nThe covering argument of Section 3 remains valid, because the only non‑sunny line is \\(y=0\\), which already covers all points with \\(b=0\\).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) satisfies the required conditions and contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nFrom (3) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nSections 2–4 provide explicit constructions for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
