\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-2\\)*, the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n*If \\(a\\ge n-1\\)*, then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le 2\\); in particular \\(b=0\\), so the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=0\\).  \n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) covers every point of \\(T_n\\). This yields a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Introducing sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nI=\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\subset\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in I\\) delete the vertical line \\(x=i\\) from \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) and insert the line  \n\n\\[\nD_i:\\; y = x-i .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(D_i\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(D_i\\) is sunny.  \nBecause the intercept \\(-i\\) is distinct for different \\(i\\), the lines \\(D_i\\) are distinct.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_{0}\\setminus\\{x=i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{D_i\\mid i\\in I\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|\\mathcal L_{k}|=n\\) and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(D_i\\) are sunny.\n\n#### Covering proof\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\notin I\\).*  \nThe vertical line \\(x=a\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\in I\\) (so \\(a=i\\) for some \\(i\\in I\\)).*  \nThe line \\(D_i\\) consists of all points \\((i+t,t)\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb R\\).  \nChoosing \\(t=b\\) gives the point \\((i+b,b)=(a+b,b)\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a=i\\le n-2\\), we have \\(b\\ge0\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) itself is of the required form; therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(D_i\\).\n\nHence every lattice point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).  \nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) therefore satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The extreme case \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nTake \\(I=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\); i.e. replace **all** vertical lines.  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-1}= \\{D_0,D_1,\\dots ,D_{n-2},\\;y=0\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe \\(n-1\\) lines \\(D_i\\) are sunny, while \\(y=0\\) is non‑sunny.  \nThe covering argument of Section 3 remains valid because the only non‑sunny line already covers all points with \\(b=0\\).  \nThus a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nFrom (2) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nSections 2–4 provide explicit constructions for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
