            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only admissible value of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, it is possible to choose \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem, but **none** of those lines can be sunny.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Construction with \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \n   Every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\); hence all required points are covered and none of the lines is sunny.\n\n2. **Upper bound on the number of points a sunny line can cover.**  \n   *Lemma.* If a line is sunny (i.e. its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)), then it contains **at most one** point of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof idea.*  \n   Suppose a sunny line contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2\\).  \n   The slope of the line is \\(\\displaystyle m=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\).  \n   Because both points satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b_2-b_1\\le (n+1-a_2)-(n+1-a_1)=a_1-a_2=-(a_2-a_1).\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\le -1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Equality \\(m=-1\\) would make the line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), which is forbidden; therefore a sunny line must have \\(m<-1\\).  \n   But if \\(m<-1\\) then for any integer \\(x\\) between \\(a_1\\) and \\(a_2\\) the corresponding \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(mx+c\\) is **strictly less** than the integer \\(b\\) that would be required to stay inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Consequently no third lattice point of \\(T_n\\) can lie on the same line, and the two points would have to satisfy \\(a_2-a_1=1\\) and \\(b_2-b_1=-1\\), i.e. the line would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), a contradiction.  \n   Therefore a sunny line cannot contain two distinct points of \\(T_n\\); it can contain at most one.\n\n3. **Impossibility of any \\(k\\ge 1\\).**  \n   The set \\(T_n\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\qquad (n\\ge 3)\n   \\]\n\n   points.  \n   If at least one of the chosen lines were sunny, that line would contribute at most one point of \\(T_n\\) (by the lemma). The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines can each contribute at most the whole of a horizontal (or vertical) line, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points altogether. Hence the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n\\) lines with at least one sunny line is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   1+(n-1)\\cdot n = n^{2}-n+1 < \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\quad\\text{for }n\\ge 3,\n   \\]\n\n   which is insufficient to cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Consequently no configuration with a sunny line can satisfy the covering requirement, and the only possible value of \\(k\\) is \\(0\\).\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
