ots ,L_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(S\\) and contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining any prescribed \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nFrom the construction above we already have \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nTo reduce the number of sunny lines to \\(k\\) we replace \\(n-2-k\\) of the lines \\(L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n}\\) by lines parallel to one of the three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nBecause each of those lines already contains a point of \\(S'\\), we can keep the same point and simply change the slope to a forbidden one; the line remains distinct from all others and still covers its assigned point.  \nConsequently the new family still covers all points of \\(S\\) and now has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, together with \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) we have \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines).\n\nHence for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration satisfying the two conditions exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (2) we have the necessary bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction of Sections 2–3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\) is attainable.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
