 \\(u\\) can take the values \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\), there are at most \\(n-2\\) distinct slopes.  \n   Consequently all points of \\(S\\setminus\\{a=1\\text{ or }b=1\\}\\) are covered by **at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines** of the form (1).\n\n4. **Construction achieving any \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   *Base configuration.*  \n   Take the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{1}\\colon y=1 ,\\qquad L_{2}\\colon x=1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(u\\) with \\(1\\le u\\le n-2\\) choose the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{u}=(u+1,\\;n+1-u)\n   \\qquad(\\text{so }u+ (n-1-u)=n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   The line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\(P_{u}\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{u+2}\\colon y-1=\\frac{n-u-1}{u}\\,(x-1).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope in (3) is positive and different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_{u+2}\\) is sunny.  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, and covers every point of \\(S\\):\n   * points with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) lie on \\(L_{2}\\) or \\(L_{1}\\);\n   * a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) satisfies \\(a-1+ b-1\\le n-1\\); let \\(u=a-1\\).  \n     Then \\(b-1\\le n-1-u\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{u+2}\\) defined in (3).\n\n   Hence the configuration realises \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n   *Reducing the number of sunny lines.*  \n   To obtain any prescribed \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we modify the base configuration as follows.\n   Keep the two non‑sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\).  \n   Among the \\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n}\\) replace the last \\(n-2-k\\) of them by lines parallel to one of the three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Each replacement can be performed while preserving the point of \\(S\\) that the original line was covering, because a line parallel to a forbidden direction can be shifted to pass through that same point.  \n   After the replacement we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and still cover all points of \\(S\\).\n\n   Thus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) together with the explicit construction for every admissible \\(k\\) shows that the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
