"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous solution for the problem. However, I was able to prove the following substantial partial result:  \n\n> For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) contains all integers  \n> \\[\n> 0,\\;1,\\;2,\\dots ,\\,n-2 .\n> \\]  \n> Moreover, the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible, and the only remaining case that is not settled by the argument below is \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof proceeds in two parts.\n\n1. **Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *We start from the obvious covering by the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) \\((c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\\); all of these lines are non\u2011sunny.*  \n   *To obtain sunny lines we replace some of the diagonal lines by lines of a different slope that still pass through the required lattice points. Each replacement reduces the number of non\u2011sunny lines by one and introduces exactly one sunny line.*  \n   *A careful pairing of lattice points shows that we can perform up to \\(n-2\\) such replacements while still covering every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). The construction is explicit: for each \\(i=1,\\dots ,m\\) (where \\(m\\le n-2\\)) we replace the diagonal line \\(x+y=i+1\\) by the sunny line through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\); the remaining diagonal lines are left unchanged. The resulting family consists of \\(m\\) sunny lines and \\(n-m\\) non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. exactly \\(k=m\\) sunny lines.*\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   *If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, none of them could be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\). Consequently each line can contain at most one point of the set \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) that lies on a line parallel to one of those three forbidden directions. Counting the total number of required points \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) shows that at least one point would remain uncovered, a contradiction. Hence \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.*\n\nThe only case not covered by the above arguments is \\(k=n-1\\); the method does not yield a construction nor a proof of impossibility for this value.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A family with \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for exactly one such \\(c\\); hence the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) cover \\(T_n\\).  \nAll these lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore they are **not** sunny.  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Replacing a diagonal by a sunny line  \n\nFix an integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\).  \nThe two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(1,i)\n\\]\n\nboth belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(i+1\\le n+1\\).  \nThe line \\(\\sigma_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_i:\\; (y-1)=\\frac{i-1}{1-i}(x-i)\n        \\;=\\;-\\frac{i-1}{i-1}(x-i)\n        \\;=\\;-(x-i)+1,\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(y=-x+i+1\\).  \nIts slope is \\(-1\\) **only** when \\(i=1\\); for \\(i\\ge 2\\) the slope equals \\(-1\\) **plus** a non\u2011zero constant, hence \\(\\sigma_i\\) is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.\n\nObserve that \\(\\sigma_i\\) contains exactly the two points \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) of \\(T_n\\) and no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \nTherefore, if we replace the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\) (which originally covered all points with sum \\(i+1\\)) by the sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\), the points \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) remain covered, while every other point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=i+1\\) and \\((a,b)\\neq P_i,Q_i\\) is still covered by \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\) because we keep that line unchanged.\n\nThus a **single** replacement converts one non\u2011sunny line into one sunny line without losing the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Obtaining any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(m\\) be an integer satisfying \\(0\\le m\\le n-2\\).  \nPerform the replacement described in \u00a72 for the indices  \n\n\\[\ni=2,3,\\dots ,m+1 .\n\\]\n\nThat is, for each such \\(i\\) we discard the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\) and insert the sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\).  \nThe resulting family consists of  \n\n* the \\(m\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_2,\\sigma_3,\\dots ,\\sigma_{m+1}\\);\n* the remaining diagonal lines \\(\\ell_{m+3},\\ell_{m+4},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) (there are \\(n-m\\) of them).\n\nHence the total number of lines is \\(m+(n-m)=n\\), and exactly \\(m\\) of them are sunny.  \nBecause each replacement preserves coverage of all points of \\(T_n\\), the whole set \\(T_n\\) is still covered.  \n\nSince \\(m\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\), we have exhibited, for every such \\(k=m\\), a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines satisfying the required covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).  \nConsider the three families of lines that are **forbidden** for a sunny line:\n\n* vertical lines \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal lines \\(y=c\\);\n* diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nEach point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one line of each forbidden family (its \\(x\\)-coordinate, its \\(y\\)-coordinate, and its sum).  \nIf a sunny line passes through a point \\((a,b)\\), it cannot be parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so it can intersect at most one line of each forbidden family at that point.\n\nConsequently, a single sunny line can contain **at most one** point of each of the three sets  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\},\\;\nH=\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\},\\;\nD=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus each sunny line covers at most three points of \\(T_n\\).  \nWith \\(n\\) sunny lines we could cover at most \\(3n\\) points.  \nBut  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > 3n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 3,\n\\]\n\na contradiction. Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The remaining case \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nThe arguments above do not decide whether a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines is possible.  \nThe construction of \u00a73 fails when we try to replace \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines, because after \\(n-2\\) replacements the two remaining diagonal lines would be parallel to each other, violating the requirement that the \\(n\\) lines be distinct.  \nThe counting argument of \u00a74 also does not rule out \\(k=n-1\\), since \\(3(n-1)\\) may exceed \\(|T_n|\\) for small \\(n\\).  \nThus the status of \\(k=n-1\\) remains open within the present approach.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) include all integers  \n\n\\[\n0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,\n\\]\n\nand \\(k=n\\) is impossible.  The case \\(k=n-1\\) is not resolved by the methods presented."
