t lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(n-1\\) of them sunny.  \nThus only one line, say \\(\\ell\\), is non‑sunny (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nConsider the set of “border’’ points  \n\n\\[\nB_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a=1\\text{ or }b=1\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince the points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) and \\((1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) are all in \\(T_n\\) and the point \\((1,1)\\) is counted twice,  \n\n\\[\n|B_n|=2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is neither vertical nor horizontal; consequently it can contain **at most one** point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) and **at most one** point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(b=1\\).  \nHence the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points of \\(B_n\\).\n\nBecause \\(2(n-1)<2n-1\\), at least one point of \\(B_n\\) is not covered by any sunny line and must therefore lie on the unique non‑sunny line \\(\\ell\\).\n\nNow examine the possibilities for \\(\\ell\\).\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is vertical* (\\(x=c\\)), it can contain only points with \\(a=c\\).  To cover a point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(b=1\\) we would need \\(c=1\\); then \\(\\ell\\) would contain **all** points of \\(B_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) but none with \\(b=1\\) and \\(a\\neq1\\).  Consequently at least one point of \\(B_n\\) (for example \\((2,1)\\)) would remain uncovered – contradiction.\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal* (\\(y=c\\)), the symmetric argument shows a contradiction.\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is diagonal* (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)), all its points satisfy \\(a+b=d\\) for a fixed integer \\(d\\).  Among the points of \\(B_n\\) only \\((1,1)\\) satisfies such an equation, so \\(\\ell\\) can cover at most one point of \\(B_n\\).  Since at least one point of \\(B_n\\) is already uncovered by the sunny lines, this case also leads to a contradiction.\n\nThus no configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line exists; i.e. \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Im