            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A family with \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for exactly one such \\(c\\); hence the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) cover \\(T_n\\).  \nAll these lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore they are **not** sunny.  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Replacing a diagonal by a sunny line  \n\nFix an integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\).  \nThe two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(1,i)\n\\]\n\nbelong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(i+1\\le n+1\\).  \nThe line \\(\\sigma_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_i:\\;y=-x+i+1 .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(-1\\) only when \\(i=1\\); for \\(i\\ge 2\\) the line is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to \\(x+y=0\\).  Hence \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.\n\n\\(\\sigma_i\\) contains exactly the two points \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) of \\(T_n\\) and no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \nConsequently, if we discard the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\) (which originally covered all points with sum \\(i+1\\)) and insert the sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\), the points \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) remain covered, while every other point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=i+1\\) and \\((a,b)\\neq P_i,Q_i\\) is still covered by the unchanged diagonal line \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\).\n\nThus a **single** replacement converts one non‑sunny line into one sunny line without destroying the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Obtaining any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nPerform the replacement of §2 for the indices  \n\n\\[\ni=2,3,\\dots ,k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThat is, for each such \\(i\\) we delete the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\) and insert the sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\).  \nThe resulting family consists of  \n\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_2,\\sigma_3,\\dots ,\\sigma_{k+1}\\);\n* the remaining diagonal lines \\(\\ell_{k+3},\\ell_{k+4},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) (there are \\(n-k\\) of them).\n\nHence we have exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and the covering of \\(T_n\\) is preserved.  \nTherefore every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(n-1\\) of them sunny.  \nThus only one line, say \\(\\ell\\), is non‑sunny (i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nConsider the set of “border’’ points  \n\n\\[\nB_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a=1\\text{ or }b=1\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince the points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) and \\((1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) are all in \\(T_n\\) and the point \\((1,1)\\) is counted twice,  \n\n\\[\n|B_n|=2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is neither vertical nor horizontal; consequently it can contain **at most one** point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) and **at most one** point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(b=1\\).  \nHence the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points of \\(B_n\\).\n\nBecause \\(2(n-1)<2n-1\\), at least one point of \\(B_n\\) is not covered by any sunny line and must therefore lie on the unique non‑sunny line \\(\\ell\\).\n\nNow examine the possibilities for \\(\\ell\\).\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is vertical* (\\(x=c\\)), it can contain only points with \\(a=c\\).  To cover a point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(b=1\\) we would need \\(c=1\\); then \\(\\ell\\) would contain **all** points of \\(B_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) but none with \\(b=1\\) and \\(a\\neq1\\).  Consequently at least one point of \\(B_n\\) (for example \\((2,1)\\)) would remain uncovered – contradiction.\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal* (\\(y=c\\)), the symmetric argument shows a contradiction.\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is diagonal* (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)), all its points satisfy \\(a+b=d\\) for a fixed integer \\(d\\).  Among the points of \\(B_n\\) only \\((1,1)\\) satisfies such an equation, so \\(\\ell\\) can cover at most one point of \\(B_n\\).  Since at least one point of \\(B_n\\) is already uncovered by the sunny lines, this case also leads to a contradiction.\n\nThus no configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line exists; i.e. \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so it can intersect each of the following three families of lines in at most one point:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &=\\{x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\nH &=\\{y=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\nD &=\\{x+y=c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nConsequently a sunny line contains at most three points of \\(T_n\\).  \nWith \\(n\\) sunny lines we could cover at most \\(3n\\) points, but  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>3n\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nHence a covering with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final classification  \n\nFrom §§ 3, 4 and 5 we have:\n\n* For every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a construction exists.\n* \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
