"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have corrected the mistake pointed out in the bug report and now present a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\nIn particular, \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Base family (no sunny lines).**  \n   The \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_c : x+y=c\\;(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\\) cover all points of  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   and none of them is sunny (they are all parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Hence \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n2. **Replacing a diagonal by a sunny line.**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(1,i+2).\n   \\]\n\n   Both points belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(i+1\\le n+1\\) and \\(i+3\\le n+1\\) when \\(i\\le n-2\\); for the two largest values \\(i=n-1,n\\) we will treat them separately (see step\u202f3).  \n   The line \\(\\sigma_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) has equation  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_i:\\;y=\\frac{i+1}{1-i}\\,x+ i+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope \\(\\displaystyle m_i=\\frac{i+1}{1-i}\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i\\neq 0,\\qquad m_i\\neq \\infty,\\qquad m_i\\neq -1\\qquad (2\\le i\\le n),\n   \\]\n\n   so \\(\\sigma_i\\) is **sunny**.  \n   Moreover \\(\\sigma_i\\) contains exactly the two points \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) of \\(T_n\\); no other point of \\(T_n\\) lies on \\(\\sigma_i\\).\n\n   Consequently, if we delete the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\) (which originally covered all points with sum \\(i+1\\)) and insert the sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\), the two points \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) remain covered, while every other point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=i+1\\) is still covered by the unchanged diagonal line \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\) (because we have not removed it).  Thus a **single** replacement converts one non\u2011sunny line into one sunny line without destroying the covering property.\n\n3. **Realising any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be an integer satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Perform the replacement described in step\u202f2 for the indices  \n\n   \\[\n   i=2,3,\\dots ,k+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   After these \\(k\\) replacements we have  \n\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\sigma_2,\\sigma_3,\\dots ,\\sigma_{k+1}\\);\n   * the remaining diagonal lines \\(\\ell_{k+3},\\ell_{k+4},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) (there are \\(n-k\\) of them).\n\n   Hence we obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and the family still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  This shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Suppose a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines contains exactly one non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  \n   Consider the \u201cborder\u2019\u2019 set  \n\n   \\[\n   B_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a=1\\text{ or }b=1\\},\n   \\qquad |B_n|=2n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   A sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) and at most one point with \\(b=1\\); therefore the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points of \\(B_n\\).  Since \\(2(n-1)<2n-1\\), at least one point of \\(B_n\\) is not covered by any sunny line and must lie on \\(\\ell\\).\n\n   The non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\) is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or diagonal (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \n   *If \\(\\ell\\) is vertical*, it can contain only points with \\(a=c\\); to cover a point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(b=1\\) we would need \\(c=1\\), but then \\(\\ell\\) cannot cover any point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(a\\neq1\\).  \n   *If \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal*, the symmetric argument applies.  \n   *If \\(\\ell\\) is diagonal*, all its points satisfy \\(a+b=c\\); among the points of \\(B_n\\) only \\((1,1)\\) satisfies such an equation, so \\(\\ell\\) can cover at most one point of \\(B_n\\).  \n\n   In every case the single non\u2011sunny line cannot cover the uncovered point of \\(B_n\\), a contradiction.  Hence a configuration with exactly one non\u2011sunny line does not exist; i.e. \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, each line could intersect the three families  \n\n   \\[\n   V=\\{x=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n   H=\\{y=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n   D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   in at most one point, because a sunny line is not parallel to any of them.  Consequently a sunny line contains at most three points of \\(T_n\\).  With \\(n\\) sunny lines we could cover at most \\(3n\\) points, but  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>3n\\qquad (n\\ge 3),\n   \\]\n\n   a contradiction.  Therefore \\(k=n\\) is also impossible.\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n   Steps\u202f3,\u202f4 and\u202f5 together give the complete classification: the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]"
