f \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   For every \\(n\\ge 5\\) we have \\(\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2>3n\\); therefore the covering is impossible when \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n   The remaining small cases are handled directly.\n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  Here \\(|T_3|=6\\) and we would have three sunny lines, each containing at most three points, so the counting bound does not rule out a covering.  However, among the six points of \\(T_3\\) there are three points lying on the same diagonal, namely \\((1,2),(2,1),(1,1)\\).  Any sunny line can contain at most one of these three points (otherwise its slope would be \\(-1\\)).  Thus three sunny lines can cover at most three of the diagonal points, leaving at least one uncovered – contradiction.\n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  Here \\(|T_4|=10\\) and the bound \\(3n=12\\) is not sufficient.  Observe that the four points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1),(1,1)\\) all lie on the diagonal family \\(x+y=4\\).  As before, a sunny line can contain at most one of them, so four sunny lines can cover at most four of these four diagonal points, leaving at least one of them uncovered – contradiction.\n\n   Hence for every \\(n\\ge3\\) a covering by only sunny lines is impossible; i.e. \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n   Steps 3, 4 and 5 together give the complete classification:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n   \\]\n\n   for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\n---  \n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for some \\(c\\) in the displayed range; hence it lies on \\(\\ell_c\\).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A sunny replacement\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(1,i+1).\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to \\(T_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\ni+1\\le n+1,\\qquad 1+(i+1)=i+2\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\sigma_i\\) be the line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).  Its equation is  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_i:\\;y-1=\\frac{i}{1-i}(x-i)\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\ny=\\frac{i}{1-i}\\,x+i+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(m_i=\\dfrac{i}{1-i}\\) satisfies \\(m_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) for every \\(i\\ge2\\); therefore \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Only \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) of \\(T_n\\) lie on \\(\\sigma_i\\).*  \nSuppose \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\sigma_i\\).  Multiplying by \\(1-i\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(1-i)b=i a-(i^2-i).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nReducing (1) modulo \\(i\\) yields \\((1-i)b\\equiv -i\\pmod i\\), i.e. \\(b\\equiv1\\pmod i\\).  \nSince \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(i\\ge2\\), the only possibilities are \\(b=1\\) or \\(b=i+1\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\), then (1) becomes \\((1-i)=i a-(i^2-i)\\), whence \\(a=i\\).\n* If \\(b=i+1\\), then (1) gives \\(0=i a-(i^2-i)\\), whence \\(a=1\\).\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) is either \\((i,1)=P_i\\) or \\((1,i+1)=Q_i\\).  No other lattice point of \\(T_n\\) lies on \\(\\sigma_i\\).\n\nConsequently, deleting the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\) (which originally covered all points with sum \\(i+1\\)) and inserting the sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\) leaves every point of \\(T_n\\) covered: the two points \\(P_i,Q_i\\) are now on \\(\\sigma_i\\), while all other points with sum \\(i+1\\) remain on the unchanged diagonal lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Obtaining any \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nPerform the replacement described above for the indices  \n\n\\[\ni=2,3,\\dots ,k+1 .\n\\]\n\nAfter these \\(k\\) steps we possess  \n\n* the sunny lines \\(\\sigma_2,\\sigma_3,\\dots ,\\sigma_{k+1}\\);\n* the untouched diagonal lines \\(\\ell_{k+3},\\ell_{k+4},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\).\n\nThere are \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and the covering property holds by the argument of Section 2.  Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Non‑existence of a covering with exactly one non‑sunny line (\\(k=n-1\\))\n\nAssume a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines contains \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and a single non‑sunny line \\(\\ell\\).\n\nDefine the border set  \n\n\\[\nB_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a=1\\text{ or }b=1\\},\n\\qquad |B_n|=2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it can contain **at most one** point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) and **at most one** point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(b=1\\).  Hence the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points of \\(B_n\\).\n\nSince \\(2(n-1)<2n-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), at least one point of \\(B_n\\) is uncovered by the sunny lines and must lie on \\(\\ell\\).\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) can be of three types:\n\n* **Vertical** (\\(x=c\\)): it meets \\(B_n\\) only at \\((c,1)\\).  Thus it can cover at most one border point, contradicting the fact that at least one border point is uncovered.\n* **Horizontal** (\\(y=c\\)): symmetric to the vertical case.\n* **Diagonal** (\\(x+y=c\\)): among the border points it can contain at most the two points \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\); consequently it can cover at most two border points.  Because at least one border point is uncovered, \\(\\ell\\) would have to cover *all* uncovered border points, which is impossible.\n\nHence a covering with exactly one non‑sunny line cannot exist; i.e. \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Non‑existence of a covering with only sunny lines (\\(k=n\\))\n\nAssume all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  For a sunny line \\(\\gamma\\) consider the three families  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{x=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n\\},\\;\nH=\\{y=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n\\},\\;\nD=\\{x+y=c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\gamma\\) is not parallel to any of these families, it meets each family in at most one point; consequently \\(\\gamma\\) contains at most three points of \\(T_n\\).  Therefore the whole collection can cover at most \\(3n\\) points.\n\nNow  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\ge5\\)*, then \\(\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2>3n\\), contradicting the covering requirement.\n\nIt remains to treat \\(n=3,4\\).\n\n* \\(n=3\\): \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}\\).  \n  The three points \\((1,2),(2,1),(1,1)\\) lie on the same diagonal \\(x+y=3\\).  A sunny line cannot contain two of them (otherwise its slope would be \\(-1\\)).  Hence three sunny lines can cover at most three of these three diagonal points, leaving at least one uncovered – contradiction.\n\n* \\(n=4\\): \\(T_4\\) contains the four points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1),(1,1)\\) all on the diagonal \\(x+y=4\\).  As before, a sunny line can contain at most one of them, so four sunny lines can cover at most four of these points, leaving at least one uncovered – contradiction.\n\nThus a covering by only sunny lines is impossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\); i.e. \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer  \n\nCombining the constructive part (Section 3) with the impossibility results (Sections 4 and 5) we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3,\\;k\\text{ is possible iff }0\\le k\\le n-2.}\n\\]"
