covering by only sunny lines is impossible when \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n   The remaining small cases are handled directly:\n\n   *\\(n=3\\).*  The three points \\((1,2),(2,1),(1,1)\\) lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=3\\).  A sunny line can contain at most one of them, so three sunny lines can cover at most three of these three diagonal points, leaving at least one uncovered – contradiction.\n\n   *\\(n=4\\).*  The four points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1),(1,1)\\) all lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=4\\).  Again a sunny line can contain at most one of them, so four sunny lines cannot cover all of them.\n\n   Hence for every \\(n\\ge3\\) a covering by only sunny lines is impossible; i.e. \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n   Steps 3, 4 and 5 give the complete classification:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n   \\]\n\n   for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Covering with no sunny lines  \n\nThe \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for some \\(c\\) in the displayed range; hence it lies on \\(\\ell_c\\).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A sunny line that replaces a diagonal line  \n\nFix an integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(1,i+1).\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to \\(T_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\ni+1\\le n+1,\\qquad 1+(i+1)=i+2\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nLet \\(\\sigma_i\\) be the line through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).  \nThe two‑point form gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y-1}{x-i}=\\frac{i+1-1}{1-i}=\\frac{i}{1-i},\n\\]\n\nhence the equation of \\(\\sigma_i\\) can be written as  \n\n\\[\ni\\,a-(i-1)\\,b=i(i-1). \\tag{★}\n\\]\n\nEquation (★) is a linear Diophantine equation in the integers \\(a,b\\).  \nA particular solution is \\((a,b)=(i,1)\\); the general integer solution is\n\n\\[\na=(i-1)t+i,\\qquad b=i t+1\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb Z). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(1\\le a,b\\le n\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we must have  \n\n\\[\n1\\le i t+1\\le n,\\qquad 1\\le (i-1)t+i\\le n,\\qquad (i-1)t+i+i t+1\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThese three inequalities force \\(t=0\\).  Substituting \\(t=0\\) into (1) yields exactly the two points \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).  Therefore **the only points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\sigma_i\\) are \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).**\n\nThe slope of \\(\\sigma_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{i}{1-i},\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); consequently \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.\n\nNow delete the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\) (which originally covered all points with sum \\(i+1\\)) and insert the sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\).  The two points \\(P_i,Q_i\\) remain covered (by \\(\\sigma_i\\)), while every other point with sum \\(i+1\\) stays on the unchanged diagonal lines.  Hence the covering property is preserved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Obtaining any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nPerform the replacement of step 2 for the indices  \n\n\\[\ni=2,3,\\dots ,k+1 .\n\\]\n\nAfter these \\(k\\) replacements we have  \n\n* sunny lines \\(\\sigma_2,\\sigma_3,\\dots ,\\sigma_{k+1}\\);\n* the untouched diagonal lines \\(\\ell_{k+3},\\ell_{k+4},\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\).\n\nThere are \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and the family still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Non‑existence of a covering with exactly one non‑sunny line (\\(k=n-1\\))\n\nAssume a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines contains \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and a single non‑sunny line \\(\\ell\\).\n\nDefine the border set  \n\n\\[\nB_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a=1\\text{ or }b=1\\},\n\\qquad |B_n|=2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it can contain **at most one** point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) and **at most one** point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(b=1\\).  Hence the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points of \\(B_n\\).\n\nSince \\(2(n-1)<2n-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), at least one point of \\(B_n\\) is uncovered by the sunny lines and must lie on \\(\\ell\\).\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) can be of three possible types:\n\n* **Vertical** (\\(x=c\\)): it meets \\(B_n\\) only at \\((c,1)\\); thus it can cover at most one border point.\n* **Horizontal** (\\(y=c\\)): symmetric to the vertical case.\n* **Diagonal** (\\(x+y=c\\)): among the border points it can contain at most the two points \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\); consequently it can cover at most two border points.\n\nIn every case \\(\\ell\\) cannot cover the uncovered border point(s) required by the counting argument, a contradiction.  Therefore a covering with exactly one non‑sunny line does not exist; i.e. \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Non‑existence of a covering with only sunny lines (\\(k=n\\))\n\nAssume all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  For a sunny line \\(\\gamma\\) consider the three families  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{x=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n\\},\\;\nH=\\{y=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n\\},\\;\nD=\\{x+y=c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\gamma\\) is not parallel to any of these families, it meets each family in at most one point; consequently \\(\\gamma\\) contains at most three points of \\(T_n\\).  Hence the whole collection can cover at most \\(3n\\) points.\n\nNow \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \n\n*If \\(n\\ge5\\)*, then \\(\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2>3n\\), contradicting the covering requirement.\n\nIt remains to treat \\(n=3,4\\).\n\n* \\(n=3\\): the three points \\((1,2),(2,1),(1,1)\\) lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=3\\).  A sunny line can contain at most one of them (otherwise its slope would be \\(-1\\)).  Thus three sunny lines can cover at most three of these three diagonal points, leaving at least one uncovered – contradiction.\n\n* \\(n=4\\): the four points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1),(1,1)\\) all lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=4\\).  Again a sunny line can contain at most one of them, so four sunny lines cannot cover all of them.\n\nTherefore a covering by only sunny lines is impossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\); i.e. \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer  \n\nCombining the constructive part (Section 3) with the impossibility results (Sections 4 and 5) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3,\\;k\\text{ is possible iff }0\\le k\\le n-2.}\n\\]"
