(\\sigma_i\\). The resulting family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   *Define the border set*  \n   \\[\n   B_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a=1\\text{ or }b=1\\},\\qquad |B_n|=2n-1 .\n   \\]\n   A sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it can contain **at most one** point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) and **at most one** point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(b=1\\). If a sunny line contains the point \\((1,1)\\) it uses both of these possibilities simultaneously, so the total number of *distinct* border points that the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover is at most  \n   \\[\n   2(n-1)-1 = 2n-3 .\n   \\]\n   Hence at least two border points of \\(B_n\\) are not covered by the sunny lines.  \n\n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the unique non‑sunny line.  \n   * If \\(\\ell\\) is vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), it meets \\(B_n\\) in at most one point, contradicting the need to cover the two uncovered border points.  \n   * If \\(\\ell\\) is diagonal (\\(x+y=c\\)), then the only border points it can contain are \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\); thus it can cover at most two border points, exactly the two that are uncovered. In this case \\(\\ell\\) must be the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\).  \n\n     Consider the remaining border points on the same side of the diagonal, namely  \n     \\[\n     \\{(1,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le c-2\\}\\cup\\{(a,1)\\mid 2\\le a\\le c-2\\}.\n     \\]\n     Their total number is \\(2c-4\\). All of them must be covered by the sunny lines. Each sunny line can contain at most one point with \\(a=1\\) and at most one point with \\(b=1\\); consequently the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) such points. Since \\(c\\le n\\), we have  \n     \\[\n     2c-4 \\ge 2n-4 > 2(n-1)-2 = 2n-4 ,\n     \\]\n     a contradiction. Hence the diagonal case is also impossible.  \n\n   Therefore a covering with exactly one non‑sunny line cannot exist; i.e. \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.  \n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   A sunny line meets each of the families \\(\\{x=c\\},\\{y=c\\},\\{x+y=c\\}\\) in at most one point, so it contains at most three points of \\(T_n\\). Hence \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(3n\\) points, whereas \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>3n\\) for all \\(n\\ge5\\). The cases \\(n=3,4\\) are checked directly and also fail. Thus \\(k=n\\) is impossible.  \n\n6. **Conclusion** – Steps 3, 4 and 5 give the complete classification:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Covering with no sunny lines  \n\nThe \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in this range; hence it lies on \\(\\ell_c\\).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A sunny line that replaces a diagonal line  \n\nFix an integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(1,i+1).\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(i+1\\le n+1\\) and \\(1+(i+1)=i+2\\le n+1\\).\n\nThe line \\(\\sigma_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y-1}{x-i}=\\frac{i+1-1}{1-i}=\\frac{i}{1-i}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\((1-i)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(1-i)(y-1)=i(x-i).\n\\]\n\nExpanding and rearranging yields the linear Diophantine equation  \n\n\\[\ni\\,x+(i-1)\\,y=i^{2}+i-1. \\tag{★}\n\\]\n\nA particular integer solution is \\((x,y)=(i,1)\\).  \nAll integer solutions of (★) are obtained by adding the general solution of the homogeneous equation \\(i\\,x+(i-1)\\,y=0\\), namely \\((x,y)=\\bigl((i-1)t,\\,-i t\\bigr)\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n(x,y)=\\bigl(i+(i-1)t,\\;1-i t\\bigr),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNow impose the conditions that a point belongs to \\(T_n\\):\n\n\\[\n1\\le x\\le n,\\qquad 1\\le y\\le n,\\qquad x+y\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFrom (1),\n\n\\[\nx=i+(i-1)t,\\qquad y=1-i t .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(t\\ge 1\\)* then \\(y=1-i t\\le 1-i<0\\), contradicting \\(y\\ge1\\).  \n*If \\(t\\le -1\\)* then \\(x=i+(i-1)t\\le i-(i-1)=1\\) and \\(y=1-i t\\ge 1+i\\ge n+1\\) for \\(i\\ge2\\), violating \\(y\\le n\\) or the sum condition.  \n\nThus the only admissible integer is \\(t=0\\), which gives precisely the two points \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\).  Consequently **the only points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(\\sigma_i\\) are \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i+1)\\).**\n\nThe slope of \\(\\sigma_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{i}{1-i},\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) for every \\(i\\ge2\\); therefore \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.\n\nNow delete the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{i+1}\\) (which originally covered all points with sum \\(i+1\\)) and insert the sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\).  The two points \\(P_i,Q_i\\) remain covered (by \\(\\sigma_i\\)), while every other point with sum \\(i+1\\) stays on the unchanged diagonal lines.  Hence the covering property is preserved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Obtaining any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nReplace the diagonal lines \\(x+y=i+1\\) by the sunny lines \\(\\sigma_i\\) for the indices  \n\n\\[\ni=2,3,\\dots ,k+1 .\n\\]\n\nAfter these \\(k\\) replacements we have  \n\n* sunny lines \\(\\sigma_2,\\sigma_3,\\dots ,\\sigma_{k+1}\\);\n* the untouched diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c=k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\).\n\nThere are exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and the family still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  Hence every integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Non‑existence of a covering with exactly one non‑sunny line (\\(k=n-1\\))\n\nAssume a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines contains \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and a single non‑sunny line \\(\\ell\\).\n\nDefine the **border set**\n\n\\[\nB_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a=1\\text{ or }b=1\\},\n\\qquad |B_n|=2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it can contain **at most one** point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) and **at most one** point of \\(B_n\\) with \\(b=1\\).  \nIf a sunny line contains the point \\((1,1)\\) it uses both of these possibilities simultaneously. Consequently the total number of *distinct* border points that the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover is at most  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)-1 = 2n-3 .\n\\]\n\nHence at least two border points of \\(B_n\\) are not covered by the sunny lines.\n\nNow examine the possible types of the non‑sunny line \\(\\ell\\).\n\n* **Vertical** (\\(x=c\\)) or **horizontal** (\\(y=c\\)).  \n  Such a line meets \\(B_n\\) in exactly one point, so it cannot cover the two uncovered border points – contradiction.\n\n* **Diagonal** (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \n  The only border points lying on this line are \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\); thus \\(\\ell\\) can cover at most two border points, exactly the two that are uncovered. In this situation \\(\\ell\\) must be the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n  Consider the remaining border points on the same side of the diagonal, namely  \n\n  \\[\n  S_c=\\{(1,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le c-2\\}\\cup\\{(a,1)\\mid 2\\le a\\le c-2\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Their cardinality is \\(|S_c|=2c-4\\). All points of \\(S_c\\) must be covered by the sunny lines. As argued above, each sunny line can contain at most one point with \\(a=1\\) and at most one point with \\(b=1\\); therefore the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\) points of \\(S_c\\).\n\n  Since \\(c\\le n\\) (otherwise the diagonal would contain points outside \\(T_n\\)), we have  \n\n  \\[\n  2c-4 \\ge 2n-4 > 2(n-1)-2 = 2n-4 .\n  \\]\n\n  Thus \\(|S_c|>2(n-1)\\), contradicting the ability of the sunny lines to cover all points of \\(S_c\\).\n\nIn every case a contradiction is reached; therefore a covering with exactly one non‑sunny line cannot exist. Hence **\\(k=n-1\\) is impossible**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Non‑existence of a covering with only sunny lines (\\(k=n\\))\n\nAssume all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  For a sunny line \\(\\gamma\\) consider the three families  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{x=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n\\},\\qquad\nH=\\{y=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n\\},\\qquad\nD=\\{x+y=c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\gamma\\) is not parallel to any of these families, it meets each family in at most one point; consequently \\(\\gamma\\) contains at most three points of \\(T_n\\).  Hence the whole collection can cover at most \\(3n\\) points.\n\nNow \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \nFor \\(n\\ge5\\) we have \\(\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2>3n\\), a contradiction.  \n\nThe remaining small cases are checked directly:\n\n* \\(n=3\\): the three points \\((1,2),(2,1),(1,1)\\) all lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=3\\).  A sunny line can contain at most one of them (otherwise its slope would be \\(-1\\)), so three sunny lines cannot cover all three points.\n\n* \\(n=4\\): the four points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1),(1,1)\\) lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=4\\).  Again a sunny line can contain at most one of them, so four sunny lines cannot cover them all.\n\nThus a covering by only sunny lines is impossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\); i.e. **\\(k=n\\) cannot occur**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Final answer  \n\nFrom Sections 3, 4 and 5 we obtain the complete classification:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3,\\;k\\text{ is possible iff }0\\le k\\le n-2.}\n\\]"
