st \\(n\\) possibilities.  \n   *Horizontal* line \\(y=c\\) is analogous.  \n   *Diagonal* line \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) meets \\(T_n\\) at the integer points \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n\\) such points. ∎\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, and by Lemma 2 each of them covers at most \\(n\\) points.  \n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   2k + n\\,(n-k).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Since every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, we require  \n\n   \\[\n   2k + n\\,(n-k)\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   2k \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - n(n-k)\n        = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - n^2 + nk\n        = -\\frac{n(n-1)}2 + nk .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   2k - nk \\ge -\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   k\\,(2-n) \\ge -\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(n\\ge3\\), \\(2-n<0\\); dividing by the negative number reverses the inequality:\n\n   \\[\n   k \\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2\\,(n-2)} = n-2+\\frac{2}{n-2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\) is an integer, we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines**.\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines ( \\(n-k\\) of them ).*  \n   - The vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   - The horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   - For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-1-k\\) the diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\).  \n\n   There are \\(1+1+(n-2-