ontains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *Proof.* As in the original solution: a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) at points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\), giving at most \\(n\\) points; the horizontal and diagonal cases are analogous. \u220e  \n\n5. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Split the sunny lines into two groups:\n\n   * \\(k_1\\) lines have slope \\(1\\);  \n   * \\(k_2=k-k_1\\) lines have slope different from \\(1\\).\n\n   By Lemma\u202f1 each of the \\(k_2\\) lines covers at most \\(2\\) points, and by Lemma\u202f2 each of the \\(k_1\\) lines covers at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\) points.  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines each cover at most \\(n\\) points (Lemma\u202f3).  \n   Consequently the total number of points that can be covered satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   2k_2 + k_1\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor + n\\,(n-k)\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k_2\\ge0\\) we may drop the term \\(2k_2\\) to obtain a weaker but sufficient inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   k_1\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor + n\\,(n-k)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k_1\\le k\\) and \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\), inequality (1) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\frac{n+1}{2}+n(n-k)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl(\\frac{n+1}{2}-n\\Bigr)\\ge -\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   k\\le n-2+\\frac{2}{\\,n-2\\,}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\) is an integer and \\(n\\ge3\\), we conclude  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines**.\n\n6. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\