d \\(Q_i\\) defined in (3).  \n     The line \\(\\ell_i\\) contains precisely these two points; because its slope is not \\(1\\) it does not contain any other lattice point with the same sum.  \n     Hence the only points of \\(T_n\\) with sum \\(n-i\\) that are not already covered by Cases\u202f1 or\u202f2 are \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\), and both are covered by \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n   The three cases exhaust all possibilities for \\((a,b)\\); therefore every element of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n   By construction exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_0,\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1}\\) are sunny, while the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  Hence a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The counting argument (Lemmas\u202f1\u20133) shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   The explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \n\n   Consequently the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\] \u220e"
