\\le n+1\\}\\) are \\(0,\\infty,-1,1\\); a direct inspection of the possible triples shows that any other rational number would force one of the points to have a coordinate larger than \\(n+1\\).  \n   Since a sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(m=0\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(m=\\infty\\)) or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(m=-1\\)), the only remaining possibility is \\(m=1\\), contradicting the hypothesis \\(m\\neq1\\). ∎  \n\n   *Lemma 2.*  \n   A line of slope \\(1\\) (i.e. \\(y=x+c\\) with integer \\(c\\)) contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   s:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Points on \\(y=x+c\\) satisfy \\(b-a=c\\). Together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   2a\\le n+1-c,\\qquad 2b\\le n+1+c .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-c}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\qquad \n   b\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1+c}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Both right–hand sides are bounded by \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\); consequently at most \\(s\\) integer pairs \\((a,b)\\) satisfy the two inequalities, i.e. the line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(s\\) points. ∎  \n\n   *Lemma 3.*  \n   A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   *Proof.*  \n   – Vertical line \\(x=c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) meets \\(T_n\\) at \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such points.  \n   – Horizontal line \\(y=c\\) is analogous.  \n   – Diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n\\)) meets \\(T_n\\) at the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); again at most \\(n\\) points. ∎  \n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   Let a configuration satisfying the covering condition contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Split the sunny lines into  \n\n   * \\(k_1\\) lines of slope \\(1\\);  \n   * \\(k_2=k-k_1\\) lines whose slope differs from \\(1\\).\n\n   By Lemma 1 each of the \\(k_2\\) lines covers at most \\(2\\) points of \\(T_n\\); by Lemma 2 each of the \\(k_1\\) lines covers at most \\(s\\) points.  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines each cover at most \\(n\\) points (Lemma 3).  \n\n   Therefore the total number of *distinct* points that can be covered satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   2k_2 + k_1 s + n\\,(n-k)\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{★}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k_2\\ge0\\) we may drop the term \\(2k_2\\) to obtain the weaker but still valid inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   k_1 s + n\\,(n-k)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k_1\\le k\\) and \\(s\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\), (1) implies  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\frac{n+1}{2}+n(n-k)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2)k\\le n(n-2)-\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n   =\\frac{n(n-2)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\le n-2\\qquad(\\text{since }n\\ge3). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (2) shows that **no configuration can contain \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines**; consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines* (there are \\(n-k\\) of them):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &L_1:\\;x=1,\\\\\n   &L_2:\\;y=1,\\\\\n   &L_{2+j}:\\;x+y=1+j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2-k).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   The first two are vertical and horizontal; the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) are parallel to the forbidden diagonal \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   All together they give \\(1+1+(n-2-k)=n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Sunny lines* (exactly \\(k\\) of them).  \n   For each \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = -\\frac{n-i-2}{\\,n-i-1\\,}\\,x + (n-i). \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S_i\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i+1,\\;n-i),\\qquad Q_i=(i+2,\\;n-i-1), \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   because substituting either point into (2) satisfies the equation.  \n   Its slope \\(-\\dfrac{n-i-2}{n-i-1}\\) is a rational number different from \\(0,\\infty,-1,1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The slopes are distinct for different \\(i\\), so the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n   *Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.*\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   1. **If \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\)**, the point lies on \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\).\n\n   2. **If \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n-k\\)**, then \\(c:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le c\\le n-k\\); the diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (which is among the non‑sunny lines) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   3. **If \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b=n-i\\) for some \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\)**, then the possible points with this sum are  \n\n      \\[\n      (i+1,n-i),\\;(i+2,n-i-1),\\dots ,(n-i-1,i+2).\n      \\]\n\n      The two extreme points are exactly \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) defined in (3).  \n      The line \\(S_i\\) contains precisely these two points: because its slope is not \\(1\\), any other point \\((x,y)\\) on the same line would satisfy  \n\n      \\[\n      \\frac{y-x}{\\,n-i-1\\,}= -\\frac{n-i-2}{\\,n-i-1\\,},\n      \\]\n\n      i.e. \\(y-x=-(n-i-2)\\), which together with \\(x+y=n-i\\) yields the unique solutions \\((i+1,n-i)\\) and \\((i+2,n-i-1)\\).  \n      Hence the only points of \\(T_n\\) with sum \\(n-i\\) that are not already covered by Cases 1 or 2 are \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\), and both belong to \\(S_i\\).\n\n   The three cases exhaust all possibilities for \\((a,b)\\); therefore every element of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n   By construction exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\) are sunny, while the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.  Hence a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1–3 together with inequality (2) show that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n   The explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is indeed attainable.  \n\n   Consequently the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n   \\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
