 \n*Statement.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  \nBecause points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), we have \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nFor such a \\(c\\) the admissible \\(b\\) satisfy \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); the number of possibilities is \\(n+1-c\\le n\\).\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\) is symmetric and also yields at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  \nSince \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(c\\le n\\) then the points are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n-1\\) of them.  \nIf \\(c=n+1\\) the points are \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\); there are exactly \\(n\\) of them.  \nThus in every case the number of points does not exceed \\(n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(k_1\\) be the number of sunny lines of slope \\(1\\);  \n* \\(k_2=k-k_1\\) be the number of sunny lines whose slope differs from \\(1\\).\n\nBy Lemma 1 each of the \\(k_2\\) lines covers at most \\(2\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nBy Lemma 2 each of the \\(k_1\\) lines covers at most \\(s=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, so by Lemma 3 each of them covers at most \\(n\\) points.\n\nHence the total number of (not necessarily distinct) incidences between the \\(n\\) lines and the points of \\(T_n\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n2k_2 + k_1 s + n\\,(n-k) \\;\\ge\\; |T_n| = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{★}\n\\]\n\nBecause the left‑hand side of (★) is an upper bound for the *maximum* possible number of distinct points that can be covered, (★) is a necessary