ve the universal bound \\(k\\le n-2\\); the explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\) is attainable. Hence the required set of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and from \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Lemmas  \n\n#### Lemma\u202f1  \n*Statement.*  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(m\\neq 1\\) then \\(|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le 2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for a contradiction, that three distinct points  \n\\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\).  \nBecause they are collinear we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}=m .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll coordinates are positive integers and satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a_i\\le n,\\qquad 1\\le b_i\\le n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the differences \\(a_j-a_i\\) and \\(b_j-b_i\\) are integers whose absolute values are at most \\(n-1\\).  \nThus each fraction in (1) is a rational number whose numerator and denominator are bounded in absolute value by \\(n-1\\).\n\nIf \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1,1\\) then the reduced fraction of \\(m\\) has denominator at least \\(2\\).  \nLet the reduced form be \\(m=\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(|q|\\ge 2\\).  \nFrom (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1 = m\\,(a_2-a_1)=\\frac{p}{q}(a_2-a_1).\n\\]\n\nSince the left\u2011hand side is an integer, \\(q\\mid (a_2-a_1)\\).  \nBecause \\(|a_2-a_1|\\le n-1\\) and \\(|q|\\ge 2\\), we have \\(|a_2-a_1|\\ge |q|\\).  \nSimilarly \\(q\\mid (a_3-a_1)\\).  \n\nNow write \\(a_2-a_1 = q\\alpha\\) and \\(a_3-a_1 = q\\beta\\) with non\u2011zero integers \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) sat