ntains at most \\(s\\) points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma\u202f3  \n*Statement.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  \nBecause points of \\(T_n\\) satisfy \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), we have \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nFor such a \\(c\\) the admissible \\(b\\) satisfy \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); the number of possibilities is \\(n+1-c\\le n\\).\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\) is symmetric and also yields at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  \nSince \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(c\\le n\\) then the points are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there are \\(c-1\\le n-1\\) of them.  \nIf \\(c=n+1\\) the points are \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\); there are exactly \\(n\\) of them.  \nThus in every case the number of points does not exceed \\(n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(k_1\\) be the number of sunny lines of slope \\(1\\);  \n* \\(k_2=k-k_1\\) be the number of sunny lines whose slope differs from \\(1\\).\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 each of the \\(k_2\\) lines covers at most \\(2\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 each of the \\(k_1\\) lines covers at most \\(s=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points.  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, so by Lemma\u202f3 each of them covers at most \\(n\\) points.\n\nHence the total number of (not necessarily distinct) incidences between the \\(n\\) lines and the points of \\(T_n\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n2k_2 + k_1 s + n\\,(n-k) \\;\\ge\\; |T_n| = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{\u2605}\n\\]\n\nBecause the left\u2011hand side of (\u2605) is an upper bound for the *maximum* possible number of distinct points that can be covered, (\u2605) is a necessary condition for any feasible configuration.\n\nFrom (\u2605) we obtain a weaker inequality by dropping the non\u2011negative term \\(2k_2\\):\n\n\\[\nk_1 s + n\\,(n-k) \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k_1\\le k\\) and \\(s\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\), (1) implies  \n\n\\[\nk\\frac{n+1}{2}+n\\,(n-k)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n+1}{2}k - nk \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - n^2\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n-\\frac{n-1}{2}k \\ge -\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying by \\(-2\\) (which reverses the inequality) gives  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)k \\le n(n-1) .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nk \\le n .\n\\]\n\nTo sharpen this bound we return to the original inequality (\u2605).  \nWrite \\(k_2 = k - k_1\\) and substitute \\(s=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\):\n\n\\[\n2(k-k_1) + k_1\\frac{n+1}{2} + n(n-k) \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nCollect the terms in \\(k_1\\):\n\n\\[\nk_1\\Bigl(\\frac{n+1}{2}-2\\Bigr) + 2k + n(n-k) \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}-2 = \\frac{n-3}{2}\\ge 0\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), the left\u2011hand side is minimized when \\(k_1=0\\).  \nHence a necessary condition is obtained by setting \\(k_1=0\\):\n\n\\[\n2k + n(n-k) \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSimplifying,\n\n\\[\n2k + n^2 - nk \\ge \\frac{n^2+n}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n\\bigl(2-n\\bigr)k \\ge \\frac{n^2+n-2n^2}{2}\n= -\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(2-n\\le 0\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), dividing by the negative number reverses the inequality:\n\n\\[\nk \\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} = \\frac{n}{2}\\cdot\\frac{n-1}{n-2}.\n\\]\n\nFor integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the right\u2011hand side is strictly smaller than \\(n-1\\); indeed\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n}{2}\\cdot\\frac{n-1}{n-2}= n-2+\\frac{2}{n-2}\\;<\\; n-1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus **any** admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \n\n#### 4.1  Non\u2011sunny lines ( \\(n-k\\) of them)\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: x=1,\\\\\nL_2 &: y=1,\\\\\nL_{2+j} &: x+y = 2+j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2-k).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThese are respectively vertical, horizontal and diagonal lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); none of them is sunny.  \nThe total number is \\(1+1+(n-2-k)=n-k\\).\n\n#### 4.2  Sunny lines ( \\(k\\) of them)\n\nFor each \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = -\\frac{n-i-2}{\\,n-i-1\\,}\\,x + (n-i). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Verification that \\(S_i\\) is sunny.*  \nIts slope is \\(-\\dfrac{n-i-2}{n-i-1}\\).  \nSince \\(n-i-2\\) and \\(n-i-1\\) are consecutive positive integers, the fraction is neither \\(0\\), nor \\(\\infty\\), nor \\(-1\\), nor \\(1\\).  \nHence \\(S_i\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.\n\n*Points on \\(S_i\\).*  \nSubstituting \\(x=i+1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{n-i-2}{n-i-1}(i+1)+(n-i)=n-i,\n\\]\n\nso \\((i+1,n-i)\\in S_i\\).  \nSimilarly, with \\(x=i+2\\) we obtain \\(y=n-i-1\\); thus \\((i+2,n-i-1)\\in S_i\\).  \nThese two points are distinct and belong to \\(T_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\n(i+1)+(n-i)=n+1\\le n+1,\\qquad\n(i+2)+(n-i-1)=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n*No other point of \\(T_n\\) lies on \\(S_i\\).*  \nSuppose \\((x,y)\\in T_n\\) also satisfies (3).  \nFrom (3) we have  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{n-i-2}{n-i-1}x + (n-i)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n(n-i-1)(y-n+i) = -(n-i-2)x .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n(n-i-2)x + (n-i-1)(y-n+i)=0 .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\gcd\\bigl(n-i-2,\\;n-i-1\\bigr)=1\\), (4) forces  \n\n\\[\nx = (n-i-1) t,\\qquad y-n+i = -(n-i-2) t\n\\]\n\nfor some integer \\(t\\).  \nInsert these expressions into the condition \\(x+y\\le n+1\\):\n\n\\[\n(n-i-1)t + \\bigl[n-i-(n-i-2)t\\bigr] \\le n+1\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nt\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, positivity of the coordinates forces \\(t\\ge 1\\).  \nHence \\(t=1\\), which yields exactly the two points \\((i+1,n-i)\\) and \\((i+2,n-i-1)\\) found above.  \nThus \\(S_i\\) contains **only** these two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe slopes in (3) are distinct for different \\(i\\) because the numerator\u2013denominator pairs \\((n-i-2,n-i-1)\\) are distinct; consequently the lines \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\) are pairwise different.\n\n#### 4.3  Covering all points of \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n-k\\).  \nThen the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\) is among the lines \\(L_{2+j}\\) (with \\(j=a+b-2\\)) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 3.* \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b = n-i\\) for some \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with this sum are  \n\n\\[\n(i+1,n-i),\\;(i+2,n-i-1),\\dots ,(n-i-1,i+2).\n\\]\n\nThe first two belong to the sunny line \\(S_i\\) (by construction).  \nAll the remaining points have either coordinate equal to \\(1\\) or have sum \\(\\le n-k\\) (because \\(k\\le n-2\\)), so they fall under Case\u202f1 or Case\u202f2.  \nHence every point with sum \\(n-i\\) is covered.\n\nThe three cases exhaust all possibilities, therefore every element of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\nWe have built exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\)) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all distinct, covering the whole set \\(T_n\\).  \n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f1\u20133 imply that any configuration satisfying the covering condition must have at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction shows that each integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\) is attainable.  \n\nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed"
