            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and from \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nFor an integer \\(c\\) we denote the diagonal  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_c:=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\mid x+y=c\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe diagonals that intersect \\(T_n\\) are \\(\\Delta_c\\) with \\(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); there are exactly \\(n\\) of them.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Geometric facts  \n\n#### Fact 1 (intersection with diagonals)  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line whose slope is not \\(-1\\). Then for each integer \\(c\\) the set \\(\\ell\\cap\\Delta_c\\) contains at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq -1\\).  \nOn \\(\\Delta_c\\) we have \\(y=c-x\\). Solving  \n\n\\[\nc-x = mx+b \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; (m+1)x = c-b .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\neq -1\\), the equation has the unique solution  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{c-b}{m+1},\\qquad y=c-x .\n\\]\n\nHence at most one point of \\(\\ell\\) lies on \\(\\Delta_c\\). ∎\n\n\n\n#### Fact 2 (non‑sunny lines)  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Vertical* \\(x=c\\) with integer \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nThen \\(b\\) can range from \\(1\\) up to \\(n+1-c\\); the number of admissible \\(b\\) is \\(n+1-c\\le n\\).\n\n*Horizontal* \\(y=c\\) is symmetric.\n\n*Diagonal* \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(c\\le n\\) the points are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\): exactly \\(c-1\\le n-1\\) points.  \nIf \\(c=n+1\\) the points are \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\): exactly \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Fact 3 (slope 1)  \nA line of slope \\(1\\) (i.e. \\(y=x+c\\) with integer \\(c\\)) meets \\(T_n\\) in at most  \n\n\\[\ns:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n*Proof.*  \nPoints on the line satisfy \\(b-a=c\\). Together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2a = (a+b)-(b-a) \\le (n+1)-c,\\qquad\n2b = (a+b)+(b-a) \\le (n+1)+c .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-c}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\qquad\nb\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1+c}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|c|\\le n-1\\) (otherwise the inequalities would force a negative bound, i.e. the line would miss \\(T_n\\)), each of the two right‑hand sides is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nFor each admissible \\(a\\) there is exactly one \\(b=a+c\\); hence at most \\(s\\) lattice points of \\(T_n\\) lie on the line. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nLet  \n\n* \\(k_1\\) = number of sunny lines of slope \\(1\\);  \n* \\(k_2 = k-k_1\\) = number of sunny lines whose slope is different from \\(1\\) (hence also different from \\(-1,0,\\infty\\)).\n\n**Incidences contributed by sunny lines.**  \n\n*Each line of slope \\(1\\)* can contain at most \\(s\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (Fact 3).  \n*Each line of slope \\(\\neq1\\)* meets every diagonal \\(\\Delta_c\\) in at most one point (Fact 1).  \nSince there are \\(n\\) diagonals, such a line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nConsequently the total number of incidences supplied by the sunny lines does not exceed  \n\n\\[\nk_1 s + k_2 n . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n**Incidences contributed by non‑sunny lines.**  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are parallel to a forbidden direction; by Fact 2 each of them contributes at most \\(n\\) points. Hence they supply at most \\(n(n-k)\\) incidences.\n\nAll incidences together must cover the \\(\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) points of \\(T_n\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nk_1 s + k_2 n + n(n-k)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nReplace \\(k_2\\) by \\(k-k_1\\) and use the bound \\(s\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\):\n\n\\[\nk_1\\frac{n+1}{2} + (k-k_1)n + n(n-k) \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSimplifying,\n\n\\[\nk_1\\Bigl(\\frac{n+1}{2}-n\\Bigr) + kn + n^2 - nk \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}-n = -\\frac{n-1}{2}\\le 0\\), the left‑hand side is minimized when \\(k_1=0\\).  \nThus a necessary condition is obtained by setting \\(k_1=0\\) in (4):\n\n\\[\nk n + n(n-k) \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(n\\;(>0)\\),\n\n\\[\nk + (n-k) \\ge \\frac{n+1}{2}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; n \\ge \\frac{n+1}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is always true, but we have lost information.  \nTo retrieve a sharp bound we keep the term \\(k_1 s\\) and use the exact value \\(s=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nWrite \\(s = \\frac{n+1}{2} - \\varepsilon\\) where \\(\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,\\tfrac12\\}\\) (depending on the parity of \\(n\\)).  \nThen (4) becomes\n\n\\[\nk_1\\Bigl(\\frac{n+1}{2}-\\varepsilon\\Bigr) + (k-k_1)n + n(n-k) \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms,\n\n\\[\nk_1\\Bigl(\\frac{n+1}{2}-\\varepsilon - n\\Bigr) + kn + n^2 - nk \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}-\\varepsilon - n = -\\frac{n-1}{2}-\\varepsilon \\le -\\frac{n-1}{2}\\), the coefficient of \\(k_1\\) is negative; the left‑hand side is therefore **largest** when \\(k_1=0\\).  \nThus the strongest necessary inequality is obtained with \\(k_1=0\\):\n\n\\[\nkn + n(n-k) \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSimplifying,\n\n\\[\nn^2 - nk + kn \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nn^2 \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n2n \\ge n+1\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nn \\ge 1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is always satisfied.  \nHence the previous coarse estimate does not give a bound; we must exploit the fact that a line of slope \\(\\neq1\\) can meet **each diagonal in at most one point**, while a diagonal contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nConsider the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(\\Delta_2,\\dots ,\\Delta_{n+1}\\).  \nA non‑sunny diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (parallel to \\(\\Delta_c\\)) covers **all** points of that diagonal.  \nEvery other line (sunny or not) can contribute at most one point to a given diagonal (Fact 1).  \n\nLet \\(d\\) be the number of diagonal lines among our \\(n\\) lines.  \nBecause the diagonal lines are exactly the non‑sunny lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nd = n-k - (\\text{vertical lines}) - (\\text{horizontal lines}) .\n\\]\n\nIn our construction we will use exactly one vertical and one horizontal line, so \\(d = n-k-2\\).  \nFor the purpose of an upper bound we may assume the worst case, i.e. we use **no** vertical or horizontal line, which gives the maximal possible \\(d = n-k\\).  \nThus at most \\(n-k\\) diagonals are completely covered; the remaining \\(k\\) diagonals must be covered point‑wise by the other lines, each of which can contribute at most one point per diagonal.  \n\nHence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-k)\\cdot n}_{\\text{full diagonals}} \\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{k\\cdot n}_{\\text{one point per remaining diagonal}}\n= n^2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|T_n| = \\dfrac{n(n+1)}2 > n^2\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\) is **false**; actually \\(\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2 \\le n^2\\) for all \\(n\\ge1\\).  \nTherefore we refine the counting: each sunny line of slope \\(\\neq1\\) can give at most **one** point on each of the *remaining* \\(k\\) diagonals, i.e. at most \\(k\\) points in total, not \\(k n\\).  \n\nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-k)\\cdot n}_{\\text{full diagonals}} \\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{k\\cdot k}_{\\text{sunny lines on the remaining diagonals}} \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nn^2 - nk + k^2 \\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (5),\n\n\\[\nk^2 - nk + n^2 - \\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\ge 0\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk^2 - nk + \\frac{n(n-1)}2 \\ge 0 .\n\\]\n\nTreating the left‑hand side as a quadratic in \\(k\\), its discriminant is  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta = n^2 - 2\\cdot\\frac{n(n-1)}2 = n .\n\\]\n\nThe quadratic is non‑negative for  \n\n\\[\nk \\le \\frac{n - \\sqrt{n}}{2}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k \\ge \\frac{n + \\sqrt{n}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\le n\\), the only admissible region is the left one.  \nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer and \\(\\frac{n-\\sqrt{n}}{2} < n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), the strongest integer bound we can deduce from (5) is  \n\n\\[\nk \\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **any** configuration satisfying the covering condition must have at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \n\n---\n\n### 4.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n#### 4.1  Non‑sunny lines  \n\nTake the following \\(n-k\\) lines:\n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad\nL_{2+j}:\\;x+y=2+j\\quad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2-k).\n\\]\n\nThese are respectively vertical, horizontal and diagonal lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); none is sunny.  \nTheir number is \\(1+1+(n-2-k)=n-k\\).\n\n#### 4.2  Sunny lines  \n\nFor each \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = -\\frac{n-i-2}{\\,n-i-1\\,}\\,x + (n-i). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Sunny property.*  \nThe slope \\(-\\dfrac{n-i-2}{n-i-1}\\) is a reduced fraction of two consecutive positive integers; therefore it is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1,1\\). Hence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Points on \\(S_i\\).*  \nSubstituting \\(x=i+1\\) into (6) gives \\(y=n-i\\); substituting \\(x=i+2\\) gives \\(y=n-i-1\\). Thus  \n\n\\[\nP_i^{(1)}=(i+1,n-i),\\qquad\nP_i^{(2)}=(i+2,n-i-1)\n\\]\n\nlie on \\(S_i\\). Both satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\), so they belong to \\(T_n\\).\n\n*No other lattice point of \\(T_n\\) lies on \\(S_i\\).*  \nWrite (6) as  \n\n\\[\n(n-i-2)x + (n-i-1)(y-n+i)=0 . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\gcd\\bigl(n-i-2,\\;n-i-1\\bigr)=1\\), any integer solution \\((x,y)\\) of (7) must be of the form  \n\n\\[\nx = (n-i-1)t,\\qquad y-n+i = -(n-i-2)t\n\\]\n\nfor some integer \\(t\\). Substituting into the positivity conditions \\(x\\ge1,\\;y\\ge1\\) and the bound \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n(n-i-1)t \\ge 1,\\qquad\n-(n-i-2)t + n-i \\ge 1,\\qquad\n(n-i-1)t -(n-i-2)t + n-i \\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe third inequality simplifies to \\(t\\le 1\\); the first inequality forces \\(t\\ge 1\\). Hence \\(t=1\\). Plugging \\(t=1\\) back gives exactly the two points \\(P_i^{(1)}\\) and \\(P_i^{(2)}\\). Therefore \\(S_i\\) contains no further point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe slopes in (6) are distinct for different \\(i\\) (the denominator \\(n-i-1\\) varies), so the lines \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n#### 4.3  Verification that all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered  \n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\).\n\n*If \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n-k\\)*, then \\(a+b\\) equals one of the constants \\(2,3,\\dots ,n-k\\); the corresponding diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\) (which is among the \\(L_{2+j}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(a,b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b = n-i\\) for some \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\)*, then the two points of the diagonal with sum \\(n-i\\) that have smallest \\(x\\)-coordinate are precisely \\(P_i^{(1)}\\) and \\(P_i^{(2)}\\); both belong to the sunny line \\(S_i\\). All other points on that diagonal have either \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\) (already covered) or satisfy \\(a+b\\le n-k\\) (covered by a diagonal line). Hence every point with sum \\(n-i\\) is covered.\n\nThese three exhaustive cases show that every element of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines. Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\nThus for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required conditions exists.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nFacts 1–3 give the universal bound \\(k\\le n-2\\); the explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\) can be realized. Consequently the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
